 Orissa. \n(c) The Ministry of Tourism under its Scheme of 'Product/Infrastructure \nDevelopment for De stinations and Circuits' provides Central Financial Assistance to \nStates/UTs for construction of Budget Accommodation in North -East, Jammu & \nKashmir and Eco-Tourism Projects in the whole of the country. \nMS. PRAMILA BOH1DAR: Mr. Chairman, Sir, I would like to know from the hon. \nMinister in what way Palace on Wheels from Orissa would promote tourism in different \nparts of the State? What will be the total cost of the project of Palace on Wheels? - Has \nany survey taken place? Has the Government received any prop osal from the State \nGovernment? \nSHRIMATI AMBIKA SONI: Under the scheme of luxury trains which are State-\nspecific, 1 would like to inform the hon. Member that there has been no proposal for \nsuch a scheme received from the Orissa Government till date. But, u nder the large \nrevenue-generating projects, there is a scheme with the Ministry of Tourism to give, at \nleast, a certain amount of money in three instalments for trains which are State- specific \nfor the promotion of tourism as has been done in a few other St ates. We are looking at \nissues as to how to promote this in other States as well. But it is difficult to expand \nfurther till we resolve the on-going projects. \nMS. PRAMILA BOHIDAR: What is the proposed action plan of the Centre to boost \ntourism in Orissa to improve economy of this backward region? \nSHRIMATI AMBIKA SONI: Sir, in the Tenth Five Year Plan, we have given large \namounts to Orissa for tourism development, both destination and circuits. Funds have \nbeen allocated from the Ministry of Tourism annually for the celebrations of fairs and \nfestivals. There is an ongoing effort to promote places of Buddhist interest, \n††The question was actually asked on the floor of the house by Ms. Pramila Bohidar \n\nOral answers [14 AUG, 2007] to Questions       9 \nespecially in the last year, when there was celebration to commemorate 2550 years of \nBuddha Mahaparinirvana. To develop sites of Buddhist interest, there is a pending \nrequest with the Ministry of Finance also to give us some kind of tax incentives for \nbuilding budget hotels in cities  of Buddhist interest for foreign and domestic tourists. \nSHRI B.J. PANDA: Sir. the hon. Minister has stated that there is no proposal under \nconsideration of her Ministry to introduce Palace on Wheels train in Orissa. That is \ntechnically correct. But, I would like to bring to the Minister's attention a report in the \nNew Indian Express of 18th April of this year which says that the IRCTC, which is a \ncorporation under the Ministry of Railways, has indeed made such a proposal and I \nquote the report, \"If the IRCTC has its way, then, a Palace on Wheels train will shortly \nbe starting from Orissa.\" Now, it is obvious that the Railway Ministry is not allowing \nthe IRCTC to have its way. I would seek an assurance from the hon. Minister that in the \ninterest of developing tourism in Orissa, about which she has told so much just now, she \nwill take up this issue with the Railway Ministry to allow the IRCTC to implement its \nplan to have a Palace on Wheels rolling out from Orissa. \nSHRIMATI AMBIKA SONI: Sir, I am sure that hon. Member will agree that I can't \ngive assurance on behalf of another Ministry. \nSHRI B.J. PANDA: I would seek an assurance from the hon. Minister only for \ntaking it up with the Railway Ministry. \nSHRIMATI AMBIKA SONI: I have already said that we have not received an \nofficial proposal. There are some ongoing proposals to have tourism -centric trains for \nsome other States. It will be my constant endeavour to try and replicate the success \nstories of either Palace on Wheels or Deccan Splendour, or  other such tourism trains, \nBuddhist Circuit trains to other States as well and Orissa certainly has to be one, among \nother States to be on priority basis. \n \nश्री  मती  �वप्लव  : माननीय  सभप‍�त  महोदय , म�  माननीय  \nमंत्री  महोदया  से  कहना  चाहती  हूं  �क  उन्होन�  प्रश्न  \nके  पाटर्  “सी ” को  जो  जवाब  �दया  है  उसम�  कहा  है  ‍ि◌क 'Product \ninfrastructure/development for destination and circuit -  budget accommodation ' \nइन्होन�  इसका  एक क्राइटे �रया  रखा  है  ? इ स के  अलावा  \nनॉथर्  ईस्ट  तथा  जम्मू  और कश्मीर  के  संबध  म�  कहा  �क  \nवहां  पर  होटल्स  बनाए  जाएग�  ।  �हमाचल  प्रदे श , जो  \nटू �रज्म  के  �लहाज  से  बहुत  ज्यादा  महत्वपूणर्  ह� , उ स के  \n�लए  यह  क्य�  नह�ं   रखा  गया  ह�  – यह  म�  मंत्री  महोदय  \nसे  जानना  चाहती  हूं  ?  \n \nश्री  मती  अिम्बका  सोनी  : सर , हमार�  टू �रज्म  \n�म�नस्टर�  क�  एक स्क�म  बजट  होटल्स  के  �लए  सिब्सडी  दे ने  \nक�  है  ।  एक, दो  और तीन  �सतारा  होटल�  के  �लए  हम तमाम  उन  \nलोग�  और संस्थाओं  को  सिब्सडी  दे ते  ह�  जो  बजट  होटल  \nबनाना  चाह�  ।  अभी  तक थ्री -स्टार  होटल्स  के  �लए  सिब्सडी   \n75 लाख  तक थी  ।  जहां  तक नॉथर्  ईस्ट  क�  आठ स्टेट्स  और \nजम्मू -कश्मीर  को  यह  प्रपोजल  दे ने  क�  बात  है ,माननीय  \nसदस्या  को  जरूर  इस बात  क�  जानकार�  है  �क  हमार�  नी�त  के  \nअनु\nसार  हमारे  बजट  का  दस परस�ट  एक्स्ट्रा  प्रा�वजन  \nनॉथर्  ईस्टनर्  प्रदे श  क�  बात  है  जो  हम हर  स्टे ट  म�  \nसिब्सडी  दे ते  ह� , वह  �हमाचल  प्रदे श  म�  भी  द�  जाती  है  \nऔर आगे  भी  हम दे ने  का  प्रयास  जार�  रख�गे  ।   \n\n10     Oral answers [RAJYA SABHA] to Questions \n \nश्री  रूद्रनारायण  पा�ण  : धन्यवाद  सभाप�त  महोदय  आप \nराज्य  सभा  के  तेरहव�  सभाप�त  है  और कल जब आपने  हमारा  \nयहां  सभाप�तत्व  �कया  था  तो  कल 13 तार�ख  थी  और हम भी  तेरह  \nवाले  ह�  ।  हमारे  नेता  श्री  वाजेई  जी  पहले  13 �दन  के  �लए  \nदे श  के  प्रधान  मंत्री  रहे  और बाद  म�  13 साल  तक रह�  ।   \nMR. CHAIRMAN: What is your question? (Interruptions) \nश्री  रूद्रनारायण  पा�ण  : सर , आप भी  तेरह  वाले  ह�  ।  \n...(व्यवधान )... \nMR. CHAIRMAN: Please put the question? (Interruptions) \nश्री  रूद्रनारायण  पा�ण  : महोदय , मै  अवश्य  सवाल  पर  \nआ ऊं ग ा  ।  महोदय , �पछले  बजट  सत्र  म�  म�ने  एक सवाल  �कया  \nथा  ।   \nMR. CHAIRMAN: Please put the question? (Interruptions) \n \nश्री  रूद्रनारायण  पा�ण  : महोदय , उड़ीसा  के  कोणाक र्  म�  \nएक सूयर्  मं�दर  ह�  ।  हम सब जानते  ह�  �क  भगवान  सू\nयर्  \nसकल शिक्त  के  स्त्रोत  होते  ह�  ।  कोणाक र्  के  सू\nयर्  \nमं�दर  म�  सू\nयर्  पू\nजा  होती  ह�  या  नह�ं ,  क्या  यह  \nमाननीय  मंत्री  महोदया , मंत्रालय  और वतर्मान  सरकार  को  \nपता  है  ? मेरा  सीधा  सवाल  है  �क  कोणाक र्  के  सूयर्  मं�दर  \nम�  अब सूयर्  पू\nजा  होती  है  या  नह�ं  ? अगर  नह�ं  होती  ह�  \nतो  सू\nयर्  का  प्रबंध  करने  के  �लए  सरकार  के  पास  क्या   \nउपाय  है  और क्या  प्रस्ताव  ह�  ? ...( व्यवधान )... हम�  यह  \nभी  पता  है  और आपक�  राजनी�त  भी  पता  है  ।  ...(व्यवधान )... \nMR. CHAIRMAN: Please sit down. (Interruptions) \nश्री  रूद्रनारायण  पा�ण  : हम�  पता  है , ले�कन  के व ल  \nपदार्फाश  करने  के  �लए  यह  सवाल  है।  ...(व्यवधान )... \nश्री  मती  अिम्बका  सोनी  : सर , इस प्रश्न  का  बार�क�  म�  \nउत्तर  दे ने  के  �लए  मुझे  समय चा�हए , क्य��क  मान्यवर  \nसदस्य  को  जो  प्रश्न  है , वह  ऑ�रजनल  प्रश्न  से  संब�धत  \nनह�ं  ह�  ।  म�  पूर�  जानकार�  हा�सल  क र के  मान्यवर  सदस्य  \nको  द�  दूंगी  ।   \nश्री  कलराज  �मश्र  : इस मं�दर  क�  मरम्मत  करने  क�  भी  \nआवश्यकता  है  ।  इस संबध  म�  भी  कु छ  अलग से  व्यवस्था  कर�  \nतो  ज्यादा  उपयु\nक्त  होगा  ।   \nMR. CHAIRMAN: Next, Dr. Chandan Mitra. \nDR. CHANDAN MITRA: Sir, my question to the hon. Minister concerns the \nsituation where there are circuits which cross between States like the Buddhist Circuit, \nfor instance, which goes from Eastern U.P., Bihar, Jharkhand and Orissa, and, if there \nare proposals to run a train connecting all these Buddhist sites — specifically, I would \nlike to mention the Dhaulagiri site in Orissa near Bhubaneshwar, which is a very famous \nsite where the battle of Kalinga was fought — to which the Minister said there was no \nproposal from the State Government, then, I would like to know, for an inter -State \ncircuits of this nature, who is the proposing authority to run special trains, and, how can \nthese be implemented, if the Ministry of Tourism does not take initiative in this ma tter. \nSHRIMATI AMBIKA SONI: Sir, the Ministry of Tourism is more than willing to \ntake initiatives of this kind but there are financial implications. The three trains for \ntourists that we have started with the Ministry of Railways have not yet taken off \nbecause there are some financial implications not only of the capital investment, \n\nOral answers [14 AUG., 2007] to Questions     11 \nwhich is needed for getting the train on track, but also of the running cost and issues like \nwho will bear the running cost, wh o will take care of it in the first few years if the train \ndoes not run on profit. Those things are being sorted out. Once the Ministry of Railways, \nwith our help and support, is able to work out the first few trains, which have already \nbeen announced, I a m confident that we will be able to take up trains, which will have \nan inter -State impact on connotation. At the moment, there is no train yet, which is \nrunning on inter-State basis. \nState-run low cost domestic airlines \n*44. SHRI MANOHAR JOSHI: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be pleased \nto state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that the first State -run low cost domestic airline is all set to \ntake wings very shortly to target truck routes between big cities as also sectors that are \nnot fully covered by full service carriers; and \n(b) what would be ticket pricing and whether it would be comparable to. those \noffered by current LCCs (Low Cost Carriers)? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI \nPRAFUL PATEL): (a) and (b) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \nStatement \n(a) Yes, Sir. Air India Express, the budget carrier launched by Air India's subsidiary \ncompany, is planning to commence domestic operations from October, 2007 in addition \nto its existing international operations. \n(b) The fares charged by Air India Express would be competitive and comparable td \nthe other low cost carriers operating on the same routes. \nSHRI MANOHAR JOSHI: Mr. Chairman, Sir, the Budget Carrier is to start from \ncoming October. I would like to know from the hon. Minister whether  this budget \ncarrier will be going to the remote areas, as is expected, and, what is the exact \ncompetitive rate, which the Minister proposes for the budget carriers. \nSHRI PRAFUL PATEL: Sir, the Air India Express, the budget carrier of the national \nairline, is already in operation for the last two years or more. It is primarily having \noperations in the international sectors. Again, it is, primarily, driven from Kerala, \nAmritsar and from many cities to the Middle East and the South -East Asian \ncountries.After the merger of Air India and Indian Airlines, the budget carrier will now \nalso have a domestic footprint because there is a lot of competition in this segment even \nwithin the country, and, as all of us are aware, because of budget carriers now flying in \nthe country, the scope of air  travels has widened. Many people are now able to afford \nair travel. So, it would be equally in the interest of the national carrier to expand its \noperation to be able to enter that segment in the domestic sector. And, that is the  reason \nwhy, as we have answered, from October, post merger, this budget carrier will now also \noperate within India to be able to take on competition and also to enter that segment. \nAs far as pricing is concerned, I am sure, all of us will agree that on account of \nderegulation, the Government does not regulate prices of tickets to be charged b> the \nairlines. It has also helped in again widening the entire sector. And. as a result \n\n12     Oral answers [RAJYA SABHA] to Questions \nof this, we have seen today th at no regulation of pricing on the part of the Government \nhas led to better choice, further competition and better fares. So, we will not have any \nregulatory aspect as far as pricing is concerned. \nSHRI MANOHAR JOSHI: Mr. Chairman, Sir, my question, whether  this particular \nairline will go to the remote areas, has not been replied to. Secondly, Sir, 1 would also \nlike to know what special additions the Government proposes to do in this particular \ncarrier because of the competition. And, since the other airlines have already started the \nbudget carriers, 1 would like to know what would be the specility of the Air India \nExpress. \nSHRI PRAFUL PATEL: Sir, it is a fact that our country is large and we need more \nconnectivity to newer cities. In fact, the real growth is  going to come from level 2 and \nlevel 3 cities where a large chunk of our population resides and is keen to have services \nof air travel. I would also like to inform the House that three years ago the number of \ncities connected within India was approximately 50. And, that number has now grown up \nto almost 80. Almost 80 cities are now being connected within the country. The \nendeavour of the Ministry is to ensure that more and more cities are brought into the air \ntravel map. The Air India Express certainly wil l also endeavour to connect cities which \nare remote, as you would like to call it, or cities which have not yet been connected. \nThere is also a policy of the Government which is known as Route Disbursal Guidelines. \nEvery airline which operates in the count ry has to adhere to category 1, 2, 2 A and 3. \nThese are the four categories of routes. It primarily means that if an airline is operating \nin a major metro route, say, Mumbai -Delhi or Mumbai -Bangalore or whichever the \nmetro cities, it also is obliged, under  the Route Disbursal Guidelines, to operate to cities \nwhich are level 2 cities and then go to 2A, that is, the cities within the North -East or in \nJ&K. Such areas have to be connected by every airline. So, regardless of whether we \ndefine it or not, every ai rline is obliged to go by all these Route Disbursal Guidelines so \nthat the cities are connected. Again, it is our constant endeavour and we have been \ntalking to the State Governments. There is also a response now which we are saying \nwhich is emerging where  we are finding that newer cities are now sought to be \nconnected by whatever the infrastructure is available with the State Governments. In this \nfurther endeavour, the Government has now recently announced within the schedule \noperators permit, a new classification known as Schedule Operators Permit Regional \nAirlines. This primarily is again to drive aviation to the smallest of towns wherever \ninfrastructure is available. So, it is our constant endeavour. We are keen, and I am sure \nall of us are keen here. We always see within the House that all Members seek \nconnectivity to their region which is very appropriate. We shall strive to achieve that.  \nSHRIMATI BRINDA KARAT: Sir, through you, I would like to ask the Minister \nwhether there is any truth in the reports that some foreign airlines have evinced interest \nin coming into the domestic sector on no - frills/low-cost basis and whether the Minister \nhas had some meetings with owners of those airlines. And, if there is any truth in those \nreports, what is the Government's approach to that? \nSHRI PRAFUL PATEL: Brindaji, I can assure you and the House that there is no \nchange in policy at the moment. 1 can only talk of policy as it is today and the policy is \nvery clear that the FDI in the domestic aviation sector is at 49 per cent, but with the \nspecific provision that there shall not be any foreign airline participation in the domestic \naviation sector. \n\nOral answers [14 AUG., 2007] to Questions     13 \nAs far as any reports which have emanated are concerned, unfortunately, I  have not \nbeen able to see them. But I can assure you that there is no proposal at the moment with \nthe Government. As the Government's policy stands today, no foreign airline is allowed \nto participate in the domestic aviation sector. \nSHRI SANTOSH BAGRODIA:  Sir, with the opening up of the skies, really the \nmost ordinary person in India can travel. I congratulate the hon. Minister on that. This is \nvery good. But the problem is that safety precautions have gone to the winds. Every day \nwe hear that two aircrafts missed. That is one side. \nAnother thing is delay at all the airports; even smaller airports also. The delay is \nbecause of crowd or jamming up. What precautions the hon. Minister is planning to \ntake? The moment our national carrier also starts bringing 'c heap routes' into the picture, \nor whatever you call it 'no frills,' there will be further crowd. Do we have enough \ninfrastructure to handle it? That is one thing. \nAnother point is this. Does the national carrier plan to have very small aircrafts like \na 20 -seater or a 50 -seater? That only can go to the level C and D cities. Does the \nGovernment plan for that also? \nSHRI PRAFUL PATEL: Sir, none of these arises out of this question of Air Indian \nExpress operating in domestic routes. However, I would like to assu re the House, first \nand foremost, that when it comes to safety and security, I do not think there is any \ndisagreement. We are bound by an international charter of the International Civil \nAviation Organisation where all member -states have to adhere to certain norms and \nlevels of safety. I would also go one step further and would say that the House should \nalso join me in congratulating the people who are actually manning and making aviation \nsafe in our country. The International Civil Aviation Organisation co nducts a safety \naudit of each and every member -state, that means virtually every country of the world, \nand in their audit, they have ranked India number one in the world. The House must \ncongratulate the people who are doing a good job. \nI do appreciate that  there are concerns. Newspaper reports do sometimes give an \nimpression that everything is very unsafe or things are not as good as they should be. I \ncan assure you that also. When sometimes newspapers report 'misses,' you see an \nincident is reported as 'mi sses,' incident means any deviation of procedure. There are \nbands of safety within bands of safety. There is no reason to be overly alarmed by what \nis being reported. I am not saying that it should be overlooked or that the regulatory \nauthorities are not giving it due consideration. But, at the same time, there is sometimes \nan impression being created that all  is not well. That is not true. If that were the case, \nthen why would the International Civil Aviation Organisation conduct its audit and give \nus this kind of a ranking? \nYes, there would be some tolerance levels which will have to be accepted by all of \nus in terms of congestion. Congestion is not a phenomenon which was known in India; \nit is a worldwide phenomenon. We were never used to it because India never had that \nkind of air traffic in the country. With the boom, there is now a situation which is \ntherefore leading all of us to think collectively that infrastructure is needed as early as \npossible. That is what the Government is endeavouring. The Gover nment is also doing, \nits best. Today, I can assure you that three years ago, seventy per cent \n\n14     Oral answers [RAJYA SABHA] to Questions \nof the traffic was only concentrated in Mumbai and Delhi. Now, with the pressure and \nthe initiatives of the Minist ry and all stakeholders, we have been able to get other cities \ninto the aviation network and reduce the pressure of seventy per cent of entire India. \nIndia is not Mumbai and Delhi alone. Therefore, we have now brought down that share \nto well below fifty pe r cent. It's our endeavour. When we talk of air congestion, \nunfortunately, we talk only of Mumbai and Delhi. Mumbai and Delhi again are not \nIndia. There is no air congestion at other 70 or 80 airports of the country. (Interruptions) \nGoa is a different case where we only operate for three- hour window; it's a naval \nairfield. But, I am talking of all airfields across the country and in Delhi also, the second \nrunway will be operational by June -July, 2008 which will reduce congestion; virtually, \nthe congestion o ver Delhi will disappear. Mumbai would, therefore, be the only airport \nwhich will have the congestion problem for a couple of years till the new airport is \nready. So, I would also like to assure everybody that congestion at Mumbai and Delhi \ncannot be looke d upon as congestion of the skies in India. Others should also reap the \nbenefits. And, therefore, it is a good thing that now many a city, right from Amritsar to \nAhmedabad, to Lucknow, to just about Thiruvananthapuram, to everywhere, there is \nmore and more  air traffic which is a good sign and, I think, all of us should appreciate \nthis. As far as smaller aircraft are concerned, yes, Air India will be operating ATR and \nsmaller aircraft in future. (Interruptions) \nMR. CHAIRMAN: Shri Malaisamy. (Interruptions) \nDR. K. MALAISAMY: Thank you, Chairman, Sir. Though we are very happy to \nhear the Minister, I am sorry, the ground reality is totally different. I am inclined to \nagree with Shri Santosh Bagrodia. \nMR. CHAIRMAN: Question please. \nDR. K. MALAISAMY: Sir, every Me mber here is travelling everyday by a flight. \nWe face the greatest ordeal to go by a flight. To be honest with the Chairman and with \nthe House, the entire airport, whether small or big, is worse than a bus stand. This is the \nway that I am able to see it. T here is lot of congestion kutcha shed and the crowd is so \nmuch. We are so happy that the Minister has got an ambitious programme for expansion, \nfor low cost carriers, etc. We are happy about it, but, will be kind enough to initiate the \ninfrastructure for t hat? Has he got the management system to manage all these things? \nTo be honest with you, Sir, 1 would like to ask: What is his monitoring system to \nmonitor the low cost carriers? Many times, what happens, many of the air carriers are \ncancelled abruptly and  there is no alternative arrangement at all. There is absolutely \nnothing. I am asking a specific question through the Chairman as to whether he has got a \nmonitoring system in terms of quality and quantity in service as far as low cost carriers \nare concerned. That is number one. \nMR. CHAIRMAN: Only one question. No, please, (Interruptions) One question \nplease. (Interruptions) Please, hon. Member. Interruptions) \nDR. K. MALAISAMY: Sir, the second part of the question is this. Sir, I will stop. \nCan he assure a t ime-frame, 1 underline the word 'time -frame', before which he can \ndevelop the infrastructure in such a way that he can afford to give a satisfactory service?  \n\nOral answers [14 AUG., 2007] to Questions     15 \nSHRI PRAFUL PATEL: Sir, I would like to inform the hon. Member. I am sorry, \nyou travel everyday but, probably, you travel only between Chennai and Delhi. That is \nwhy, you are not aware as to how much of infrastructure upgradation is taking place all \nacross the country. {Interruptions) No, no. I did not say that. There is no inference about \nChennai. I am saying that an infrastructure upgradation programme is going across all \nover the country within whatever resources are there. Thirty -five non-metro airports, we \nhave mentioned in the past, are being upgra ded and many smaller airports, which are \nnot even in the thirty-five non-metro airports, are also being upgraded. Chennai is going \nto be upgraded. There was some issue of land on which the State Government has now \nagreed. Everywhere this programme is going on. I would also like to inform the \nMembers that in many airports where we are willing to spend the money, the State \nGovernments have not been able to give us ten acres or twenty acres of additional land \nwhich also comes in the way of expansion and modern isation. So, please, I would \nappreciate that all Members should also join us in supporting the programme by helping \nus, through the State Governments, and getting us the additional infrastructure \nrequirements. {Interruptions) I don't want to enter into an argument. {Interruptions) \nSHRI N. JOTHI: Your airports are looking like bus stands. ..(Interruptions)... \nSHRI PRAFUL PATEL: In the name of the concerns of the common man, I think, \nall of us should be happy that our airports are looking like bus stands. At least, the \ncommon man is flying. Why are we ignoring that fact and how do you want us to stop \nthe growth of aviation in the name of the airports being less crowded? I think, it is a \nnatural phenomenon. You should appreciate that. You cannot have everything at one \ntime. It is leading towards infrastructure development because there is a growth in \naviation. The pressure is there, and we are doing everything possible. \nAcute shortage of small. coins \n*45. SHRIMATI JAYA BACHCHAN: will the Minister of FINANCE be p leased to \nstate: \n(a) whether he is aware that there is at present acute shortage of small coins in a \nnumber of city markets and commuters/consumers are facing acute shortage of coins at \nthe hands of bus conductors, taxi and scooter drivers and vendors of daily necessities; \n(b) whether the shortage is very sudden; and \n(c) if so, the steps being taken to address the problem? \nTHE MINISTER OF FINANCE (SHRI P. CHIDAMBARAM): (a) to (c) A Statement \nis laid on the Table of the House. \nStatement \n(a) to (c) There have been some r eports as well as complaints from public/trade \nbodies regarding shortage of coins in certain markets. The Reserve Bank of India has \nreported that the shortage is being felt for the last one year. The following steps have \nbeen taken to address the problem: \n\n16     Oral answers [RAJYA SABHA] to Questions \n(i) The Government has been minting Rs. 2 coins in Ferritic Stainless Steel (FSS) \ninstead of curpro -nickel since 2005 and has also decided to mint Rs. 5 coin in \nFerritic Stainless Steel instead of curpro-nickel. \n(ii) Minting of Rs. 1, Rs. 2 and Rs. 5 coins has been placed on fast track. Several \nissue offices of Reserve Bank of India held coin melas, distributed coins \nthrough mobile banks at market place etc. Reserve Bank of India has also \nadvised select currency chest branches to do the same. \n(iii) To cater to the large demand of hotels, restaurants, retail shops etc., distribution \narrangements have been made through registered Association/Trade bodies \nwherever they have come forward for the purpose. \n(iv) In Mumbai, arrangements have been made for distribution of coins through five \npost offices and similar arrangement is being put in place in other States. \n(v) All the Banks have been advised to ensure distribution of coins through their \ncurrency chest branches p roactively and they have also been advised to identify \nsome branches in each district for the specific purpose of coin distribution. \n(vi) Banks have been advised to consider installation of coin vending machines. \nSHRIMATI JAYA BACHCHAN: Sir, I have seen th e answer given by the Minister \nvery extensively. But the question that I have asked is, the cause for the shortage. That \nquestion has not been answered. If you want to answer that question first, it is all right. \nThen I will ask my second supplementary. I want to know one thing. Why is there \nshortage of coins? \nSHRI P. CHIDAMBARAM: Sir, there is adequate inventory of coins in the mints. \nThe RBI places indents upon the mints and directs delivery from the inventory to various \nissuing offices, currency chest et c. For example, as of 31 st of July, we have nearly 500 \nmillion pieces of Re. 1 coin, 290 million pieces of Rs. 2 coin, 18 million pieces of Rs. 5 \ncoin and 17 million pieces of ...(Interruption)... Therefore, there is no shortage of coins \nin the inventory.  The shortage is being felt in the banks, in the currency chests and the \nissuing offices. Now, I have asked the RBI why is there adequate number of coins in the \nmints and why is there a shortage in the issuing offices? It appears that in 2005 -06 and in \n2006-07, there was, in fact, a reverse flow of coins to the currency chest. According to \nthe RBI and their estimate, there is excessive supply to demand. For example, in the \nsecond half of 2005- 06, 107 million pieces of Re. 1 coin came back to the currency \nchest; 66 million pieces of Rs. 5 coin came back to the currency chest. That perhaps is \nthe reason why in pockets of India, shortages emanated, Since the beginning of 2006- 07, \nthe RBI has taken corrective action. Now, the flow is a positive flow. For example , in the \nfirst half of 2006- 07, they have already lifted and released 239 million pieces, and in the \nsecond half of 2006- 07, they have lifted and released 917 million pieces. The RBI has \nassured me that shortages which have been noticed in Maharashtra, Kar nataka, the \nNorth-East and Orissa, will be addressed over the next 4 to 6 weeks. \nSHRIMATI JAVA BACHCHAN: Sir, if what the hon. Minister says is correct, then \nI do not understand why this shortage is there. You did say that, but you have not given \nit in specific words. You are saying that the money is there. There are coins \n\nOral answers [14 AUG., 2007] to Questions     17 \nand the RBI has given a specific answer to you on that score. But, why are the coins not \navailable in the market? \nSHRI P. CHIDAMBARAM: As I tried to explain, certain issuing offices and certain \ncurrency chests, they may have made a wrong estimate of the demand in that area. \nTherefore, they are correcting that by increasing the supply to those currency chests. \nThis has been corrected substan tially. There are still some pockets in which this \nmismatch is to be corrected, and this mismatch is being corrected. \nMR. CHAIRMAN: Shri Ajay Maroo...{Interruption). \nSHRIMATI JAYA BACHCHAN: Sir, I have not asked my second supplementary. \nMR. CHAIRMAN: You have already asked your second question. \nश्री  अजय मारू  : महोदय , अभी  मंत्री  महोदय  ने  अपने  \nजवाब  म�  कहा  �क  ...(व्यवधान )... \nश्री  मती  जया  बच्चन  : सर , आपने  तो  मुझे  स � कं ड  \nसप्ल�म�टर�  पू\nछने  का  मौका  ह�  नह�ं  �दया  ।   \nश्री  सभाप�त  : आपने  दो  सवाल  पू\nछ  �लए  ह�  \n...(व्यवधान )... \nश्री  मती  जया  बच्चन  : नह�ं , सर  ।  अभी  मैन�  एक ह�  \nसवाल  पू\nछा  है  ...(व्यवधान )... \nश्री  अजय मारू  : महोदय , कई ऐसे  प्रदे श  है , जहां  पर  \nअभी  भी  �सक्क�  क�  कमी  है , िजसम�  मेरा  प्रदे श  झारखंड , \nसाथ  ह�  उड़ीसा , पिश्चमी  बंगाल  और �बहार  भी  आते  ह�  ।  इन  \nसभी  प्रदे श�  म�  हमने  �सक्क�  क�  कमी  महसू\nस  क�  है  ।  \nझारखंड  जैसे  प्रदे श  मे  इस कमी  का  एक प्रमु\nख  कारण  है  �क  \n�सक्क�  को  गला  कर उसे  धातु  के  रूप  म�  उपयोग  �कया  जाता  \nहै।  झारखंड  म�  ऐसे  कई �कस्से  सामने  आए ह�  ।  पु\n�लस  ने  \nपु\nरू�लया , जो  पिश्चमी  बंगाल  मे  आता  है , वहां  पर  भी  कई \nऐसे  स्थान�  पर  छापे  मारे  गए ह� , जहां  पर  �सक्क�  को  \nगलाया  जा  रहा  है  ।   \nम�  मंत्री  महोदय  से  यह  जानना  चाहता  हूं  �क  क्या  यह  \nबात  सह�  है  या  नह�ं  ह�  ? क्या  मंत्री  महोदय  के  ध्यान  \nम�  यह  आया  है  ‍ि◌क �सक्क�  को  गलाकर  उसे  अन्य  प्रकार  के  \nउपयोग  म�  लाया  जा  रहा  है  ? \n \nSHRI P. CHID AMBARAM: Sir, he is right. We have received reports of coins \nbeing melted because earlier, they were curpro -nickel coins, coins made out of copper \nand nickel. We have since changed the metal to FSS (Ferritic Stainless Steel). The \nearlier coins which were m ade of curpro- nickel, reports have come of being melted, \nreports have come of some coins being smuggled. But that is an issue which has to be \naddressed as a larger issue of law and order. But today, we have switched over to FSS \ncoins. FSS coins, I don't think there is a chance of melting FSS coins because there is no \nadvantage in melting FSS coins. \nMR. CHAIRMAN: Mr. Robert Kharshiing. ...(Interruptions)... \nSHRI JANARDHANA POOJARY: Sir, this is not the first time that we are facing \nthis shortage. In 1980s, t here was shortage of coins. Shortage was there earlier also. Sir, \nI do not know whether the Finance Minister is aware of the fact that hoarding is taking \nplace. And black marketing is also being done. The hon. Member from Chhattisgarh has \nstated that melti ng is also taking place. Sir, whether the hon. Finance  Minister has  \nmade an assessment so far as the shortage  of coins is \n\n18     Oral answers [RAJYA SABHA] to Questions \nconcerned; this is part (a) of my question. Then, Sir, part (b) is whether he has t aken \nany steps to take action against these hoarders; whether the hon. Finance Minister  \nhas taken steps to address the problem, and whether he has taken the assistance of  \nRailways to supply these coins to the remote areas, particularly the rural areas. Thank \nyou, Sir.  \n/ SHRI P. CHIDAMBARAM: Sir, the only reason for possible hoarding could be, \nif there is an advantage, if there is an opportunity to make a gain, for melting the coins. \nAs long as the coins were curpro -nickel coins, there were cases of melting , there were \nreports of melting; I do not deny that. But, then, this is, as I said, a law and order issue \nwhich has to be addressed by the law and order agencies of the State Government and \nthe Central Government. RBI is responsible for distributing the co ins. RBI's attention \nhas been drawn, on a number of occasions, to shortages which have shown up in some \nparts of the country. RBI has admitted that shortages have shown up in some parts of the \ncountry. They have taken corrective steps, and certainly, I wil l pass on the suggestion of \nthe hon. Member that RBI should liaise with the Ministry of Railways to see that coins \nare transported to remote parts of the country so that the banks and the currency chest \nbranches in those parts of the country are also adequ ately replenished. I shall certainly \npass on the suggestion to the RBI. \n \nश्री  अमर  �सहं  : माननीय  सभाप�त  महोदय , म�  आ प के  \nमाध्यम  से  माननीय  �वत्त  मंत्री  जी  से  पूछना  चाहूंगा  �क  \nआ प के सं�ान  म�  क्या  यह  है  �क  कु छ  स्मॉल  आपॅरे टर  लोग  \n�सक्क�  को  �पघला  कर अ�धक  मू\nल्य  प्राप्त  करने  के  �लए  \nउसे  बाजार  म�  बेच  रह�  ह� , साथ  ह�  उसे  बांग्लादेश  म�  \nभेज  रहे  ह�  ।  अगर  इस तरह  क�  कोई  घटना  हुई  है  तो  क्या  \nउनक�  �गरफ्तार�  हुई  ह�  ?  \n \nक्या  आपने  � स क् के  बनाने  के  �लए  कोई  अन्य  �वकल्प  खोजा  \nहै  �क  � स क् के  ऐल्यु�म�नयम , �स�लकॉन  या  फाइबर  से  बनाएं  \nजांए  ? इस तरह  से  �सक्क�  को  �पघला  कर उसका  ज्यादा  \nमूल ् ‍य प्राप्त  करने  का  कायर्  कु छ  फज�  लोग�  ने  चला  \nरखा  ह� , �वशेष  रूप  से  कोलकाता  म�  इस प्रकार  के  �गरोह  \nह�  ।  क्या  इस प्रकार  के  �कसी  �गरोह  के  बारे  म�  आपको  \nकोई  जानकार�  है   अथवा  आपने  इस संबध  म�  कु छ  \n�गरफ्ता�रयां  क�  ह�  ? या  जैसे  आपने  स्वंय  अभी  माना  ह�  \n�क  आ प के मंत्रायल  ने  गलत estimate �कया , इस�लए  �सक्क�  क�  \nइस तरह  से  unavailability ह� , तो  क्या  आ प के मंत्रालय  ने  यह  \nभी  आ प के सं�ान  म�  नह�ं  डाला  है  �क  कु छ  फज�  लोग  इस \nतरह  से  �सक्क�  को  �पघला  कर के  चोर�  का  काम  कर रहे  ह�  ? \nSHRI P. CHIDAMBARAM: Sir, I have already answered it and I am happy to \nanswer again. Yes, there were reports of melting of coins. That is when the coins were \ncurpro-nickel coins. We replaced curpro-nickel coins by ferritic stainless steel coins and, \ntherefore, I think the opportunity or arbitrage opportunity for melting stainless steel \ncoins will not arise. Action has been taken against people who have been suspected of \nmelting. But these are within the powers of the State and the law and order agencies of \nthe Central Government. I don't have any information about specific cases being filed \nagainst anyone. But this information has come to our knowledge and one of the reasons \nwhy we switched over from curpro -nickel to FSS is because we wanted to a void the \nopportunity to melt these coins. \nMR. CHAIRMAN: Q. No. 46. (Interruptions).. Please. Shrimati Sushma Swaraj. \n(Interruptions).. \n\nOral answers [14 AUG, 2007] to Questions     19 \n \nश्री  �वनय  क�टयार  : सर , एक �मनट  ...( व्यवधान )...दो  \nरूपये  के  � स क् के  पर  से  महात्मा  गांधी  जी  गायब  हो  गये , \nतो  उसम�  यह  �नशान  �कसका  है  ...(व्यवधान )... \nMR. CHAIRMAN: Please sit down, (Interruptions)... Q. No. 46. \nश्री  �वनय  क�टयार  : क्या  माननीय  मंत्री  जी  बतांएगे  \n�क  यह  क्या  ह�  ? ...(व्यवधान )...  \nश्री  सभप�त  : क्वैश्चन   न. 46 ।  \n \nडु प् ल � के ट  पैन  काड�  को  रद्द  �कया  जाना  \n \n * 46 श्रीमती  सुषमा  स्वराज  : क्या  �वत्त  मंत्री  यह  \nबताने  क�  कृ पा  कर� गे  �क : \n \n(क)      यह  सच है  �क  13 लाख  डु प् ल � के ट  पैन  काड्र्स  \nरद्द  �कए  जा  रह�  ह� , \n \n(ख) डु प् ल � के ट  पैन  काड्र्स  को  रोकने  के  �लए  \nबायोमै�ट्रक  पैन  काड्र्स  कब तक जार�  �कए  जाएंगे , और  \n \n(ग) क्या   बायोमै�ट्रक  पैन  काड्र्स  जार�  होने  पर  \nमौजूदा  पैन  काड्र्स  भी  मान्य  ह�गे ? \n \n�वत्तमंत्री  ( श्री  पी .�चदम्बरम ) :  ( क) से  ( ग) एक \n�ववरण  सभा  पटल  पर  रख �दया  गया  है  ।   \n \n�ववरण  \n \n(क) डी  – डु प् ल � के श न  से  संब�धत  कारर्वाई  पूर�  होने  के  \nउपरांत  ह�  उन डु प् ल � के ट  पैन  काड�  क�  कु ल  संख्या , \nिजन्ह�  रद्द  �कए  जाने  क�  संभावना  ह� , का  पता  चल स के ग ा  \n।  अब तक कर - �नधार्रण  अ�धका�रय�  द्वारा  11.43 लाख  पैन  \nधारक�  के  संबध  म�  डी  \n– डु प् ल � के श न  क�  कारर्वाई  पूर�  \nक�  जा  चु\nक�  ह�  ।   \n(ख) यह  मामला  �वचारधीन  ह� , �कन्तु  इस समय उस समय \nसीमा  का  उल्लेख  करना  संभव  नह�ं  होगा  �क  कब तक \nबायोम��ट्रक  पैन  काड्र्स  जार�  �कए  जाएंगे  ।   \n \n(ग) जी , हां  ।  परवत�  स्क�म  के  लागू  होने  के  पश्चात  \nजब तक पैन  काडर्  धारक  / धा�रती  अपने  पैन  काडर्  को  नए \nबायोमै�ट्रक  काडर्  से  बदल  नह�ं  लेता  / लेती  है  ।   \nCancellation of duplicate PAN cards \n†*46. SHRIMATI SUSHMA SWARAJ: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased \nto state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that 13 lakh duplicate PAN cards are being cancelled; \n(b) by when the Biometric PAN cards will be issued in order to check duplicate \nPAN cards; and \n(c) whether current PAN cards will be valid even after the issuance of Biometric \nPAN cards? \n†Original notice of the (question was received in Hindi. \n\n20     Oral answers [RAJYA SABHA] to \nQuestions \nTHE MINISTER OF FINANCE (SHRI P. CHIDAMBARAM): (a) to (c): A  \nStatement is laid on the Table of the House. \nStatement \n(a) The total number of duplicate PAN cards, which are likely to be cancelled, \ncan only be ascertained after the exercise relating to de- duplication is over. So far, \nthe de-duplication work has been compl eted by the Assessing Officers in respect of \n11.43 lakh PAN holders. \n(b) The matter is under consideration, but at present it would not be possible to \nindicate a time frame by which bio-metric PAN cards will be issued. \n(c) Yes, until the PAN card holder replaces h is/her card by a new bio -metric \nPAN card when the latter scheme is introduced. \n \nश्री  मती  सुषमा  स्वराज  : सभाप�त  जी , म�  मंत्री  \nमहोदय  से  जाना  चाहती  हूं  ‍ि◌क मेरे  सवाल  के  “सी ” भाग  म�  \nम�ने  पूछा  था  �क  ...(व्यवधान )... \nSHRI P. CHIDAMBARAM: No, Madam. First  you p ut your question: \n{Interruptions)... Madam, first you put your question. {Interruptions)... \nSHRIMATI SUSHMA SWARAJ: ... \"whether current PAN cards will be valid \neven after the issuance of biometric PAN cards\". \nSHRI P. CHIDAMBARAM: Madam, first you put the question, then I will \nanswer. \nSHRIMATI SUSHMA SWARAJ: Q. No. 46. \nSHRI P. CHIDAMBARAM: \"(a) to (c) A statement is laid on the Table of the \nHouse\". \n \nश्री  मती  सुषमा  स्वराज  : मै  तो  समय बचा  रह�  थी  ।  \nआपने  स्टेडम�ट  म�  टाइम  लगा  �दया।  यह  तो  म�  पढ़  चुक�  \nथी  ।   \nसभाप�त  जी , म�  यह  जानना  चाहती  हूं  �क  मैन�  अपने  \nक्वैश्चन  के  भाग  “सी ” म�  पू\nछा  था  �क  आपने  इसका  जवाब  \n�दया  है  �क - “(c) whether current PAN cards will be v alid even after the \nissuance of biometric PAN cards\", आपने इसका जवाब �दया है �क “Yes, until the PAN \ncard holder replaces his/her card by a new biometric PAN card when the latter \nscheme is introduced\". \n \nम�  आपसे  यह  जानना  चाहती  हूं  �क  आपका  यह  जवाब  \nसुनकर  तथा  पढ़कर  यह  इम्प्रेशन  बनता  है  ‍ि◌क बायोमै�टर्क  \nपैन  काडर्  जार�  हो  जाने  के  बाद  भी  यह  इच्छा  पैन  काडर्  \nहोल्डसर्  क�  होगी  �क  वह  कर� ट  पैन  काडर्  रखे  तो  \nबायोमै�ट्रक  पैन  काडर्  को  यू\nज  कर�  ।  अगर  मेरा  यह  \nइम्प्रेशन  सह�  है , तो  क्या  आ प के द्वारा  इंट्रोड्यूस  \nक�  जाने  वाल�  बायोमै�ट्रक  पैन  काडर्  क�  योजना  \n�रट� ड�ट  नह�ं  हो  जाएगी  क्य��क  अपनी  इच्छा  से  कोई  \nबायोमै�ट्रक  पैन  काडर्  क्य�  लेगा  ?  \nSHRI P. CHIDAMBARAM: Sir, let me clarify. There are lakhs of PAN cards. \nWhen we introduce the scheme, the new PAN card will be a biometric PAN card for \nthe new applicant who wants a PAN card. But I can't overnight ask lakhs of PAN \ncard holders to replace their cards by biometric PAN cards. The present thinking -it is \nonly present thinking—is that we will allow the option of either to use the old ca rd or \n\nto \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Starred questions     21 \nsurrender the old card and apply for a biometric card. Over a period of time, we will \nreplace all the old cards by biometric cards. But since this can't be done overnight \nwithout dislocating the system, initially  the idea is to give the option to the old PAN \ncard holder, \"either you use your old PAN card or please surrender it and apply for a \nbiometric PAN card\", \n \nश्री  मती  सुषमा  स्वराज  : सर , मेर�  स � कं ड  सिप्लम�टर�  \nयह  है  �क  अभी  आपने  दे खा  होगा  �क  कु छ  �दन�  पहले  उड़ीसा  \nऔर महाराष्ट्र  म�  ऐसे  के स े ज़  आएं  ।  जहां  तीन  या  चार  \nमह�ने  के  बच्च�  के  भी  पैन  काड्र्स  बने  हुए  ह�  ।  आज के  \nपैन  काडर्  पर  के व ल  तस्वीर  लगती  ह�  ।  अगर  वह  पैन  काडर्  \nआगे  तक चल जाएगा  तो  एक साल  बाद  या  दो  साल  बाद  जब बच्चा  \nबड़ा  होगा  तो  तस्वीर  उसक�  बदल  जाएगी  क्य��क  उसका  चेहरा  \nबदल  जाएगा  ।  तो  क्या  आप यह  कर� गे  �क  सबसे  पहले  ये  जो  \nछोटे  बच्च�  के  पैन  काड्र्स  ह� , इनको  बायौमै�ट्रक  पैन  \nकाडर्  पर  डाल� , क्य� ‍‍�क पैन  काडर्  म�  जो  �डटेल्स  होती  \nहै , वह  कभी  भी  बदलती  नह�ं  ह�  ?  \n \nSHRI P. CHIDAMBARAM: Sir, these are extremely rare cases where the child is an \nassessee, and the father has to apply and the father has to sign. These are very rare cases, \nthree or four cases.But he will soon introduce a biometric card system. Once the \nbiometric card system is introduced, all  new PAN cards issued after that date will be \nonly biometric PAN cards. \nMR. CHAIRMAN: Question Hour is over. \n__________ \nWRITTEN ANSWERS TO STARRED QUESTIONS \nCPSEs in the country \n*42. SHRI DARA SINGH: Will the Minister of HEAVY INDUSTRIES AND PUBLIC \nENTERPRISES be Pleased to state: \n(a) what is the number of Central Public Sector Enterprises (CPSEs) at present \nfunctioning in the country; \n(b) the details of CPSEs conferred with 'Navratna' status and criteria laid down \ntherefor; \n(c) what is the total investment  made in such CPSEs during the year 2006 -07 as \ncompared to the year 2005- 06, State -wise indicating the total dividend paid to the \nGovernment in the year 2005-06; \n(d) what was the total contribution to Government through dividend, interest and \ntaxes as on 1.4.2006; and \n(e) what is the percentage of share in GDP which comes from the above \nmentioned CPSEs? \nTHE MINISTER OF HEAVY INDUSTRIES AND PUBLIC ENTERPRISES (SHRI \nSONTOSH MOHAN DEV): (a) As on 31.3.2006, there were 245 Central Public Sector \nEnterprises (CPSEs) in the country. \n\n22      Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Starred questions \n(b) Presently, there are 12 CPSEs which have been conferred Navratna status.  \nThese 12 CPSEs are (i) Bharat Electronics Limited, Bangalore (ii) Bharat Heavy  \nElectricals Limited, New  Delhi (iii) Bharat Petroleum Corporation Limited, Mumbai  \n(iv) GAIL (India) Limited, New Delhi (v) Hindustan Aeronautics Limited, Bangalore  \n(vi) Hindustan Petroleum Corporation Limited, Mumbai (vii) Indian Oil Corporation  \nLimited, New Delhi (viii) Mahanagar Telephone Nigam Limited, New Delhi (ix) NTPC  \nLimited, New Delhi (x) Oil & Natural Gas Corporation Limited, Dehradun (xi) Power  \nFinance Corporation Limited, New Delhi and (xii) Steel Authority of India Limited,  \nNew. Delhi. \nThe following criteria are adopted for conferment of Navratna status to CPSEs:—  \n(i)     CPSE should be of Schedule 'A' category; \n(ii)    CPSE'should be Miniratna Category - I; \n(iii)   CPSE should have obtained 'excellent' or 'very good' MOU: rating in three \nof the last five years; and \n(iv)   CPSE should have obtained a composite score of 60 or above in six \nselected performance indicators.  \n(c) As per available information, investments made in these 12 Navratna CPSEs as \non 31.3.2006 stood at Rs. 88,263 crore. The dividend paid to Government by  these 12 \nNavratna CPSEs in the year 2005 -06 was Rs. 11,406.64 crore. The details are given in \nthe enclosed Statement (See below). \n(d) The 12 Navratna CPSEs contributed Rs. 80,718 crore to the Government by way \nof dividend, interest on Government loans, taxes and duties. \n(e) As per the Quick Estimates made by Central Statistical Organization, the share of \nthese 12 Navratna CPSEs in the Gross Domestic Product (at factor cost) for the year \n2005-06 was 2.74%. \nStatement \nDetails of Dividend paid to Government by \nNavratna CPSEs during the year 2005-06 \n(all figures in Rs. crore)  \nSI. Name of Navratna CPSE Headquarters D ividend \ni No.   to Governm\n 1 2 3  4 \n1. Bharat Electronics Limited Bangalore. Karnataka  67.97 \n2 Bharat Heavy Electricals Limited New Delhi  282.00 \n3. Bharat Petroleum Corporation Limited Mumbai. Maharashtra  148.95 \n4. GAIL (India)  Limited New Delhi  581.87 \n5. Hindustan Aeronautics Limited Bangalore. Karnataka  252.51 \n6. Hindustan Petroleum Corporation \nLi i d  \nMumbai. Maharashtra  173.08 \n7. Indian Oil Corporation Limited New Delhi  958.08 \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Starred questions     23 \n1 2 3 4 \n8. Mahanagar Telephone Nigam Limited New Delhi 194.91 \n 9. NTPC Limited New Delhi 2638.60 \n10.  Oil & Natural Gas Corporation Limited Dehradun, Uttarakhand 4757.30 \n11. Power Finance Corporation Limited New Delhi 270.24 \n12. Steel Authority of India Limited New Delhi 1081.13 \n                               TOTAL:   11406.64 \nSource:  DPE Survey 2005-06 \nIrrigation projects in Andhra Pradesh \n*47. SHRI M.V. MYSURA REDDY: Will the Minister of WATER RESOURCES be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether Government are aware of the progress of the ongoing irrigation projects \nin Andhra Pradesh; \n(b) if so, the details thereof alongwith the time-frame for completion, project- wise; \n(c) whether any fresh project proposals terming as National Importance have been \nreceived from the State Government of Andhra Pradesh for assistance as a special \npackage to development the backward areas of the State; and \n(d) if so; the details thereof alongwith  status and the estimated assistance being \nearmarked? \nTHE MINISTER OF WATER RESOURCES (PROF. SAIF -UD-DIN SOZ): (a) As \nreported by Government of Andhra Pradesh, there are 37 Major and 17 Medium \nongoing irrigation projects in the State of Andhra Pradesh. Det ails of the projects are \ngiven in the enclosed Statement-I (See below). \n(b) Details of the projects along with project -wise time frame of their completion \nare given in the enclosed Statement-II (See below). \n(c) No sir'; \n(d) Does not arise. \nStatement-I \nIrrigation Projects of Andhra Pradesh \nMajor Irrigation Projects  \nSI. \nNo \nName of the Project Status of \nInvestment \nClearance \nYear of \ncompletion \n1 2 3 4 \n1 \n2 \nVamsadhara Project Stg-II Ph-I \nVamsadhara Project Stg-II Ph-II \nApproved \nApproved \n2007-08 \n2009-10 \n\n24      Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Starred questions  \n1 2 3 4 \n3