 GLIS Unapproved 2010-11 \n34. Sripadasagar (Yellampally) LIS Unapproved 2009-10 \n35. Rajiv Sagar LIS Unapproved .2010-11 \n36. Indira Sagar LIS Unapproved 2010-11 \n37. Singur canals Approved 2008-09 \nStatement-II \nMedium Irrigation Projects  \nSI. Name of the Project Status of Year of \nNo  Investment \nClearance \ncompletion \n1 2 3 4 \n1. Tarakarama thirthasagaram Unapproved 2010-11 \n2. Peddagedda Reservoir Unapproved 2008-09 \n3   . Jhanjhavathi Project Unapproved 2008-09 \n4 Musurumilli Project Approved 2009-10 \n5 Bupathipalem Approved 2008-09 \n6 Swarnamuki Barrage Approved 2009-10 \n7 Veligallu Approved 2007 -08 \n8 Neelwai Approved 2007 -08 \n\n26     Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Starred questions  \n1 2 3 4 \n9. Ralivagu Approved 2007-08 \n10. Gollavagu Approved 2007 -08 \n11. Peddavagu near Jagannathpur Approved 2008-09. \n12. Komaram Bheem Approved 2008-09 \n13. Mathadivagu Approved 2007 -08 \n14. Modikuntavagu Unapproved 2009-10 \n15. Kinnerasani (1.5 TMC part of \nKinnerasani. project) \nUnapproved 2009-10 \n16. Palemvagu Approved 2007-08 \n17. Choutapally Hanumanthareddy Unapproved 2008-09 \nVoted and charged budget estimates \n*48. SHRI SANTOSH BAGRODIA: \nSHRI GIREESH KUMAR SANGHI: \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n(a) the details of voted and charged BEs and R\\Es of the last three years; \n(b) the financial year -wise details of the Voted and Charged components of the \nSupplementary Demands for Grants during last three years; \n(c) whether it is a fact that the charged component has increased over the years; and  \n(d) if so, reasons therefor and measures to alter the situation? \nTHE MINISTER OF FINANCE (SHRI P. CHIDAMBARAM): (a) The details of \nvoted and charged Budget Estimates and Revised Estimates of the last 3 years are as \nunder: \n(Rupees in crore)  \nFinancial Year Budget Estimates Revised Estimates \n Charged Voted Charged Voted \n2004-05 522378.55 339299.91 693264.17 345120.94 \n2005-06 767678.08 384843.50 1192373.71 398600.21 \n2006-07 1280587.12 448109.43 1631966.29 503688.79 \n(b) The details of the Voted and Charged components of the Supplementary \nDemands for Grants for last three years are as under: \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG, 2007] Starred questions     27 \n(Rupees in crore)  \nFinancial Year Charged Voted \n2004-05 181491.16 26400.38 \n2005-06 425495.21 38170.56 \n2006-07 355506.12 100523.53 \n(c) Yes Sir. \n(d) The rise in charged expenditure is mainly due to the increase in — (i) Interest \nPayments; (ii) Repa yment of Debt and (iii)  Grants-in-aid to State Governments, in \nterms of the award of the 12th Finance Commission. Government has been following a \ncomprehensive strategy to moderate the debt/liabilities which, inter -alia, include \nreduction of fiscal deficit in a phased manner, as mandated by Fiscal Responsibility and \nBudget Management Act, 2003, various debt restructuring measures, general policy on \nbench marking of administered interest rate etc. It may be observed that while Interest \nPayments and Repayment  of Debt has shown an increase in absolute terms during the \nlast 3 years upto 2006- 07, the interest burden both as a proportion of total expenditure \nand as percentage of GDP has actually come down during the said period. \nAid to industrial units at Kutch, Gujarat \n*49.  SHRI KANJIBHAI PATEL: \nSHRI KESHUBHAI S. PATEL: \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to refer to answer to Unstarred Question \n756 given in the Rajya Sabha on the 1st August, 2006'. and state: \n(a) the details of relevant factors which have compel led Government not to grant \nany further extension of the time limit beyond 31st  December, 2005 to units located at \nKutch in Gujarat; and \n(b) whether Government have any proposal to help at least the pipeline cases and \nthe industrial units which are being set u p in this remote drought prone area and could \nnot get the benefit of excise duty exemption? \nTHE MINISTER OF FINANCE (SHRI P. CHIDAMBARAM): (a) Following \nearthquake in Kutch region of Gujarat in January, 2001, Government had, on 31st July. \n2001 extended an excise duty exemption scheme for new units to be set up in the Kutch \ndistrict of Gujarat. The intention was to attract immediate fresh investment so as to help \nemployment generation. To ensure time bound investments, a time period of two years \nwas initially prescribed for establishing new units for being eligible for the exemption. \nSubsequently, Government had received requests for seeking extension of the time \nperiod for setting up of new units. Taking into consideration these representations, \nGovernment has thrice extended the time limit for setting up new units as under: \n0      First, from 31st July, 2003 to 31st July, 2004, \n0      Second, from 31st July, 2004 to 31st  December, 2004 and \n\n28     Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Starred questions \n0      Finally, from 31st December, 2004 to 31st December, 2005. \nWhile examining the request for extending the period for setting of new units \nbeyond 31st December, 2004, it was observed that any further extension of period for \nsetting up of new units, would defeat the very purpose of the exemption scheme to \nattract immediate fresh investments and would only result in windfall gains for those \nunits which would now be investing in Kutch, much after the earthquake when the \nconditions have improved. In any case, as a policy, Government is not in favour of \nproliferation of area based exemption, as they result in economic distortions, reduce tax \nbase and generally do not result in attracting Jong-term investments. \nHowever, keeping in view the requests from Government of Gujarat, final extension \nwas granted upto 31st December,. 2005. While granting the extension upto 31 st \nDecember, 2005, Government of Gujarat was specifically requested to inform the \ninvestors that there would be no further extension of the exemption beyond 31.12.2005 \nand that potential investors should be accordingly advised to expedite their investments. \n(b) No. Sir. \nInvestment for Purchasing dollar to control appreciation of rupee \n*50. DR. E.M. SUDARSANA NATCHIAPPAN: Will the Minister of FINANCE be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether Government and RBI are investing huge money to purchase dollars just \nto control the appreciation of Indian rupee; \n(b) if so, whether it is one of the reasons for inflation in India; and \n(c) whether Government would expedite the implementation of  the scheme of rupee \ndollar convert ability?  \nTHE MINISTER OF FINANCE (SHRI P. CHIDAMBARMA): (a) The exchange rate \npolicy of the Reserve Bank of India is guided by the broad, principles of careful \nmonitoring and management of exchange rates with flexibilit y, without a fixed or a pre -\nannounced target or a band, coupled with the ability to intervene as and when necessary. \nThe overall approach to the management of India's foreign exchange reserves takes into \naccount the changing composition of the balance of payments and 'liquidity risks' \nassociated with different types of flows and other requirements. Excess volatility in \nforeign exchange markets is contained through intervention as and when necessary and \nfollowed up by sterilisation of liquidity through issua nce of treasury bills/dated \nsecurities under the Market Stabilisation Scheme. \n(b) The Reserve Bank of India has been sterilising the liquidity impact of such \nforeign exchange purchases, placing emphasis on price stability and anchoring inflation \nexpectations. Point-to-point inflation -as measured by wholesale price index declined \nfrom 5.94 per cent at end -March, 2007 to 4.45 per cent in the week ending July 28, \n2007. \n(c) The movement towards fuller convertibility of Rupee will necessarily have to be \ngradual, sequenced and calibrated to the overall macroeconomic situation and emerging \nneeds of the economy. \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Starred questions     29 \nStrike by ground staff of Indian' \n†*51. SHRI SATYAVRAT CHATURVEDI: \nSHRI MOTILAL VORA: \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be pleased to state: \n(a) the number of days for which the employees of 'Indian' were on strike in \nJune,2007 and the reasons therefor; \n(b) the total financial loss incurred to the 'Indian' due to strike; and \n(c) the agreement reached between 'Indian ' and employees and by when it would be \nimplemented? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI \nPRAFUL PATEL): (a) Employees of Indian Airlines covered by Air Corporation \nEmployees Union (ACEU) had gone on flash strike w.e.f. night shirt of 12th June, 2007 \nall over India. The strike was called off on the afternoon of 14th June, 2007. ACEU had \nteen demanding revision of wages and allowances w.e.f. 1 .1.1997 and better career \nprogression. The negotiations in this regard were initiated in Feb ruary, 2007 but, when \nno satisfactory agreement was reached and the ACEU adopted an agitational approach, \nthe matter was seized with Chief Labour Commissioner (Central). Even as the \nconciliation proceedings were underway with the Chief Labour Commissioner \n(Central), the ACEU suddenly called for a flash strike. \n(b) The estimated loss incurred by Indian Airlines (including Alliance Air) due to \nstrike is Rs.3.5 Crores by way of loss of contribution, additional expenditure on hotel, \nfood and conveyance charges for disrupted flights etc. \n(c) Memorandum of Settlement covering revision of pay scales and allowances, \npayment of arrears, career progression of specified category of workmen etc. has been \nsigned in conciliation with the representatives of ACEU on 15th, June 2007. The revision \nof pay scales will be effected in the salary of August 2007. However, the first \ninstallment of arrears has already been paid in July 2007. \nPurchase of Serbian Airlines by Air India \n*52. SHRI GIREESH KUMAR SANGHI: \nSHRI SANTOSH BAGRODIA: \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government agrees with the reports regarding proposed purchase of the \nSerbian Airlines by the Air India; \n(b) whether it is a fact that the said Airlines have incurred losses over past several \nyears; if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether it is also a fact that the fleet of the said airlines is old and requires costly \nmaintenance; \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\n30      Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Starred questions \n(d) if so, the rationale of such proposal; and \n(e) whether the cost of acquisition will be shared by Government and if so, the \ndetails thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI \nPRAFUL PATEL): (a) to (e) Air India have informed that, there is no p roposal for \npurchase of Serbian Airlines by them. However, they had received a suggestion from \nJAT airways of Serbia for a strategic relationship with Air India. No decision has been \ntaken. \nRestoration of old havelis in Chandni Chowk, Delhi \n*53. SHRIMATI SYEDA ANWARA TAIMUR: \nSHRI VIJAY J. DARDA: \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government propose to restore the old havelis in Chandni Chowk and \nnearby areas of old Delhi, some of which are also historically important and can  \nbecome major tourist attraction; and \n(b) if so, what action Government propose to take to restore the charm and  \ngrandeur of Old Delhi which existed during the past? \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM (SHRIMATI AMBIKA SONI): (a) and (b) \nDevelopment and promotion of tourism  is undertaken primarily by the State \nGovernments/UT Administrations. The Ministry of Tourism, Government of India, \nextends financial assistance to the State Governments/UT Administrations for tourism \nrelated projects which are identified in consultation a nd interaction with them under \nschemes for Product/Infrastructure Development of Destinations/Circuits and Assistance \nto Large Revenue Generating Projects. However, no such project proposal for \nrestoration of old havelis in Chandni Chowk has been submitted  by the Department of \nTourism, Government of NCT of Delhi. \nNew pension fund bill for private sector  \n*54. SHRI P R RAJAN: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government are considering a New Pension Fund Bill for private \nsector; and \n(b) if so, the nature and details of the legislation? \nTHE MINISTER OF FINANCE (SHRI P. CHIDAMBARAM) (a) and (b) The \nPension Fund Regulatory and Development Authority Bill, 2005 was introduced in the \nParliament in March, 2005 to, inter -alia, regulate the New Pen sion System and to \nestablish a Pension Fund Regulatory and Development Authority to promote old age \nincome security by establishing, developing and regulating pension funds and to protect \nthe interests of subscribers to schemes of pension funds. The Bill w as referred to the \nStanding Committee on Finance, which examined it and recommended the same for \nenactment subject to some modifications/amendment The Bill is pending before the \nParliament \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Starred questions     31 \nDevelopment of Tourism in Karnataka \n*55. SHRI RAJEEV CHANDRASEKHAR: Will the Minister of TOURISM be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether any proposals/suggestions made by the State Government of Karnataka \nfor the development of tourism in the State are pending with Jhe Central Government \nfor approval/clearance; and \n(b) if so, the details thereof and by when final approval/clearance of the Central \nGovernment is likely to be accorded, case-wise? \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM (SHRIMATI AMBIKA SONI): (a) and (b) \nDevelopment and promotion of places of tourist interests/spots is primarily undertaken \nby the State Governments/UT Administrations themselves. However, the Ministry of \nTourism provides Central Financial Assistance under the following schemes to State \nGovernments/Union Territories: \n(i) Product/Infrastructure development for destinations and circuits \n(ii) Rural Tourism Development \n(iii) Large Revenue Generating projects \n(iv) Fairs/Festivals/Events \n(v) Information Technology \nThe following project proposals have been received from the State Government of \nKarnataka:  \nSI. No. Name of the project \n1. Rural tourism project at Gokarna, District Uttar Kannada. \n2. Rural tourism at Village llkal, District Bagalkore \n3. Channapatana rural development. \n4. Rural Tourism development at Village Kumta,  District Uttara \n Kannada. \n5. Village Sagar, District Shimoga - Rural tourism project. \n6. Integrated development of tourism circuit on  Bijapur-Bidar- \n Gulbarga. \n7. Development of Eco-Tourism Park at Hanumantanagar, Maddur \n Taluk Mandya District. \n8. Development of Bijapur-Bidar-Gulbarga-Pattadakal circuit. \n9. Destination development of Pilikula Nisargadhama near Mangalore. \n10. Development of Srirangapattana-Shivapura-Ramaswamy —  Freedom \n circuit. \n\n32  Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Starred questions \nThe project proposals, which are complete in all respect, are appraised as per \nguidelines and processed on the basis of inter-se-priority and funds released, subject to \navailability under the respective head during the concerned financial year. During the \nTenth Five Year Plan projects worth Rs. 9052.02 lakh have been sanctioned to the State \nGovernment of Karnataka for integrated development of tourism. \nOffices for Village Accountants \n*56. DR. VIJAY MALLYA: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n(a) how many offices of Village Accountants are functioning in Karnataka; \n(b) how many offices are functioning in rented premises, district-wise; \n(c) how many offices are functioning from Government -owned buildings, district -\nwise; and \n(d) what are the reasons for not getting Government-owned buildings? \nTHE MINISTER OF FINANCE (SHRI P. CHIDAMBARAM): (a) As per the \ninformation received from Government of Karnataka, it is learnt that there are 9213 \noffices of Village Accountants functioning in Karnataka spread over 27 districts. \n(b) to (d) The Government of Karnataka have stated that details in respect of the \noffices of Village Accountants functioning in rented premises and Government owned \nbuildings have to be procured from the District Collectors/village level and would take \nsome time in collecting. \nSixth Pay Commission \n*57. SHRI SHANTARAM LAXMAN NAIK: Will the Minister of FINANCE be \npleased to state: \n(a) the number of meetings of the Sixth Pay Commission held so far; \n(b) the subjects covered in these meetings; \n(c) whether an interim report is likely to be presented to the Government by the \ncommission; \n \n(d) the date by which the interim report is likely to be presented to Government; and  \n(e) the date by which final report will be submitted by the Commission to \nGovernment. \nTHE MINISTER O F FINANCE (SHRI P. CHIDAMBARAM): (a) to (e) The Sixth \nCentral Pay Commission has held 20 (twenty) meetings so far with various \nAssociations, Department Heads etc. wherein different issues relating to matters covered \nby the Terms of Reference of the Commiss ion were discussed. The Commission has \ninformed the Government that it expects to submit its final report within the stipulated \ndate, which is April 4, 2008. Hence no interim report is contemplated. \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Starred questions     33 \nRehabilitation of Indira Sagar Project oustees \n*58. SHRI K. CHANDRAN PILLAI: Will the Minister of WATER RESOURCES be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that the proper resettlement and rehabilitation of the \nOmkareshwar and Indira Sagar Project oustees have not been done;. \n(b) whether Narmada Tribunal Award and Supreme Court have given directions to \nGovernment towards the rehabilitation of Narmada dam oustees; \n(c) if so, the details thereof; and \n(d) the details of the action taken by Government in this regard? \nTHE MINISTER OF WATER RESOURCES (PROF. SAIF -UD-DIN SOZ): (a) \nResettlement and rehabilitation work of Omkareshwar and Indira Sagar Projects has \nbeen carried out in accordance with the rehabilitation policy of the Government of \nMadhya Pradesh dated 31.5.06. \nThe question related to the proper resettlement and rehabilitation of Indira Sagar \nProject oustees is under consideration in the Hon'ble Supreme Court through Special \nLeave Petition No.935 of 2007 filed by Narmada Bachao Andolan which is still \npending and h ence the matter is subjudice. Similarly, the matter related to \nOmkareshwar project is subjudice in Jabalapur High Court as the Narmada Bachao \nAndolan has filed a petition No.4457/07 in Jabalapur High Court. \n(b) Narmada Water Dispute Tribunal Award had not give n stipulations/directions \nwith respect to resettlement and rehabilitation of Omkareshwar and Narmada Sagar \n(now renamed as Indira Sagar Project) dam oustees. In respect of Indira Sagar dam, the \nmatter is under consideration in Hon'ble Supreme Court in SLP No.935 of 2007 as \nmentioned above. \n(c) and (d) Do not arise. \nMortgaging of gold for loans        \n†*59. SHRI JAI PARKASH AGGARWAL: Will the Minister of FINANCE be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether the Nationalised Banks are still following 1986 as a base year for \nadvancing loan by mortgaging gold with the banks; \n(b) if so, the details thereof: \n(c) whether Government propose to take any steps for granting loan by mortgaging \ngold with the banks as per the prevailing rates of gold instead that of the base year of \n1986; \n(d) if so, the details thereof; and \n(e) if not, the reasons therefor? \n†Original notice of the questions was received in Hindi. \n\n34      Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Starred questions \nTHE MINISTER OF FINANCE (SHRI R CHIDAMBARAM): (a) No, Sir. \n(b) Does not arise. \n(c) to (e) As per  extant RBI guidelines, scheduled commercial banks are permitted \nto grant loans to individuals only against gold ornaments and jewellery, and not against \nbullion/primary gold. For grant of such advances, the banks frame a suitable policy in \nthis regard wit h the approval of their Board of Directors, including ceiling for such \nloans, margin and rates of interest etc. Based on the prevailing market price of gold, the \nnationalized banks periodically revise the rate of gold for calculating the value of the \njewellery/ornaments for granting such loans. \nLPG and CNG retail units in Tamil Nadu \n*60. SHRI R. SHUNMUGASUNDARAM: Will the Minister of PETROLEUM AND \nNATURAL GAS be please to state: \n(a) the total quantum of LPG consumed by automobile owners in Tamil Nadu; \n(b) the number of LPG retail units which have been established in Tamil Nadu for \nautomobile users alongwith details in each town in Tamil Nadu; \n(c) the proposals to meet the growing demands; and \n(d) whether CNG retail units are functioning in Tamil Nadu and if so, how many, \ntown-wise? \nTHE MINISTER OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS (SHRI MURLI DEORA). \n(a) The total sales of auto -LPG through Auto LPG Dispensing Stations (ALDS) set up \nby the Public Sector Oil Marketing Companies (OMCs) during the year 2006 -07, is \n8751 Metric Tonnes (MT s) and during April -June, 2007 is 3447 MTs in the State of \nTamil Nadu. \n(b) OMCs are presently operating 34 ALDS in the State of Tamil Nadu. The \ndistrict/town-wise details are given in the enclosed Statement (See below). \n(c) OMCs propose to set up 37 more ALDS in the State of Tamil Nadu. \n(d) No, Sir. \nStatement \nDetails of Auto LPG dispensing Stations (ALDS) in the State \nof Tamil Nadu as on 30.06.2007  \nDistrict/town where ALDS are operating Number of ALDS \n1 2    \nChennai 22 \nTiruvallur 2 \nSalem 1 \nMadurai 4 \nKanchipuram 2 \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions     35  \n1 2 \nCoimbatore \nErode \n2 \n1 \nGRAND TOTAL: 34 \nWRITTEN ANSWERS TO UNSTARRED QUESTIONS \nOpposition to the merger of Air India and Indian \n286. DR. M.A.M. RAMASWAMY: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether Government have finalized the merger of Air India and Indian; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether some employees unions have opposed the merger; and \n(d) if so, the reaction of Government in this regard? \nTHE MINIST ER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI \nPRAFUL PATEL): (a) and (b) Yes/ Sir. The Government has decided to merge the two \nairlines with a new. company. For this purpose, a new company, National Aviation \nCompany of India Limited (NACIL) has been incorporated on 30.3.2007. The legal and \nprocedural formalities for merger would be completed shortly and the complete merger \nof the two airlines is expected to be effected in a phased manner over the next two \nyears. \n(c) and (d) Some employee unions have s ubmitted a representation expressing their \nconcerns on the merger. On receipt of the representation, Government had invited the \nconcerned Unions/Associations on 27.07.2007 and had allayed their apprehensions and \nreiterated that all employees would remain unaffected as a result of the merger. \nNew aviation policy \n†287. SHRI PYARELAL KHANDELWAL: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that a new aviation policy has been formulated by \nGovernment; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether it is also a fact that Ministry of Defence have raised some objections \nwith regard to the newly laid policy; and \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\n36     Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \n(d) if so, the action taken by Government to overcome the objection of Ministry of \nDefence? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI \nPRAFUL PATEL): (a) to (d) The Draft National Civil Aviation Policy is presently \nunder consideration of the Government. \nConstruction of second International airport at Mumbai \n288. SHRIMATI SUPRIYA SULE: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be  \npleased to state: \n(a) whether Government gave its approval for the construction of a second  \ninternational airport for Mumbai to be con structed at Navi Mumbai with private  \nparticipation; \n(b) whether as per estimate the existing Mumbai airport will be fully \"saturated\" by \n2013 and the Ministry felt \"That a second airport for Mumbai should be constructed; \nand' \n(c) if so, by when final decision in this regard is likely to be taken? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI \nPRAFUL PATEL): (a) to (c) The Government of India have given \"in principle\" \napproval for setting up a new Greenfield airport at Navi Mumbai through Public Priva te \nPartnership (PPP). The existing CSI Airport, Mumbai is likely to get saturated by 2013. \nProcess for the implementation of the Navi Mumbai Airport Project has already \nbegun. \nExpansion plan of Dabolim airport \n289. SHRI SHANTARAM LAXMAN NAIK: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) the status of expansion plan of Dabolim Airport; \n(b) the total amount proposed to be spent; \n(c) the amount granted/sanctioned/alloted to the Airports Authority of India for the \npurpose; and \n(d) the details of the exp ansion plan and time within which each of the items  \nunder the expansion plan are proposed to be completed? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI \nPRAFUL PATEL): (a) to (c) The apron work for providing 3 numbers of additional \naircraft parking bays is in progress. There is a plan for constructon of a new Integrated \nPassenger Terminal at a cost of Rs. 397 crores approximately for which the Detailed \nProject Report (DPR) has been approved by the Airports Authority of India (AAI) \nBoard. \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions     37 \n \n(d) The estimated time for completion of the proposed construction of the new \nIntegrated Passenger Terminal is 24 months from the date of award of the work. \nCity-side development of non-metro airports \n290. MS. PRAMILA BOHIDAR: \nSHRI B.J. PANDA: \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government propose to take up city -side development of non- metro \nairports in the country; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether Orissa Governmen t has written to the Centre for inclusion of \nBhubaneshwar Airport as well in the list, as Bhubaneshwar has emerged as one of the \nfastest growing cities in the country, which is reflected in the rapid growth of its air -\ntraffic; and \n(d) if so, the status thereof , indicating the time -frame within which it would also be \ntaken up for city-side development? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI \nPRAFUL PATEL): (a) to (d) Yes, Sir. The Government has identified 24 non- metro \nairports for city sid e development through Public Private Partnership (PPP), including \nthe Bhubaneshwar airport. In the first phase 5 airports, including the Bhubaneshwar \nairport, have been taken up for city side development.- \nExpansion of runway at Madurai airport \n291. SHRI R. SHUNMUGASUNDARAM: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be \npleased to state: \n(a) what are the reasons for the delay in completion of the work on the expansion of \nrunway and other areas at Madurai Airport; and \n(b) by when the expansion work will be completed? \nTHE M INISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI \nPRAFUL PATEL): (a) The main reasons for the delay in the completion of operational \nworks at Madurai airport were -  unprecedented price rise of construction materials after \naward of work, restricted w ork hours due to operational requirements, heavy rains \nduring tenancy of the contract, extended monsoon/rainy season and restriction by \nGovernment of Tamil Nadu on quarries of sand and stone aggregate. \n(b) The targetted date for completion of the work is 30th September, 2007. \nIncome of Indian \n292. SHRI THENNALA G. BALAKRISHNA PILLAI: Will the Minister of CIVIL  \nAVIATION be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that the air passenger number is increasing every year, at the \nsame time the income of Indian is decreasing during the last five years, period; and \n\n38      Written answers to        [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \n(b) if so, what actions have been taken to enhance the income of Indian? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTER OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHR I \nPRAFUL PATEL): (a) Both, number of passengers and passenger revenue has been \nincreasing during the last five years. \n(b) Indian\" Airlines has taken a number of steps like introduction of multiple fare \nschemes, revision of fares, optimum utilisation of air craft etc. to further enhance its \nincome. \nAirline services to foreign country from Madurai airport \n293. SHRI T.T.V. DHINAKARAN: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be \npleased to state: \n. (a) whether there is any proposal to operate service to foreign count ries from \nMadurai Airport; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI \nPRAFUL PATEL): (a) No, Sir. \n(b) Does not arise. \n(c) In the existing Air Services Agreement between India and other countries, \nMadurai does not figure as a point of call for the designated airlines of foreign \ncountries.  \n Indian carriers are free to operate international services from any point in India \nsubject to compliance of guidelines and commercial viability. How ever, so far, no \nairline operation has proposed to operate international services from Madurai. \nMerchant airports \n†294. SHRI SANJAY RAUT: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) whether Government are going to follow, build and operate pol icy for \nconstruction of new airports in the country now; \n(b) whether it is also being considered to allow the private companies to build and \noperate such airports without any interference of Government under the policy named as \nmerchant airports; and \n(c) the details of the decision taken in this regard by Government? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI \nPRAFUL PATEL): (a) to (c) The extant policy on Airport Infrastructure facilitates \nconstruction of new Greenfield airports in public or private sectors or to \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions     39 \nbe taken up as a joint venture subject to obtaining technical clearance and licence from \nthe competent authority and approval of the Central Government. \nPhoto identity cards for entry at airports \n295. SHRI SANJAY RAUT: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION  be pleased to  \nstate: \n(a) whether Govenment are considering to issue new and comprehensive smart \nphoto identity cards for en try at all major international and domestic airports of the \ncountry keeping in view the increasing threat of terrorism; \n(b) if so, the details thereof and by when such smart cards are likely to be issued; \nand \n(c) if not, what are the reasons therefor? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI \nPRAFUL PATEL): (a) Yes, Sir. \n(b) Bureau of Civil Aviation Security (BCAS) have issued technical specifications \nfor the Smart Cards based airport access control system. While the system software and \ndesign as al so the Central Database would be monitored by the BCAS, the airport \noperators would be procuring, installing and operating the system as per the BCAS \nprescribed specifications. Such an arrangement will facilitate interoperability and \nenabling monitoring of system security by BCAS. \n(c) Does not arise. \nTraining of commercial pilots \n296. DR. E.M. SUDARSANA NATCHIAPPAN: Will the Minister of CIVIL  \nAVIATION be pleased to state: \n(a) whether there are heavy scarcity of pilots in India as getting commercial pilots \nlicence is costly in India; \n(b) if so, whether there is any policy formulated to use the Airports in India to have \nthe accessiblity for training at affordable cost through Government and Private Training \nInstitutes; and \n(c) how many institutes are in either area? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI \nPRAFUL PATEL): (a) Due to the recent boom in civil aviation in the country, a \nscarcity of type rated pilots is being felt in the airline industry. Cost of flying training is \ncommensurate with cost of inputs for such training. \n(b) Government has taken steps to revive non- operational training institutes/ \nschools/academies. Upgradation of infrastructure facilities, including airports, has been \ninitiated. Airports Authority of India (AAI) has been entursted w ith the responsibility of \nupgrading the Birsi aerodrome at Gondia for setting up a new flying \n\n40      Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \ntraining institute. Necessary approval for upgradation of infrastructure at Indira Gandhi \nRashtriya Uran Akademi (IGRUA) has also been conveyed to IGRUA. Government \nalso encourages private parties to set up flying training centres subject to availability of \ninfrastructure. \n(c) There are 42 flying training institutes/clubs/schools in the country of which 26 \nare operational. 15 of these are under Government and 11 are run by private enterprises. \nSetting up of International airport at Greater Noida \n297. SHRI MANOHAR JOSHI: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be pleased  \nto state: \n(a) whether Government propos e to set up an International Airport at Greater Noida \n(U.P.); \n(b) whether the Central Government have received any formal proposal from the \nState Government of Uttar Pradesh to this effect and whether any site has been \nidentified by State Gvoernment; and \n(c) if so, the details thereof and the expected time for coming up of the airport? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI \nPRAFUL PATEL): (a) and (b) A proposal has been received from the Government of \nUttar Pradesh to set an Internati onal airport near Jewar, Gautam Buddha Nagar District \nfor which a site has been identified by the State Government. \n(c) The project is under examination. \nDevelopment of infrastructure for expanding aviation sector \n298. SHRI KARNENDU BHATTACHARJEE: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION \nbe pleased to state:  \n(a) whether Government are aware that aviation sector is on the threshold of \nunprecedented expansion but the infrastructure therefor is unable to keep pace with the \ndemand; \n(b) if so, what steps Government are taki ng to develop necessary infrastructure to \nmeet the demand early along with releasing the funds therefor; and \n(c) The details thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI \nPRAFUL PATEL): (a) Yes, Sir \n(b) and (c) Airports Authority of In dia (AAI) has already initiated various measures \nto develop Airport and Air Navigation Services (ANS) infrastructure to meet the \ngrowing demand of the aviation sector. The capacity of terminals and aprons are being \nincreased. Runways are being strengthened  and their lengths are being increased \nwherever necessary ANS infrastructure is being upgraded and modernized. AAI spent \nRs. 3,535 crores during the Tenth Plan (2002-07) on these \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions    41 \ninfrastructure developments and has programmed to spend Rs. 12,434 crores during the \nEleventh Plan (2007-12). \nAAI has been mandated to expand and modernize Kolkata and Chennai airports \nbesides developing 35 non-metro airports. In addition, the airports at Delhi and Mumba i \nhave been restructured and are being expanded and modernized through Public Private \nPartnerships (PPP). Greenfield airports at Hyderabad and Bangalore are being set up \nthrough PPP. In principle approvals have been given for new Greenfield airports at \nMopa (Goa) and Navi Mumbai. Further, many State Governments are -considering \ndevelopment of several smaller airports. \nAir travel situation in North-East \n299. SHRI KARNENDU BHATTACHARJEE: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that North East is the most neglected sector of the Civil  \nAviation industry so are like ageing aircraft, lack of basic facilities at the airports,  \nnon-adherence to flight timings etc.; \n(b) whether any dedicated steps are contemplated to improve the air travel situation \nin North-East; and \n(c) if so, the details thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI \nPRAFUL PATEL): (a) No, Sir. During the last three years number of flights operated \nto/from and within North East has increased to 287 flights/week from 169 flights/week. \nAircraft which are maintained in airworthiness condition as per the stipulations of \nmanufacturers and approved by the Director General of Civil Aviation are only allowed \nto operate services. The average age of the fleet of most of the airlines is below 10 \nyears. 22 airports are maintained by Airports Authority of India (AAI) in the North \nEast, out of which 11 are operational. The operational airports are not lacking in basic \nfacilities. Generally the scheduled domestic airlines are adhering to their time schedule. \n(b) and (c) Airlines are free to operate anywhere in the country subject to \ncompliance of route dispersal guidelines issued by Government. According to these \nguidelines, all scheduled operators are required to deploy in the North Eastern region, \nJammu and Kashmir, Andaman and Nicobar Islands and Lakshadweep (Category -II \nroutes) at least 10% of their deployed capacity on trunk routes (Category -I routes), \nFurther, at least 10% of the capacity thus required to  be deployed on Category-II routes, \nis required to be deployed for connectivity exclusively within these regions. \nFor development, modernization and upgradation of airports and construction of 3 \nnew Greenfield airports in North -Eastern Region, AAI has proj ected an expenditure of \nRs. 1520 crores for various schemes to be taken up in Eleventh Five Year Plan. \nOut of the eleven non -operational airports in NER, action is in hand to prepare \ndetailed project report to opertaionalize 8 airports viz.  Passighat, Tezu, Along, \n\n42      Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \nDaparizo, Ziro, Kamalpur, Kailashahar and Tura. Feasibility study is being conducted \nfor two new Greenfield airports at Kokrajhar and Tawang. \nModernisation of Kolkata airport \n300. SHRIMATI JAYA BACHCHAN : Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that work on modernisation of Kolkata airport is moving at a \nsnail's pace; \n(b) if so, what are the reasons therefor; and \n(c) the steps being taken by Government to s peed up the tempo of work on this \nairport? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI \nPRAFUL PATEL): (a) No, Sir. \n(b) and (c) Do not arise. \nTraining of pilots \n†301. SHRI SHREEGOPAL VYAS: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be pleased \nto state: \n(a) whether there is a scaling down of pilot training; \n(b) whether private companies train their pilots through their own system, or \nthrough any Centrally provided Government system; \n(c) whether the system is standardized by Government if training is provided by \nthemselves; and \n(d) the details of pilots training system in view of increasing number of flight \nservices? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI \nPI'AFUL PATEL): (a) No, Sir. Flying training in the country is imparted as per the \nsyllab is prescribed by the Directorate General of Civil Aviation. Flying Training \nInstitutes which imparts this training, operate under regulations prescribed by the \nDGCA. \n(b) and (c) After obtaining a licence on completion of the the prescribed training, \npilots are further trained for aircraft type endorsement by private companies through \ntheir own system. Training is conducted by approved examiners as per the training \nmanual. \n(d) Scheduled and non- scheduled operators carry out conversion training of their \npilots at their own training establishments or manufacturer's training facilities. Indian \nAirlines have their own ground simulator training facilities at CTE, Hyderabad. Air \nIndia and Jetlite have got similar facilities at Mumbai. \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions     43 \nDomestic airport in Purnea (Bihar) \n302. SHRI TARIQ ANWAR: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be pleased to  \nstate: \n(a) whether it is a fact that there is an urgent necess ity of a domestic airport in \nPurnea (Bihar); \n(b) whether it is also a fact that during the previous Government, a serious effort \nwas made for establishing it and all  the preliminary works like survey etc. had been \nmade; \n \n(c) whether it is also a fact that the Air Force Headquarters had granted permission \nfor setting up a domestic airport in Purnea; \n(d) if so, why this proposal is shelved; \n(e) whether Government have any plan to establish the same; and \n(f) if not, the reasons therefor? \n. THE MINISTER OF  STATE OF THE MINISTRY O F CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI \nPRAFUL PATEL): (a) Presently no such request is under consideration. \n(b) Yes, Sir. In 2001-02, efforts were made to establish a civil enclave at Indian Air \nForce (IAF) airport, Purnea. \n(c) and (d) AAI officials, during 2001- 02, had selected two sites to initially develop \na temporary terminal infrastructure but No Objection Certificate (NOC) was not issued \nby Air Headquarters/Ministry of Defence due to security and operational implications. \n \n(e) No, Sir. \n(f) Does not arise in view of (c) and (d) above. \nDevelopment of airports in Karnataka \n303. SHRI JANARDHANA POOJARY: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be  \npleased to state: \n(a) whether Karnataka Government has requested the Centre to develop an Airport \neach at Gulbarga, Hassan, Bijapur, Shimoga, Belgaum, etc., where certain private \nairlines have evinced interest in operating their respective flights to these proposed \nairports; and \n(b) if so, the details of each project proposal and Centre's response thereto?  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATI ON (SHRI \nPRAFUL PATEL): (a) and (b) The Government of Karnataka (GoK) has decided to \ndevelop new Greenfield Airports at Gulbarga, Hassan, Bijapur and Shimoga through \nPublic-Private Partnership (PPP) route. The State Government has been advised to get \nfeasibility reports of the proposed airports prepared, obtain required statutory \napprovals/clearances from authorities concerned and submit the proposals to \nGovernment of India for grant of \"In-Principle\" approval. \n\n44      Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \nFor development of Belgaum Airport, the GoK has agreed to provide 370 acres of \nadditional land free of cost to Airports Authority of India (AAI). This airport is being \ndeveloped by AAI. \nNew Civil Aviation Policy \n304. SHRI RAMDAS AGARWAL: Wi ll the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether Government recently asked the Group of Ministers (GoM) to review the \ncurrent guidelines in which airlines.were required to adhere to route dispersal guidelines \n(RDG) to connect the remote areas  in the Jammu and Kashmir, North- Eastern Region, \nAndaman and Nicobar and Lakshadweep; \n(b) if so, the details of recommendations made by GoM; and \n(c) what new measures Government propose to undertake under its New National \nCivil Aviation Policy, including incentive s to encourage Civil Aviation Area in the \ncountry, if amy? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI \nPRAFUL PATEL): (a) and (b) The draft National Civil Aviation Policy is presently \nunder consideration of a Group of Ministers (GoM). How ever, no specific \nissues/proposals have been referred for the consideration of GoM. \n(c) Matter is under consideration of the Government. \nManpower in aviation industry \n305. SHRI VIJAY J. DARDA: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) whether it is a fact that the growth in the aviation industry has not been able  \nto match upto the availability of talent leading to high premium on skilled manpower; \n(b) if so, how we propose to get about 2000- 2500 pilots annually when our exiting \ntraining institutes produce about 300 pilots annually; and \n(c) similarly, how the requirement of about 40,000 cabin crew is going to be met in \nthe coming five years? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI \nPRAFUL PATEL): (a) In view of the recent boom in civil aviation in the country, \nshortage of skilled manpower is being felt in the industry. \n(b) The Government has taken various steps to reduce the gap between demand and \nsupply of skilled pilots. These include conditionally increasing the age -limit to 65 years \nfor exercising the privileges of their licences for commercial transport operations under \nprescribed conditions for pilots, upgradation and modernisation of training \ninfrastructure of Indira Gandhi Rashtriya Uran Akaderni (IGRUA) to enhance its training \ncapacity, setting up of a world class flying training institute at Gondia, Maharashtra and \nassistance to flying clubs by allocating trainer aircraft through \n\nwritten answers to [14 AUG, 2007] Unstarred questions     45 \nDirectorate General of Civil A viation (DGCA)/Aero Club of India (ACI). Besides, \nDGCA has approved a total of 26 flying training institutes (including 1GRUA) in the \ncountry for imparting Commercial Pilot Licence (CPL) training. \n(c) Recruitment, training etc. of cabin crew is done by the  respective airlines as per \ntheir requirement. \nConstruction of airport in Sikkim \n306. SHRI O.T. LEPCHA: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be pleased to  \nstate:  \n(a) whether there is any project for the construction of airport in Sikkim; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) the details of steps taken so far by Government to provide air -services to and \nfrom Gangtok, Sikkim? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI \nPRAFUL PATEL): (a) Yes, Sir. \n(b) Detailed project report has been prepared by Airports Authority of India (AAI) \nand submitted to State Government of Sikkim for construction of a Greenfield airport at \nPakyong, 33 km South West of Gangtok. The estimated cost of the project is Rs. 320 \ncrores. \n(c) Government have laid down Route Dispersal Gufdelines  with a view to achieve \nbetter regulation of air transport services taking into account the need for air transport \nservices of different regions of the country. Subject to these guidelines, airlines are free \nto provide air services to any place depending u pon the traffic demand and commercial \nviability. \nAccording to these guidelines, all scheduled operators are required to deploy in the \nNorth Eastern region, Jammu and Kashmir, Andaman and Nicobar Islands and \nLakshadweep (Category -II routes) at least 10% of their deployed capacity on trunk \nroutes (Category -I routes). Further, at least 10% of the capacity thus required to be \ndeployed on Category -II routes, is required to be deployed for connectivity exclusively \nwithin these regions. \nGangtok, Sikkim is being ai r-serviced from Bagdogra Airport (West Bengal) which \nis at a distance of about 125 km. Pawan Hans is providing daily helicopter services \nbetween Bagdogra and Gangtok. \nShortage of air traffic controllers \n307. SHRI B.J. PANDA: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIA TION be pleased to  \nstate: \n(a) whether India is facing shortage of Air Traffic Controllers (ATC); \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) what is the action plan of Government to bridge this gap? \n\n46     Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \nTHE MINISTER  OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI \nPRAFUL PATEL): (a) Due to sudden and unprecedented growth of air traffic, there is a \nrequirement of additional trained air traffic controllers to man the newly eastablished \nATC work stations to handle the increased growth. \n(b) and (c) During the last two and half years, 371 Junior Executives (ATC) have \nbeen inducted in Airports Authority of India (AA1). Action for filling up another 148 \nposts of JE (ATC) is in progress. AAI is also utilizing the services of retired ATCOs, \nwherever possible. It has been programmed to recruit 100 ATCOs every six months for \na period of two years to meet the additional requirement. \nModernisation of Mumbai airport \n308. SHRIMATI SUPRIYA SULE: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be pleased \nto state: \n(a) whether the Ministry has pointed out that there have been some slippages in  \ntime in the modernisation work of the Mumbai airport; \n(b) whether this problem has been referred to the GoM because a wider  \nconsultation was required on the matter; and \n(c) if so, by what time final decision in this regard is likely to be taken? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI \nPRAFUL PATEL): (a) An Operation, Management and Development Agreement \n(OMDA) has been signed between the p rivate operator M/s. Mumbai International \nAirport Private Limited (MIAL) and Airports Authority of India (AAI). Clause (8) of \nOMDA clearly stipulates various development works that are to be carried out by \nMumbai International Airport Limited for moderniza tion and development of the airport \nin a time bound manner. There is a Committee headed by Secretary (Civil Aviation) in \nthe Ministry which oversees the implementation of OMDA. \n(b) No, Sir. \n(c) Does not arise. \nStrike by ground staff of Indian \n209. SHRI RAJKUMAR DHOOT: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be pleased \nto state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that about 13,000 ground staff of Indian went on flash strike \non 11th June. 2007 causing disruption of air services and lot of inconveniences to \npassengers: \n(b) if  o. the details thereof and the reasons therefor; \n(c) the reasons for not settling the long pending issues of ground staff that led to \nstrike: and \n(d) the steps taken to ensure smooth operation of Indian and proper service to \npassengers? \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions     47 \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI \nPRAFUL PATEL): (a) and (b) Yes, Sir. Air Corporations Employees Union (ACEU) \nrepresenting around 12,200 employees, called for strike w.e.f. night shift of 12.6.2007 all \nover India. ACEU had been demanding revision of wages and allowances w.e.f. 1.1.1997 \nand better career progression. The negotiations in this regard were initiated way back in \nFebruary, 2007 and when no satisfactory agreement was reached and the ACEU  adopted \nan agitational approach, the matter was referred to the Chief Labour Commissioner \n(Central) for conciliation. Even as the conciliation proceedings were underway with the \nChief Labour Commissioner (Central), the ACEU suddenly called for a flash str ike. The \nstrike was called off from the afternoon of 14th June, 2007 after tripartite discussions \ninvolving the Ministry of Civil Aviation. Management of Indian Airlines and the office \nbearers of ACEU. \n(c) Indian Airlines being a Public Sector Enterprise, follows the guidelines issued \nby the Department of Public Enterprises on wages, allowances and other service \nconditions for its employees. The demands of ACEU with regard to wages and \nallowances were not in confirmity with these guidelines and Indian Airlines was not in \na position to bear the high financial implications of these demands. \n(d) Contingency plan was put into operation to deal with the situation and all efforts \nwere made to ensure smooth operations of flights by deputing additional officers as \nwell as e ngaging casuals and contract workers. To minimize/avoid harassment to \ncustomers, proper and timely dissemination of information regarding flight position was \nensured through Indian Airlines' call centres. \nIncidents of crash of helicopters \n310. SHRI G1REESH KUMAR SANGHI: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that incidents of crash of helicopters in different parts of the \ncountry have increased during the last three years; \n(b) if so, how many cases of minor and major accid ents of helicopters have been \nreported during the last three years; and \n(c) what are the results of the enquiries conducted by the DGCA and steps taken to \nprevent such mishaps? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI \nPRAFUL PATEL): (a) an d (b) During the last three years 7 accidents and 5 serious \nincidents to helicopters have been reported. 3 accidents and I serious incident has been \nreported in 2007 (till  date), 1 accident and 1 serious incident were reported in 2006 and \n3 accidents and 3 serious incidents were reported in 2005. \n(c) All accidents/incidents are investigated as per the procedure contained in the \nAircraft Rules and necessary corrective measures are taken based on the \nrecommendations made in the investigation report to avoid recurrence of such  haps. \n\n48      Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \nServices by Indian and private players \n311. SHRI M.V. MYSURA REDDY: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be  \npleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that Indian and its  subsidiary Alliance Air are having a fleet  \nof 51 own and 23 leased aircrafts: \n(b) if so, the details regarding the fleet strength of Jet Airways, Sahara, Air Deccan \nand Kingfisher; \n(c) whether it is also a fact' that Indian and its subsidiary are under -utilizing its \naircrafts in operation of services when compared to the other competitors and if so, the \nreasons therefor; and \n(d) the details of operations to various destinations by Indian, Jet Airways, Sahara, \nAir Deccan and Kingfisher airlines? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE O F THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI \nPRAFUL PATEL): (a) Indian Airlines including its subsidiary Alliance Air has a fleet \nof 42 own and 34 leased aircraft. \n(b) The fleet strength of Jet Airways, Jet Lite, Air Deccan and Kingfisher airlines \nare 64, 24, 43 and 30 respectively. \n(c) No, Sir. Aircraft Utilization of Indian Airlines of A320 aircraft, which constitute \na major part of the fleet of Indian Airlines, compares favourably with that of its \ncompetitors. \n(d) At present Indian Airlines and its subsidiary Alliance  Air are operating air \nservices to/from 54 domestic station and 19 international stations. The Jet Airways, Jet \nLite, Air Deccan and Kingfisher Airlines are operating air services to 44, 27,63 and 31 \ndomestic stations respectively. \nCivil Aviation Policy \n312. SHRIMATI SHOBHANA BHARTIA: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether the Ministry, has referred a Civil Aviation Policy to the Group of \nMinisters after Cabinet Ministers raised questions about certain proposals mooted in the \npolicy including allowing more domestic airlines abroad, creation of merchant airports \nand plans to corporatise the air  traffic control system; \n(b) if so, whether the GoM have considered all these aspects and have also given its \nrecommendations; \n(c) if so, what are the main details of the same; and \n(d) by when the aviation policy is likely to be announced? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI \nPRAFUL PATEL): (a) to (d) The National Civil Aviation Policy is presently under \nconsideration of a Group of Ministers (GoM). However, no specific issues/ proposals \nhave been referred for the consideration of GoM. \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG, 2007] Unstarred questions    49 \nAgreement with Government of Kuwait on operation of airlines \n313. SHRI SHANTARAM LAXMAN NAIK: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government have executed an agreement with Government of Kuwait \nwith respect to the flights to be operated by the airlines of the two countries; \n(b) whether there was any dispute on the matter, recently; \n(c) whether Government of Kuwait had warned that they would not allow Indian \nAirlines Companies to land in Kuwait from July, 1st; \n \n(d) whether the dispute has been resolved; and \n(e) if so, the details of the new arrangements agreed upon? \nTHE MINISTER OF S TATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI \nPRAFUL PATEL): (a) to (d) Yes, Sir. \n(e) The new agreement reached between India and Kuwait during the bilateral air \nservices talks held on 28 -30 June, 2007, inter -alia, provides that the capacity \nentitlements fo r each side shall be enhanced from 8320 to 12000 seats/week. The \nKuwaiti designated airlines have been granted Hyderabad and Kolkata as additional \npoints of call with immediate effect and Bangalore with effect from Winter 2008 -09 \napart from the existing Mumbai, Delhi, Trivandrum, Chennai and Ahmedabad. \nCivil aviation projects \n†314. SHRI JAI PARKASH AGGARWAL: Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) the details of civil aviation projects started during the previous Five Year Plan;  \n(b) the progress made till date under these projects; \n(c) whether these projects are running in time; \n \n(d) if so, the amount spent on these projects; and \n(e) the amount sanctioned for these projects? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION (SHRI \nPRAFUL PATEL): (a) to  (e) Information is being collected and will be laid on the \nTable of the House. \nUnfair trade practices by IIPM \n315. SHRI DHARAM PAL SABHARWAL: Will the Minister of CORPORATE \nAFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government have received complaints against I ndian Institute of \nPlanning and Management (IIPM), New Delhi for providing free laptops and foreign \ntrips to students seeking MBA/BBA courses; \n(b) if so, the details in this regard; \n†Original noiice of the question was received in Hindi \n\n50      Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \n(c) whether offering such freebies to poor students for seeking admission in \nunapproved courses amounts to unfair trade practices under the MRTP Act; and \n(d) if so, what action Government have taken against the IIPM? \nTHE MINI STER OF CORPORATE AFFAIRS (SHRI PREM CHAND GUPTA): \n(a) to (d) Yes, Sir. Based on the complaints received and suo- motu on the basis of the \ninformation given on the website of Indian Institute of Planning and Management \n(IIPM) offering free international stu dy tours and free laptops to students of MBA/ \nBBA, the Monopolies and Restrictive Trade Practices (MRTP) Commission has \ninitiated action against the IIPM. The matter has been referred to the Director General \n(Investigation and Registration) to investigate and file the Preliminary Investigation \nReport. The MRTP Commission can take cognizance of such advertisements under the \nprovisions of MRTP Act. \nAll India Council for Technical Education (AICTE) has issued a show cause notice \nto the Indian Institute of Plan ning and Management, New Delhi and has placed the \nname of the Institution in the list of institutions which are running technical courses \nwithout their approval, on their web-site. \nIllegal trafficking of antiquities \n316. SHRI ABU ASIM AZM1: Will the Minister of CULTURE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government are fully aware of the increasing illegal trafficking in \nantiquities in the country; \n(b) if so, the number of cases reported during the last two years, year-wise; \n(c) the corrective steps being taken to combat trafficking in antiquities; \n(d) whether the National Mission in Monuments and Antiquities will be able to \ncombat the trafficking in antiquities; and \n(e) if so, to what extent? \nTHE MINISTER OF CULTURE (SHRIMATI AMBIKA SONI): (a) and (b) The \navailable- data does not indicate increasing illegal trafficking in antiquities. The number \nof cases of antiquity thefts reported during the last two years is given in the enclosed \nStatement (See below). \n(c) Under the Antiquities and Art Treasures Act, 1972, provisions exist for \nregulating the trade in antiquities and for registering specific categories of antiquities. \nIndia  is also a signatory to the 1970 UNESCO Convention on the Means of \nProhibiting and Preventing the Illicit  Trafficking in Cultural Property. \nThe provision for s ecurity at museums and protected monuments has been \naugmented by deploying State Police, Private Security Guards and also CISF in selected \nplaces. Strict vigil is also maintained by the Customs Department at all airports and \nseaports and other law enforcing agencies like the CBI, DRI and the State Police. \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions     51 \n(d) and (e) The National Mission on Monuments and Antiquities would document \nthe antiquities and prepare a national database, which would help  in establishing \nprovenance in the retrieval of smuggled antiquities. The Mission would also promote \npublic awareness, concern and participation in safeguarding of antiquarian wealth. \nStatement \nThe number of thefts of antiquities during the last two years  \nSI, \nNo. \nName  of \nState \nName  of \nMonument/Site \nand the  District \nDescription \nof the \nObjects \nDate  of \ntheft \nStatus of \nthe Case \nRemarks \n1 2 3 4 5 6 7 \n   Year 2005    \n1. Rajasthan Ancient site \nNagar,  Distt. \nTonk \n7 \nSculptures \n12-1-2005  F.I.R. \nlodged. \n \n2. Chhattisgarh Centrally \nProtected  Site \nSirpur.  Distt. \nMahasamund \nStone \nimage of \nHariti \n23/24-6-05 F.I.R. \nlodged \nRecovered \n3. Madhya \nPradesh \nSculpture shed, \nKaritalai.  Distt. \nKatni \n9 \nSandstone \nsculptures \n17-8-05 F.I.R. \nlodged \n \n4. Chhattisgarh Laxman \nTemple.  Sirpur, \nDistt. \nMahasamund \nStone image \nof Ananta-\nShesh \nYear  2006 \n13/14 -10-\n05 \nF.I.R. \nlodged \nRecovered \n1. Uttarakhand Cave temple \nPatal- \nBhubneswar \nTehsil  Berinag \nDistt. \nPithoragarh \nTwo small \nsize stone \nimages of \nGanesha \n(Under \nworship) \nnot so old. \n2/3-4-2006 F.I.R. \nlodged \n \n2. Uttarakhand Temple \npremises  of \nthe Chandpur  \nFort, Distt.   \nChamoli \n1   \nCarved-\nwooden \nhead  of \nKali \n18/19 -04-\n06 \nFIR. \nlodged \n \n3. Bihar Patna  State \nGovt.   \nMuseum \n18  Bronze \nsculptures. \n26-09-2006 FIR. \nlodged \n17  have \nbeen \nrecovered \nand  1  is \nstill  to be \nrecovered \n\n52      Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions  \n1 2 3 4 5 6     7 \n4. Andhra Shiv Theft  of 6-10-2006 F.I.R.  \n Pradesh Kodandarama \nSwamy Temple, \nVomtimitta,  \nDistt Kadapa, \nAndhra-Pradesh \ncentral bud \nportion  of \nthe  inverted \nlotus carved \non  the \nKalyanama \nnda-pa \nceiling  \n lodged  \n5. Rajasthan Archaeological \nMuseum, \nKalibangan, \nDistt. \nHanumangarh \nWheel  \nand cart \nframe \n21-01-\n2006  \nF.I..R \nlodged \n \nMulti-purpose cultural complex \n317. SHRIMATI HEMA MALINI: Will the Minister of CULTURE be pleased to  \nstate: \n(a) whether there is any proposal to set up multi -purpose cultural complexes in the \ncountry; \n(b) if so, what are these places in order to promote culture itself, State-wise; and \n(c) the details thereof and what cost? \nTHE MINISTER OF CULTURE (SHRIMATI AMBIKA SONI): (a) Multipurpose \nCultural Complex Scheme which was in existence till  31-3-2007 has been dropped by \nPlanning Commission from the current financial year onwards. \n(b) and (c) Does not arise. \nUpgradation of list by World Heritage Committee \n318. SHRIMATI JAYA BACHCHAN: Will the Minister of CULTURE be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) whether it is a fact that UNESCO's World Heritage Committee has recently \nupdated list of properties of outstanding universal value that are a part of planet's \ncultural and natural heritage; \n(b) if so, what are-the details of Indian sites added to this list; and \n(c) what are the names of Indian sites that already find place on this list? \nTHE MINISTER CULTURE (SHRIMATI AMBIKA SONI): (a) and (b): The \nUNESCO World Heritage Committee during its 31st session held at Christchurch (New \nZealand) from 23rd June to 2nd July, 2007, has inscribed Red Fort Complex, Delhi on \nthe World Heritage list due to its outstanding universal value and because it is a \nbeautiful example of Mughal architecture. \nThe list of the Indian sites already inscribed on the World Heritage list is given in \nthe enclosed Statement {See below). \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions     53 \nStatement \nWorld Heritages sites in India \nCULTURAL SITES  \nSI. No. Name of site State \n1. Ajanta Caves (1983) Maharashtra \n2. Ellora Caves (1983) Maharashtra \n3. Agra Fort (1983) Uttar Pradesh \n4. Taj Mahal (1983) Uttar Pradesh \n5.1 Sun Ternple, Konarak (1984) Orissa \n6. Group of monuments at \nMahabalipuram (1984) \nTamil Nadu \n7. Churches & Convents of Goa (1986)- Goa \n8. Group of temples, Khajuraho (1986) Madhya Pradesh \n9. Group of monuments at Hampi (1986) Karnataka \n10. Group of monuments, Fatehpur Sikri (1986) Uttar Pradesh \n11. Group of temples, Pattadakal (1987) Karnataka \n12. Elephanta Caves (1987) Maharashtra \n13. Great Living Chola temples at Thanjavur, \nGangaikondacholapuram and Darasuram (1987 \nTamil Nadu \n and 2004) • \n14. Buddhist monuments at Sanchi (1989) Madhya Pradesh \n15. Humayun Tomb, Delhi (1993) Delhi \n16. Qutab Minar complex, Delhi Delhi \n17. Mountain Railway of India (1999, 2005) West Bengal and \n  Tamil Nadu \n18. Mahabodhi Temple, Bodhgaya (2002) Bihar \n19. Prehistoric Rock shelters of Bhimbetka (2003) Madhya Pradesh \n20. Champaner-Pavagarh Archaeological Park (2004) Gujarat \n21. Chhatrapati Shivaji Terminus (formerly Victoria \nTerminus) (2004) \nMaharashtra \n22. Red Fort complex (2007) Delhi \n\n54      Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \nNATURAL SITES  \nSI. No. Name of site State \n1. Kaziranga National Park (1985) Assam \n2. Manas Wild Life Sanctuary (1985) Assam \n3. Keoladeo National Park (1985) Rajasthan \n4. Sunderban National Park (1987) West Bengal \n5. Nanda Devi and Valley of Flowers National \nParks (1988, 2005) \nUttaranchal \nDeclaration of Ram Setu as national monument \n†319. SHRI SHREEGOPAL WAS: Will the Minister of CULTURE be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) whether Government are aware that the Madras High Court has directed to \ndeclare Ram Setu (between India and Sri Lanka) as national mo nument under the \nNational Monument and Archaeological Place Law, 1958; \n(b) whether its existence is the oldest in comparison to the many declared \nmonuments; \n(c) whether such sentiments alongwith logic have also been expressed by many \narchaeologists, legal experts, Members of Parliament, social and religious organizations; \nand \n(d) what is the difficulty in declaring it national monument by keeping the above \nthings in mind, if there is no difficulty or objection in it then what steps have been taken \nto make declaration by the Ministry? \nTHE MINISTER CULTURE (SHRIMATI AMBIKA SONI): (a) No such directions \nhave been received from the Madras High Court. \n(b) No archaeological study has been made in respect of Ram Setu by the \nArchaeological Survey of India. \n(c) Such sentiments have been  expressed by way of representations to the \nGovernment and petitions before Courts. \n(d) As per the scientific evidence available so far, Ram Setu does not fulfil the \ncriteria to be declared as a monument of national importance under the provisions of the \nAncient Monuments and Archaeological Sites and Remains Act, 1958. \nProtection of cultural heritage sites in Uttarakhand \n†320. SHRI HARISH RAWAT: Will the Minister of CULTURE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether work is under process to declare some villages of the S tate of \nUttarakhand alongwith Dwarahat and Adibadri as 'cultural village' and whether the work \nis going on the special plan for the protection of the cultural heritages situated in these \nvillages; and \n†Original noiice of the question was received in Hindi \n\nWritten answers to    [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions     55 \n(b) if so, the details thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF CULTURE (SHRIMATI AMBIKA SON1): (a) and (b) No, Sir. \nThere is no provision in the Ancient Monuments and Archaeological Sites and Remains \nAct, 1958 to declare a village as 'cultural village'. However, there are 42 \nmonuments/sites declared as of national importance under the jurisdiction of \nArchaeological Survey of India in the State of Uttarakhand, of which eight monuments \nare in Dwarahat and one monument is in Adibadri. The following expenditure has been \nincurred by way of structural repairs/maintenance on these monuments/sites during the \nlast three years: \n2004-05 Rs.43,87,774/- \n2005-06 Rs.38,20.346/- \n2006-07 Rs.31,71,750/- \nTardy flow of credit in Minority dominated districts \n321. MS. SUSH1LATIRIYA: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that there has been tardy progress in stepping up flow of \ncredit in the minority dominated districts; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) the steps taken in this regard? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) to (c) Several steps have been taken by the Reserve Bank of \nIndia (RBI) in the recent past to step up credit flow to minorities. In its Circ ular dated \n1.09.2006, RBI had instructed to all the Scheduled Commercial Banks to ensure that the \nminority communities receive a fair and equitable portion of credit within the overall \ntargets of Priority Sector and sub -targets of 10% earmarked for Weaker Section. The \nMaster Circular on Priority Sector lending was amended on 18.05.2007 to Include \nminority communities under \"Weaker Section\". \nIn their consolidated Master Circular dated 5.07.2007 on lending to minorities, the \nRBI has advised the banks to take care to ensure that minority communities secure in a \nfair and adequate measure, the benefits flowing from various Government sponsored \nspecial programmes and create a separate cell in each bank to ensure smooth flow of \ncredit to minority communities. \nIn its circular dated 16.07.2007, RBI has issued a consolidated list of 121 minority \nconcentration districts to Scheduled Commercial Banks for specially monitoring credit \nflow to minorities. \nEasing of norms for FDI in agro-processing \n322. SHRI RAJKUMAR DHOOT: W ill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to  \nstate: \n(a) whether it is a fact that Government have decided to further ease norms for FDI \nin agro-processing and agro-infrastructure; \n(b) if so. the details thereof; and \n\n56     Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \n(c) the States and UTs likely to derive benefits from such a decision? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) and (b) In accordance with the existing FDI policy, FDI up to \n100 per cent is permitted under t he automatic route in Floriculture, Horticulture, \nDevelopment of seeds, Animal Husbandry, Pisciculture, Acquaculture and Cultivation \nof Vegetable and Mushrooms under controlled conditions and services related to Agro \nand allied sectors. FDI upto 100 per ce nt, with prior Government approval, is permitted \nin Tea plantation subject to the conditions of divestment of 26 per cent equity of the \ncompany in favour of an Indian partner/Indian public within a period of five years and \nwith the prior approval of the St ate Government concerned in case of future land use \nchange. FDI upto 100 per cent under the automatic route is also allowed in the Food \nProcessing Industry and for infrastructure facilities like cold storage, transportation etc. \nThere is no proposal to fur ther ease the FDI norms in agro processing and agro \ninfrastructure. \n(c) Does not arise. \nStable interest rate for educational loans \n†323. SHRI AJAY SINGH CHAUTALA: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased \nto state: \n(a) whether Government will keep interest rate on educational loans stable for the \nstudents; \n(b) if so, interest rate on educational loans; \n(c) whether Government are going to fix four per cent interest rate on educational \nloans in near future; and \n(d) if so, by when? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINAN CE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) to (d) The interest rates have been deregulated and banks are \nfree to fix interest rates themselves with the approval of their Boards. Banks have been \nadvised to fix their Benchmark Prime Lending Rate (BPLR) after taking into account \ncost of funds, transaction cost, risk factor, margin and other overhead expenses. As per \nthe revised Model Education Loan Scheme, 2004, the banks are to charge interest on \neducation loan upto Rs.4 lakh at BPLR and above Rs. 4 lakh at BPLR + 1 per cent. \nSimple interest is to be charged during the repayment holiday/ moratorium period. \nInvestor's awareness programme for non-metro cities \n324. SHRI KALRAJ MISHRA: \nSHRI SANTOSH BAGRODIA: \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that the SEBI proposes to commence investor's awareness \nprogramme for the non-metro cities; \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions     57 \n(b) if so, the details of the programme and partner agencies; and \n(c) whether Government propose to introduce such programme at the school level \nthrough new NCERT books? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) Yes, Sir. \n(b) The programme envisages partnership wit h the Exchanges, National Institute of \nSecurities Markets, Investor Associations and State Governments. \n(c) There is no proposal for introducing investors awareness programme at the \nschool level through new NCERT textbooks. However, Government consider a cours e \non personal finance at school level useful. \nHiring of PAN cards by share brokers \n†325. SHRI MOTILAL VORA: \nSHRI SATYAVRAT CHATURVEDI: \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that share brokers are hiring PAN cards from the P AN card \nholders and thus, money of terrorist organizations also is being invested in the share \nmarket; \n(b) whether it is also a fact that a scandal of fake Demat accounts was detected in \nIndian market during the year 2005; \n(c) if so, the number of persons found gu ilty in it so far and the action taken against \nthem; and \n(d) the steps taken by Government to check the practice of getting PAN cards on \nhire? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) The Securities and Exchange Board of India (SEBI) has \ninformed that verification by the stock exchanges has not led to any such finding. \n(b) SEBI has noticed certain irregularities in a few Initial  Public Offerings made \nduring 2003-05. \n(c) SEBI has identified 24 key operators, 82 financiers, 12 depos itory participants \nand 2 depositories, who were associated with the irregularities and issued a variety of \ndirections, including disgorgement, monetary penalty, debarment, etc. against them.  \n(d) Does not arise in view of (a) above. \n†Original notice of the question was received  in Hindi \n\n58      Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \nFall in wholesale price index \n†326. SHRI RAM JETHMALANI: \nDR. MURLI MANOHAR JOSHI: \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that redu ction has been assessed in wholesale price index in \nthe country during the last months; \n\"(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether it is also a fact that the prices of essential consumer commodities have \nrisen 5 per cent to 10' per cent during above mentioned time when wholesale price index \nwas assessed to be low; \n(d) if so, the details thereof; and \n(e) what is Government's reaction towards the impact of this price hike of consumer \ncommodities on common people? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI P AWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) to (d) Monthly WPI of all commodities and 30 essential \ncommodities indicated in the table below reveals that while the overall WPI has \nincreased, the WPI for 30 essential commodities has generally been stable. \nTable: Monthly WPI of all commodities and 30 Essential Commodities (%)  \nMonth Wholesale price index \n All comm. Ess. comm. \nJanuary, 2007 208.8 202.1 \nFebruary, 2007 208.9 202.2 \nMarch, 2007 209.8 201.0 \nApril, 2007 211.5 201.2 \nMay, 2007 212.3 201.0 \nJune, 2007* 212.1 200.8 \nJuly, 2007* 213.0 202.8 \n*  Provisional \n(e) Containment of inflation remains high on the agenda of the Government. Anti-inflationary \npolicies of the Government include strict fiscal and monetary discipline, rationalization of \nexcise and import duties of essential items so that there is no undue burden on the poor, effective \nsupply-demand management of essential commodities through libera] tariff and trade policies, \nand strengthening the public distribution system. \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions     59 \nPrinting of polymer based currency notes \n327. SHRI AMAR SINGH: \nSHRI ABU ASIM AZMI: \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government propose to start a pilot project to print currency notes made \nto polymers instead of paper; \n(b) if so, the details thereof and by when it is likely to be printed; and \n(c) what would be the cost of polymer currency in comparison with paper currency?  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FIN ANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) to (c) Exploring scientific alternatives for increasing the life of \nIndian Bank Notes, is a continuous process undertaken by the RBI and the Government. \nAs part of this exercise, RBI is at present evaluating various opti ons for increasing the \nlife of currency notes, including printing on polymer substrate. The cost and life of \npolymer currency in comparison with paper currency will be available on completion of \nthe above said exercise. \nInflation rate \n†328. SHRI JANESHWAR MISHRA: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) whether it is a fact that despite increasing growth rate inflation could not be \ncurbed; \n(b) if so, whether Government have taken any action to check inflation; and \n(c) if so, the details thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) to (c) Sir, the year -on-year inflation based on wholesale price \nindex has declined during the recent months of current financial year, 2007 -08 as \nindicated in the Table below: \nTable: Monthly WP1 based inflation (%) during recent months of 2007- 08  \nMonth/year WPI based monthly inflation (%) \nApril, 2007 6.28 \nMay, 2007 5.46 \nJune, 2007* 4.43 \nJuly, 2007* 4.41 \n*   Provisional  \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\n60      Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \nContainment of inflation remains high on the agenda of the Government. Anti -\ninflationary policies of the Government include strict fiscal and monetary discipline, \nrationalization of excise and import duties of essential items so that there is no undue \nburden on the poor, effective supply -demand management of essential commodities \nthrough liberal tariff and trade policies, and strengthening the public distribution \nsystem. \nDonation racket in medical/engineering colleges \n329. DR. E.M. SUDARSANA NATCHIAPPAN: Will the Minister of FINANCE be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether his Ministry is aware of donation racket throughout the country in \nprivate engineering and medical colleges in the name of donation for studies; and \n(b) if so, how much revenue is generated through them by Taxation either in State \nor in Union Budget? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI S.S. \nPALAN1MANTCKAM): (a) and (b) Inquires  carried out by the Income Tax \nDepartment indicate that donations have been paid for securing admission to private \nMedical/Engineering colleges. The income embedded in such transactions is charged to \ntax in accordance with provisions of the Income Tax Act, 1961. Data in respect of taxes \npaid/payable in respect of such donations is not centrally maintained. \nBacklog vacancies for SCs/STs \n330. SHRI PRAVEEN RASHTRAPAL: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased  \nto state: \n(a) the number of backlog vacancies for SC and ST as on 1st April, 2004 in the LIC \nand four General  Insurance Corporations (Oriental, United etc.) all over the country, \nregion-wise; \n(b) whether special recruitments were done to fill up these vacancies; \n(c) it so, the details thereof, region-wise; and \n(d) what is the position/status of backlog vacancies as on 31st March, 2007  \nregion-wise? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) to (d) The Information is being collected and will be laid on \nthe Table of the House. \nSearch operations by Income Tax Department \n331. SHRI PRAVEEN RASHTRAPAL: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased  \nto state: \n(a) the number of search onerations carried out by the intelligence wings of the \nIncome Tax Department during the period from 1st April, 2006 to 28th February, 2007 \nalow with  details    for the  Slates  of   West  Benga    Tamil   Nadu,  Uttar Pradesh, \nMaharastra and Gujarat: \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions     61 \n(b) whether it is a fact that the actions of searches are always taken in the closing \nmonth of financial year; and \n(c) if so, the details of cash, ornaments and jewellery seized and actual gain to the \nrevenue on accounts of payment of tax-advance tax subsequent to the search? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI S.S. \nPALANIMANICKAM): (a) The total number of search operations carried out by the \nIntelligence Wings of the IT Department during the period from 1st April, 2006 to 28th \nFebruary, 2007 are 453 group of cases. The search operations carried out in the States \nof West Bengal, Tamil Nadu, Uttar P radesh, Maharashtra and Gujarat are 18,53,10, 126 \nand 47 group of cases respectively. \n(b) No, Sir. The observation that the actions of searches are always conducted in the \nclosing month of the Financial Year is not correct as searches are conducted throughout \nthe year. \n(c) Not applicable in view of reply at (b) above. \nGrowth rate of economy \n332. SHRIMATI N.P. DURGA: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that India has become a US $1.1 trillion  economy and \nthereby become one of the 11 powerhouses that enjoy this distinction; \n(b) how could it be possible within a span of 5 years taking into consideration that \nIndia's GDP in 2000-01 was below US $ 500 billion; and \n(c) to what extent the strong rupee helped India to become a trillion economy?  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) to (c) As per the Revised Estimates released by the Central \nStatistical Organisation (CSO) the Gross Domestic Product of India at current market \nprices for the year, 2006 -07 was Rs. 41,25,725 crore. This is equivalent to US Dollar \n912 billion  at an average exchange rate of Rs. 45.25 per US Dollar for 2006 -07. In \n2000-01 the GDP at current market prices was Rs. 21,02,376 crore equivalent to US \nDollar 460 billion  at the average exch ange rate of Rs. 45.68 per US Dollar in 2000-\n01. The GDP at current market prices in US dollar terms grew at a compound annual \nrate of 12.1 per cent during this period. \nAppreciation of Rupee \n333. SHRI SYED AZEEZ PASHA: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleas ed to.  \nstate: \n(a) whether it is a fact that the rupee has appreciated by over 6 per cent during the \nyear, 2006-07 which has hit the growth of exports and economy; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether a Government panel was set up to discuss the impact of the appreciation \nof Indian rupee on the economy and suggest measures to tackle it; and \n\n62     Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \n(d) if so, the details of its findings and measures taken to tackle the problem? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) and (b) Rupee has been appreciating on monthly basis against \nthe US dollar from September, 2006 onwards. Over the full year, 2006- 07, the rupee \ndepreciated by 2.2 percent against the US dollar, while GDP grew by 9.4 percent and \nexports by 21 percent in US dollar terms. \n(c) The rupee appreciation has been higher in the current financial year at 8.9 per \ncent in July, 2007 over March 2007, while exports grew by 18.1 per cent in April -June, \n2007 in US dollar terms. In order to assess the impact of rupee appreciation on exports \nand to suggest measures to maintain India's export competitiveness, Ministry of \nCommerce and Industry constituted a committee. \n(d) As per the findings of the Committee, while the importers and borrowe rs in \nforeign currency are not entirely unhappy with the appreciation of the rupee vis -a-vis the \nUS dollar, generally, the exporters have seen their profit margins erode as a result of the \nappreciation of the rupee. The committee has made a series of recom mendations for \nimmediate relief to the exporters. \nThe Government has announced a relief package to exporters comprising enhanced \nDuty Entitlement Passbook (DEPB) rates, reduction in Export Credit Guarantee \nCorporation (ECGC) premium, release of Rs.600 crore for clearing all arrears of terminal \nexcise duty and Central Sales tax reimbursement, enhancement of duty drawback rates \nand reduction in interest rates on pre and post shipment credit. \nSpecial Oil Bonds to Oil Companies \n334. SHRI RAHUL BAJAJ: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that the figure of petroleum subsidy for the year, 2006 -2007 \nof Rs 2785.43 crores given in the Expenditure Budget, 2007 -08 include the amount of \nspecial oil bonds issued to oil marketing companies. \n(b) if not, then what is the value of such bonds issued during 2006-07; \n(c) For what other expenditure during, 2006- 07 have similar special bonds been \nissued and of what value; and \n(d) whether these not give an inaccurate picture of the fiscal deficit, if these are  \ntreated as part of the fiscal deficit what would the revised figures of fiscal deficit for the \nyear, 2006-07 be and would it exceed the target? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) No, Sir. This amount does not i nclude the Special Securities \nissued to Oil Marketing Companies. \n(b) The amount of Special Securities issued to Oil Companies during, 2006- 07 is Rs. \n24,121 crore as against the provision of Rs. 24,150 crore approved through the \nSupplementary Demands for Grants voted by Parliament. \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG, 2007] Unstarred questions     63 \n(c) During, 2006- 07, Special Securities amounting to Rs. 16,200 crore have been \nissued to Food Corporation of India in settlement of outstanding dues on account of \nrelease of foodgrains under Sampoorana Gramin Rozgar Yojana and National Food for \nWork Programme. \n(d) No, Sir. As per extant accounting arrangement, budgetary support in the form of \nSpecial Securities are reflected in the gross expenditure but do not impact the defici t \nindicators as the investment in these Special Securities are adjusted against receipts. \nHowever, the servicing of these Securities in the form of interest payment is taken into \naccount for the calculation of fiscal deficit. \nInflow of foreign exchange  \n335. SHRI RAHUL BAJAJ: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n(a) what are the details of inflow of foreign exchange on the capital account during \nthe year, 2006 -07, category-wise, and which of these inflows are productive and which \nspeculative; and \n(b) what corrective action, if any has been taken or planned to curb speculative \ninflows? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) and (b) The details of inflow of foreign exchange on the \ncapital account during, 2006-07 are set out below: \n(in US$ million)  \nNet Capital Flows 2006-07 2006-07 (P) \nForeign Direct Investment 8437 \nPortfolio Investment 7062 \nExternal Assistance 1770 \nExternal Commercial Borrowings 16084 \nNRI Deposits 3895 \nShort-term Credits 3275 \nOthers 4421 \nTOTAL: 44944 \nP:   Preliminary \nCapital flows are not classified as productive or speculative as financial resources, \nby nature, are capable of productive uses. Usually capital flows are categorized as short -\nterm and long -term flows. Portfolio investment and short -term credits may be \nconsidered as short -term flows which could have elements of volatility associated with \nthem. \n\n64      Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \nDelay in offloading of shares by LIC \n336. SHRI RAJNATH SINGH: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to refer  \nto answer to Unstarred question 3565 given in the Rajya Sabha on 8th May, 2007  \nand state: \n(a) the reasons for delay in offloading of shares of companies by LIC where the \noffer price has been found acceptable;. \n(b) the loss incur red by LIC due to this delay as sensex has zoomed up and timely \ndisinvestment could have resulted in more then trebling the amount realized;  \n(c) what action is envisaged against LIC for this delay; \n(d) whether the proposal submitted by Chandrika Traders Ltd. with respect to Oswal \nAgro Mills has been found acceptable; and \n(e) if not, the reasons therefor and details for rejecting the proposal; and  \ninordinate delay in this disinvestment? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) Life Insurance Corporation of India (LIC) has reported that its \ninternal Committee of Officers, constituted for considering the offers received by the \nCorporation for sale of its equity stake in illiquid/unlisted companies, has not so far \nfound the offer prices acceptable and, as such, there is no delay on the part of LIC in \noffloading of shares. \n(b) and (c) LIC has reported that since these illiquid/unlisted companies are not \nactively traded on the Stock Exchanges, the upward movement in Sensex has no impa ct \non the prices of shares of these companies. As such, no loss has been suffered by the \nLIC in this regard. \n(d) and (e) LIC has reported that it received 3 offers with regard to Oswal Agro Mills \nand the offer of Chankdrika Traders Ltd. being the lowest wa s not found acceptable by \nits internal Committee of Officers. LIC has further reported that since Oswal Agro Mills \nhas decided to enter into high growth business segment like real estate development, \npaper chemicals and petroleum, etc., there is a possibil ity of realizing higher price at a \nlater stage. Moreover, the Company is making efforts to get the equity shares relisted on \nthe Bombay Stock Exchange/National Stock Exchange for resuming the trading of \nshares and in which case there would be better prospects for realising a higher price. \nCheck on increase in floating home loan rates \n337. SHRI MANOHAR JOSHI: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government have raised interest rates on floating Housing Loans from 9 \npercent last year to 1 1-12 percent this year resorting to raising EMIs, Home Loan \nborrowers are being forced to clear part of dues to keep EMI under check;  \n(b) if so, what are the reasons for raising interest rates on floating home loan so \nquickly: and \n(c) whether Government intends to introduce somemechanism to keep a check on \nsuch frequent increases in floating home loan? \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions     65 \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) to (c) With effect fro m October 18, 1994, RBI has deregulated \nthe interest rates on advances above Rs. 2 lakh and the rates of interest on such \nadvances are determined by the banks themselves with the approval, of their Boards. \nBanks have been advised to fix their Benchmark Pri me Lending Rate (BPLR) after \ntaking into account cost of funds, transaction cost, risk factor, margin and other \noverhead expenses. \nRefinancing of poor rural banks \n†338. SHRI UDAY PRATAP SINGH: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) whether Gover nment are considering for refinancing the financially poor rural \nbanks; and \n(b) if so, the details thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) and (b) National Bank for Agriculture and Rural Development \n(NABARD) div ides Regional Rural Banks (RRBs) into categories based on certain \ncriteria. The quantum of refinance for each category is decided by them based on the \nfinancial health of the RRB and the banks realistic lending programme acceptable to \nNABARD. \nIrregularities in purchase of stationery items \n339. SHRI MOTIUR RAHMAN: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) whether different departments of his  Ministry are not following the provisions \nof General Financing Rules and other instructions on the purchase of stationery etc.; \n(b) if so, the details of the irregularities brought out by the MPs and the CAG in the \nrecent past and what action has been taken thereon; and. \n(c) what steps has his Ministry taken to ensure that public money is not  \nsquandered alongwith details? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) Stationery and other items are purchased by different \nDepartments of the Ministry as per the power delegated under Delegation of Financial \nPower Rules (DFPRs). While exercising such powers, Departments are required to \nfollow the provisions of General Financial Rules (GFRs) and other relevant \ninstructions. \n(b) If any matter related with violation of provisions of General Financial Rules is \nbrought to the notice by Hon'ble Me mbers of Parliament (MPs) and/or Comptroller and \nAuditor General of India (C&AG), replies/Action Taken Notes are furnished as the case \nmay be. In the recent past, C&AG has conducted performance audit of internal control \nin selected Central Ministries, including Ministry of Finance. This is \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi \n\n66      Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred. questions \nan on-going process and feedback provided by Hon'ble MP& and C&AG is taken into \naccount for improvement in systems and procedures. \n(c) Instructions are issued from time to time to all Heads of Departments to follow \nthe provisions under GFRs and other relevant Government instructions issued from time \nto time on purchase of stationery and other items. General  principles relating to \nexpenditure and payment of money as mentioned in the GFRs are required to be \nfollowed by spending offices. Besides, systems of pre -check of bills, internal audit and \nstatutory audit are in vogue to ensure that public money is not squandered. \nPendency of Utilisation Certificate   \n340. SHRI MOTIUR RAHMAN: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether more than 37000 Utilisation Certificates (UCs) are pending upto March \n31, 2006 from several Ministries and key departments of  Government with a financial \nimplication of more than Rs. 14500 crore; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) the reasons for releasing funds to these Ministries and some statutory bodies and \nNGOs without satisfying itself about utilization of the grants; and \n(d) the measures taken by Government to check misuse of public money by these \nMinistries, statutory bodies and NGOs? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) Yes, Sir. \n(b) As per report No.3 of 2007 of Comptroller and Auditor Genera l of India, a total \nof 37428 Utilisation Certificates were pending upto March 31, 2006 involving a total \namount of Rs. 14595.32 crores. Details of outstanding utilisation certificates are given \nin the enclosed Statement-I {See below). \n(c) As per Rule 20 of Del egation of Financial Power Rules, full powers have been \ndelegated to Department of Central Government for sanctioning grants -in-aid provided \nsuch grants -in-aid are in accordance with the rules or principles prescribed with the \nprevious consent of the Minis try of Finance. Since the grants are released by the \nrespective Ministries/Departments to the statutory bodies/NGOs, these \nMinistries/Departments are to ensure that the provisions of General Financial Rules are \ncomplied with, before releasing grants to suc h organisations/statutory bodies. Ministry \nof Finance has, however, been advising the administrative Ministries/ Departments from \ntime to time, to ensure compliance and satisfy themselves about utilisation of the grants.  \n(d) As per provision of Rule 212 (1) of  General Financial Rules 2005, institutes/ \norganisations receiving non -recurring grants are required to submit utilisation \ncertificates within 12 months of the closure of the financial year, and Ministries/ \nDepartments are expected to scrutinize the receip t of these certificates. Where such \ncertificate is not received from the grantee organisation within the prescribed period, the \nMinistry is at liberty to blacklist such institutions or organisations from any future grant \nfrom the Government. Also, as per R ule 212(1) in respect of the recurring grants, \nMinistry/Department concerned should release any amount sanctioned for \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions     67 \nthe subsequent financial year only after Utilisation Certificates on provis ional basis in \nrespect of grants of the preceding financial year is submitted. Release of grants -in-aid in \nexcess of 75% of the total amount sanctioned for the subsequent financial year shall be \ndone only after the Utilisation Certificate and the Annual Au dited statement relating to \ngrants-in-aid released in the preceding year are submitted to the satisfaction of the \nMinistry/Department concerned. Ministry of Finance have also been advising the \nadministrative Ministries/Departments from time to time to ensure compliance. \nThere are some other provisions in General Financial Rules to check misuse of \npublic money. Rule 211 provides that the accounts of all grantee institutions shall be \nopen to inspection by the sanctioning authority, and audit both by the Compt roller & \nAuditor General of India and internal audit by the Principal Accounts Office of the \nconcerned Ministry, whenever the institution or organisation is called upon to do so. \nRule 212(2) of General Financial Rules provide for the inclusion of details o f grants \nreleased to such bodies in the Annual Reports of Ministries/Departments, and for the \nlaying of the annual reports and accounts of private and voluntary organisations in \nParliament, depending on the quantum of grant received from Government. \nRule 212(3) of these Rules further provides for submission of Achievement -cum-\nPerformance Reports to be submitted by grantee institutions or organisations soon after \nthe end of the financial year. \nStatement \nDetails of outstanding utilisation \n(Rupees in lakh)  \nMinistry/Department Utilisation Certificates \noutstanding in respect of \ngrants released upto March, \n2005 which were due by 31 \nst March, 2006 \n Number Amount \n1 2 3 \nAgriculture 88 6095.34 \nAndaman & Nicobar Administration 5 53.06 \nAtomic Energy 144 889.22 \n1 Central Board of Direct Taxes 10 0.93 \nChemicals and Petrochemicals 12 1108.78 \nCommerce 127 29637.91 \nTextiles 1094 7485.50 \nConsumer Affairs 113 106.18 \n\n68      Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions  \n1 2 3 \nFood and Public Distribution 7 196.61 \nEnvironment and Forests                                                       7546 92247.65 \nElection Commission of India NA 2.05 \nDepartment of Economic Affairs 12 4387.06 \nDepartment of Revenue 17 309.60 \nFood Processing Industry 902 13608.98 \nHigher Secondary Education                                                  3550 150183.81 \nElementary Education and literacy                                          1730 464198.98 \nWomen and Child Development                                             6036 34218.66 \nHealth                                                                                       1927 228311.89 \nFamily Welfare                                                                         1340 126626.16 \nAYUSH 561 26735.42 \nHome Affairs (PAO Sectt.) 2 016 \nInformation Technology 431 46631.00 \nHeavy industry 30 89-19.00 \nSmall Scale and Agro Rural Industries 30 843.00 \nIndustrial Policy & Promotion 62 24882.00 \nDeptt. of Public Enterprises 10 88.00 \nLabour 738 4422.15 \nNational Legal Services Authority 370 2024.54 \nLegislative Deptt. 32 2.16 \nLegal Affairs- 1 150.00 \nMines 6 270.65 \nGeological Survey of India 30 9.30 \nNon-conventional Energy Sources 65 4219.45 \nOcean Development M/o Earth Science 894 39469.04 \nPersonnel, Public Grievances and Pension, Personnel and 3 39.08 \ntraining   \nPlanning and Statistics Planning Commission 2 2.06 \nPower 5 929.79 \nRural Development 156 40376.94 \nDepartment of Biotechnology 118 64.61 \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG.. 2007] Unstarred questions'   69  \n1 2 3 \nShipping 1 229.00 \nSpace 326 2367.00 \nUrban Development 123 9606.15 \nHousing and Urban Poverty Alleviation 80 33891.19 \nWater Resources 39 110.96 \nYouth Affairs and Sports 8653 53581.72 \nGRAND TOTAL: 37428 1459532.74 \nAction against IIPM for evasion of Service Tax \n341. SHRI DHARAM PAL SABHARWAL: Will the Minister of FINANCE be  \npleased to refer to answer to Unstarred Question 4354 given in the Rajya Sabha on  \n15th May, 2007 and state: \n(a) what action Government have taken against the Indian Institute of Planning  and \nManagement (IIPM) for not filing the Service Tax Returns for the last two years. \n(b) what is the estimated loss of revenue on account of evasion of Service Tax \nduring the above period for conducting coaching classes and providing MBA/BBA \ncourses not approved by Government; and \n(c) what action Government proposes to take against the IIPM for recovery of \nservice tax? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI S.S. \nPALANIMAN1CKAM): (a) Indian Institute of Planning and Management has been \nissued Show C ause Notices by the Directorate General of Central Excise Intelligence \nfor non- filing of service tax returns and non -payment of service tax on Commercial \nTraining and Coaching Services. \n(b) The Total service tax along with Education Cess demanded from IIPM  for the \nperiod covering last two years is Rs. 5,03,04,802/- \n(c) Action as per law is being taken for recovery of service tax, from IIPM. \nUpgradation of economic status by USAID \n342. SHRI B.K. HARIPRASAD: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether in view of the booming economy India now enjoys, the USAID has  \nnow upgraded India's  economic status from a developing country to a transforming \ncountry; \n(b) whether because of India's  economic resurgence, USAID has now  \ndowngraded its share of fund assistance to India  to around $ 81 million  in 2008; and \n(c) whether in contrast. Afghanistan and Pakistan together will receive more than  \n85 percent of S 2.2 billion  earmarked for South-East Asian countries? \n\n70      Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) Yes, Sir. \n(b) For the US Fiscal year 2008, USAID has requested US Government  \nforeign assistance level of US$ 81 million for India. However, the budget for Fiscal \nYear, 2008 has not been approved by US Congress as yet. \n(c) For the US fiscal year 2008, USAID has requested foreign assistance levels  \nfor Afghanistan and Pakistan about 84 percent of US$ 2.198 billion, the amount  \nrequested for the South and Central Asian Countries. \nBungling of mutual funds \n343. SHRI RAJEEV CHANDRASEKHAR: Will the Minister of FINANCE be  \npleased to state: \n(a) whether SEBI is aware that Tata Tax Advantage Fund launched by Tata Mutual \nFund in Febuary, 2006, did not appreciate to the desired level inspi te of substantial \nappreciation of stock market during, 2006-07; \n(b) if so, whether SEBI propose to inquire into this bungling by the managers of the \nFund for suspected bad investment due to corruption or negligence at the cost of small \ninvestors; \n(c) if so, by when that is likely to be done; and \n(d) if not, the reasons therefor? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) The performance of a Mutual. Fund Scheme depends on \nseveral factors including scheme objective, investment strategy, market movement, etc. \nHowever, Tata Tax Advantage Fund has generated a compound annualized return of \n11.48% as on July 31,2007 since its inception. \n(b) to (d) The Asset Management Company of the Fund has confumed to SEBI that \nthey have complied with all relevant and applicable investment guidelines prescribed by \nSEBI. \nFinancial assistance to exporters \n344. SHRI RAJEEV CHANDRASEKHAR: Will the Minister of FINANCE be  \npleased to state: \n(a) whether there is any proposal under Government's consideration to extend \nfrnancial assistance to the exporters in the country who have suffered in the wake of \nappreciation of Indian Rupee vis-a-vis US Dollar; and \n(b) if so, the details thereof and by when the scheme is likely to be implemented and \nfrom which date? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) The committee to assess the impact of rupee appreciation on \nexports constituted by the Ministry of Commerce and Industry has given a series of \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions     71 \nrecommendations. The Government has already announced a relief package on 13th \nJuly, 2007 for exporters comprising enhanced Duty Entitlement Passbook (DEPB) rates, \nreduction in Export Credit Guarantee Corporation (ECGC) premium, release of Rs . 600 \ncrore for clearing all arrears of terminal excise duty and Central Sales tax \nreimbursement, enhancement of duty drawback rates and reduction in interest rates on \npre and post shipment credit. \nLending to farmers by NABARD \n345. SHRI JANARDHANA POOJARY: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased  \nto state: \n(a) whether NABARD lends to State Apex Cooperative Banks @ 2 1/2 per cent \ninterest for finally flowing down to the farmers as agricultural/crop loans via the \nDistrict, Taluk and Village Cooperative Banks; \n(b) whether every intermediary bank adds 2 -3 per cent interest while lending down -\nstream; \n(c) if so, the details thereof with ultimate rate of interest at which farmer gets loan; \n(d) whether to cut down the interest rate, NABARD proposes to directly lend to the \nfanners by doing away with the intermediary cooperative banks, so that the farmers gets \nloan @ 2 54 per cent interest, instead of 18-20 per cent; and \n(e) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) During the year 2006 -07, NABARD has provided Short Term \n(Seasonal Agricultural Operations) (ST -SAO) refinance to State Cooperative Banks \n(SCBs), on behalf of eligible District Central Cooperative Banks (DCCBs), at \nconcessional rates of interest of 2.5% p.a. with interest subvention from Government of \nIndia. The condition for availing this refinance is that Cooperative Banks provide crop \nloans upto Rs. 3.00 lakh per borrower at a rate of interest of 7% per annum. The rate of \ninterest on this concessional refinance has been raised to 3% p.a. for the year, 2007 -08. \n(b) to (e) NABARD has not prescribed any specific margin to be retained by various \ntiers of Short Term Cooperative Credit Structure (STCCS). Rate of interest on a loan \ndepends upon several factors e.g. cost of fund, transaction cost, risk cost, profit margin, \netc. As the crop loans to the ultimate borrower have to be provided at an interest rate of \n7%, the margin available to all the three tiers was 4.5% -during, 2006-07, which has been \nreduced to 4% during the year, 2007-08. \nNABARD has no proposal to lend directly to the farmers as it does not have the \ninfrastructure and organizational set up to do so. \nDifferences of farm loan interest \n346. SHRI RAVULA CHANDRA SEKAR REDDY: Will the Minister of FINANCE \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that differences still remain on farm loan interest; \n(b) if so, what steps the Ministry has taken in consultation with Ministry of \nAgriculture; \n\n72      Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \n(c) what future Government have disbursed during the last two years especially in \nAndhra Pradesh; and \n(d) what future steps Government would like to take in giving credit to farmers?  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) and (b) To ensure that farmers receive Short Term Production \nCredit at 7% per annum upto the upper limit of Rs.3 lacs on the principal amount, \nGovernment of India has formulated a scheme in consultation with other stake holders \nincluding implimented Ministry of Agriculture during 2006 -07. The scheme is being \nsince Kharif 2006, the Government is providing interest subvention of 2% per annum to \nPublic Sector Banks, Regional Rural Banks (RRBs) and Cooperative Banks on their \nlending from their own resources and refinance at concessional rates to cooperative \nbanks and RRBs on their borrowings from NABARD. \nThe condition for availing this  refinance is that Cooperative Banks provide crop \nloans upto Rs. 3.00 lakh per borrower at a rate of interest of 7% per annum. \n(c) The credit flow to the agriculture and allied  sector in the country during the \nyears, 2005- 06 and 2006- 07 has been Rs. 1,80,486 crore and Rs. 2,03,297 crore \nrespectively. Of this, the banks have disbursed Rs. 15,980 crore and Rs. 19,344 crore \nrespectively in Andhra Pradesh. \n(d) Banks have been instructed to achieve financial outreach through provision of a \nGeneral Credit Card to households, opening \"No Frills\" accounts with limited overdraft \nfacilities, to extend financial outreach by utilizing services of Civil Society Organisation \nlike Farmers Clubs, NGOs, Post Offices as Business Facilitator/Business Correspondent \nModel. Besides, banks are lending to groups through the mechanism of Self Help \nGroups, Joint Liability Group and Tenant Farmers Groups. \nSimplification of Transfer of deceased accounts \n347. SHRI TARIQ ANWAR: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government are aware that in case of death of a sole account holder, \npayment from account is made to the successor after a long  documentation or on the \nbasis of succession certification; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) what steps Government would take to simplify' the procedure? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) to (c) Reserve Bank of India  (RBI) has observed, that the \ncomplex procedures, applicable to settlement of claim  of a deceased depositor, cause \nconsiderable distress to family members. To simplify the process of settlement of claims \nof deceased depositors, RBI has advised banks to, inter alia, ensure the following:- \n(i) While making payment to the survivor(s)/nominee(s) of the deceased \ndepositor, banks should  desist  from insisting  on production of \nsuccession   certificate.    letter   of administration    or  probate,   etc.. \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG.. 2007] Unstarred questions     73 \nirrespective of the amount standing to the credit of the deceased account \nholder. \n(ii) In case where the deceased depositor had not made any nomination or for \nthe accounts other than those styled as \" either or survivor\" (such as single \nor jointly operated accounts), banks may fix a mininum threshold limit up \nto which claims in respect of the deceased depositors could be settled \nwithout insisting on production of any documentation other than a letter of  \nindemnity. \n(iii) Claims in respect of deceased depositors are settled and payments released \nto survivor(s)/nominee(s) within a period not exceeding 15 days ftom the \ndate of receipt of the claim  subject to the production of proof of death of \nthe depositor and suitable identification of the claimant (s) to the bank's \nsatisfaction. \n(iv) Wide publicity is given and guidance provided to deposit account holders \non the benefits of the nomination facility and the survivorship clause. \nVacant posts reserved for SCs/STs/OBCs \n348. SHRI O.T. LEPCHA: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether a large number of vacancies/posts reserved for SCs, STs and OBCs are \nlying vacant since>long in this Department and its subordinate offices: \n(b) if so, since when these p osts are lying vacant and the details of action so far to \nfill up the vacant posts of the above categories; and \n(c) by when all the vacant posts are likely to be filled up? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) to (c) The information is being collected and will be laid on \nthe Table of the House. \nInsurance Laws (Amendment) Bill, 2006  \n349. SHRI T.S. BAJWA: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to refer to the  \nanswer to Unstarred Question 2310 given in the Rajya Sabha o n 20th March, 2007 \nand state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that the Insurance Laws (Amendment) Bill, 2006 regarding \nincreasing of FDI in Insurance Sector was referred to a Group of Ministers for  \nclearance long back; and \n(b) if so, by when the said Bill  is like ly to be cleared by Government for.  \nintroduction in Parliament? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) and (b) Yes, Sir. The matter is still under consideration of the \nGroup of Ministers. \n\n74      Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \nParity in excise duty on Reverse Osmosis purifiers \n350. SHRIMATI HEMA MALINI: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to  \nstate: \n(a) whether Government are considering to bring the components and parts used in \nmanufacturing of Reverse Osmosis based water purifiers falling under tariff heading \n8421.21 at par with finished purifiers at nil rate of excise duty in order to compete with \nthe imported finished products; and \n(b if so, the details thereof and when it is expected to be implemented? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI S.S. \nPALANIMANICKAM): (a) and (b) Government is not considering at present any \nproposal for reduction of excise duty on components and parts of Reverse Osmosis \nbased water purifiers. \nAs regards competition faced due to imported purifiers, it is stated that there is \nprotection to the domestic products in the form of 10% customs duty which is attracted \non imported 'household type filters' and 7.5% on other water purifiers. In addition. on \nimported products, additional CVD at 4% is also leviable. \nExcise duty on Reverse Osmosis based water purifiers \n351. SHRIMATI HEMA MALIN I: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to  \nstate: \n(a) whether it is a fact that there is no excise duty imposed on the Rev erse Osmosis \nbased water purifiers; \n(b) if so, what are the reasons for charging full rate of excise duty on the \ncomponents and parts being used in water purifiers; \n(c) whether it is also a fact that charges of excise duty on components and parts of \nwater purifiers will make finished products costlier than the finished imported products; \nand \n(d) if so, Government's reaction thereto? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI S.S. \nPALANIMANICKAM): (a) Water purification equipment based on 'Reverse Osmosis \ntechnology using thin film composite membrane', other than 'household type filters' are \nexempt from excise duty. \n(b) There is no laid down policy of the Government to exempt excise duty on \ncomponents/parts of an item which does not attract excise duty. \n(c) and (d) T here are various factors like cost of raw materials, value addition etc \nwhich determine the final price of a product. Imported water purifiers will attract 10% \ncustoms duty in case of 'household type filters' and 7.5% on other water purifiers, as \nwell as 4 % additional CV duty. It is thus not possible to conclude that merely because \nthere is no exemption from excise duty on the parts, the imported filters will be \nnecessarily cheaper than domestic products. \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions     75 \nRising trends of frauds in on-line transactions \n352. SHRI VIJAY J. DARDA: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government are aware of the rising trend of frauds being committed  \nin on-line transactions and Banks or their clients suffering huge losses thereby; \n(b) if so, any measures are being taken towards countering this fast -growing \nmenace; and \n(c) what is the extent of losses suffered by PSU Banks due to such frauds during, \n2006, number-wise and amount-wise? \nTHE MINISTER OF ST ATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) and (c) RBI has reported that several instances of frauds in on -\nline transactions have been reported by banks, which include internet banking frauds, \ndebit cards/ATM frauds and credit card fraud s. The number of such frauds reported by \npublic sector banks during the year, 2006 are as under:  \nCredit Card frauds Debit Card/ATM frauds Internet Banking frauds \nNo. of    Amt. involved \nfrauds      (Rs. in lakh) \nNo. of    Amt. involved \nfrauds      (Rs. in lakh) \nNo. of \nfrauds \nAmt. involved \n(Rs. in lakh) \n10                21.36 66                82.44 Nil Nil \n(b) RBI had issued detailed guidelines on internet banking to all commercial banks \nin June, 2001, covering technology and security standards, legal issues and \nregulatory/supervisory issues. RBI had also issued a caution circular on February 16, \n2006 to all commercial banks on phishing attacks (creating fake websites of banks and \ncollecting customer details such as user ID, password etc. and thereby fr audulently \nwithdrawing money from the customer's account using fake credit card). The circular \ncontains details of the modus operandi of such attacks and the minimum set of \npreventive/detective measures to tackle them. \nInvestment of mutual funds by PSEs \n353. SHRI SITARAM YECHURY: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) whether it is a fact that Government are forcing the PSEs to enter into Mutual \nFund market; \n(b) if so, the reasons therefor; \n \n(c) whether Government would give any protection against the ma rket fluctuations; \nand \n(d) if not, how Government will compensate the loss incurred to PSEs due to \ninvestments in Mutual Funds? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) No, Sir. The Cabinet Committee on Economic Affairs h as \nrecently approved relaxation of the prohibition on investment of surplus funds by PSEs \nin mutual funds. However, it has restricted the relaxation to Navaratna and \n\n76      Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \nMiniratna Public Sector Enterprises investing in public sector mutual funds subject to \ncertain conditions.     \n(b) to (d) Do not arise in view of (a) above. \nWorld Bank assistance to State co-operatives \n†354. SHRI PYARELAL KHANDELWAL: Will the Minister of FINANCE -be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that World Bank has provided an assistance of 60 crore \ndollars to bring in improvement in the functioning of State Co -operatives, District Co -\noperative bank and agriculture loan societies; \n(b) if so. the details thereof; \n \n(c) whether Government are formulating any scheme to enable the above mentioned \ninstitutes to meet out financial needs of the poor farmers; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) and (b) Yes, Sir. Out of the total financial package of Rs. \n13,596 crore, for strengthening the Short Term Rural Cooperative Credit Structure \n(STCCS) in the country comprising State Cooperative Banks (SCBs), District Central \nCooperative Banks (DCCBs) and Primary Agriculture Credit  Societies (PACS), the \nAsian Development Bank and World Bank have agreed to provide an assistance of \nUS$1000 million  and US$600 million  respectively to the Government of India. The \nWorld Bank assistance is proposed in the form of loan of US$300 million fro m \nInternational Bank for Reconstruction and Development (IBRD) and a credit of SDR \n196.8 million  (equivalent to US$ 300 million) from International Development \nAssociation (IDA). \n(c) and (d) The Government has approved a revival package to strengthen Short  \nTerm Rural Cooperative Credit Structure (STCCS). The States willing to implement the \npackage are required to sign a Memorandum of Understanding (MoU) with Central \nGovernment and NABARD for carrying out certain legal and institutional reforms. \nFinancial as sistance for STCCS under, the package is available for cleansing of \nBalance Sheet as on 31st March, 2004 and increasing the capital to a specified minimum \nlevel subject to legal and institutional reforms. In order to ensure that STCCS continues \non sound fi nancial, managerial and governance norms, technical assistance is to be \nprovided to upgrade institutional and human resources of STCCS, computerization and \nsetting up proper internal control and accounting systems. \nLosses in Regional Rural Banks \n†355. SHRI  PYARELAL KHANDELWAL: Will the Minister of FINANCE be \npleased to state: \n(a) number of Regional Rural Banks functioning in the country at present; \n(b) the details of the branches of these banks, State-wise; \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred quest ions     77 \n(c) whether it is a fact that some of these banks are running in losses; \n(d) if so, whether Government propose to strengthen rural banks; and \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) The total number of Regional Rural Banks (RRBs) functioning \nin the country, as on date, is 96. \n(b) As on 31st March. 2007, RRBs have 14,519 branches. State -wise and RRB-wise \ndetails of branches are furnished in the Statement (See below). \n(c) As per provisional figures given by NABARD, 16 RRBs incurred losses during \nthe year, 2006-07. \n(d) and (e) The Government has decided to recapitalise RRBs having  \nnegative net worth in a phased manmer. As per provisional figures (una udited) for  \nMarch 31, 2007, there were 29 RRBs having negative net worth, comprising 11 \namalgamated RRBs and 18 stand alone RRBs. The modalities of the recapitalization  \nare being worked out \nStatement \nRRB-wise/State-wise number of branches as on 31 March, 2007   (unaudited)  