hickmagalur Kodagu Gramin \nBank \nCorporation Bank 48 \n31. Karnataka Vikash Gramin Bank Syndicate Bank 392 \n32. Krishna Gramin Bank State Bank of India 106 \n33. Pragathi Gramin Bank Canara Bank 351 \n34. Visveshwaraya Vijaya Bank 25 \n KARNATAKA  TOTAL  1125 \n35. North Malabar Gramin Bank Syndicate Bank 163 \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions     79  \n1 2 3 4 \n36. South Malabar Gramin Bank Canara Bank 218 \n KERALA TOTAL  381 \n37. Chambal Gwalior Kshetriya \nGramin Bank \nCentral Bank of India 59 \n38. Jhabua Dhar Khsetriya \nGramin Bank \nBank of Baroda 79 \n39. Madhya Bharath Gramin Bank State Bank of India 208 \n40. Mahakaushal  Kshetriya UCO Bank 42 \n Gramin Bank   \n41. Narmada Malwa Gramin Bank Bank of India 199 \n42. Ratlam Mandsaur Kshetriya \nGramin Bank \nCentral Bank of India 40 \n43. Rewa Sidhi Gramin Bank Union Bank of India 83 \n44. Satpura Kshetriya Gramin Bank Central Bank of India  237 \n45. Sharda Gramin Bank Allahabad Bank 54 \n46. Vidisha Bhopal Kshetriya \nGramin Bank \nState Bank of Indore 23 \n MADHYA PRADESH  TOTAL  1024 \n47. Aurangabad Jalna Gramin Bank Bank of Maharashtra 56 \n48. Marathvvada Gramin Bank Bank of Maharashtra 242 \n49. Ratnagiri Sindhudurg Gramin Bank Bank of India 39 \n50. Solapur Gramin Bank Bank of India 33 \n51. Thane Gramin Bank Bank of Maharashtra 20 \n52. Vidharbha Kshetriya Gramin Bank Central Bank of India 94 \n53. Wainganga Kshetriya Gramin Bank Bank of India 105 \n MAHARASHTRA TOTAL  589 \n54. Manipur Rural Bank U.B.I  27 \n MANIPUR  TOTAL  27 \n55. Khasi Jaintia Rural Bank State Bank of India 51 \n MEGHALAYA TOTAL  51 \n56. Mizoram Rural Bank State Bank of India  54 \n MIZORAM  TOTAL  54 \n57. Nagaland Rural Bank State Bank of India 8 \n NAGALAND  TOTAL  8 \n\n80      Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions  \n1 2 3 4 \n58. Baitarni Gramin Bank Bank of India 101 \n59. Dhenkanal Gramin Bank Indian Overseas Bank 54 \n60. Kalinga Gramya Bank UCO Bank 182 \n61. Puri Gramin Bank Indian Overseas Bank 114 \n62. Rushikulya Gramin Bank Andhra Bank 77 \n63. Utkal Gramya Bank State Bank of lndia 321 \n ORISSA TOTAL  849 \n64 Faridkot Bhatinda Kshetriya \nGramin Bank \nPunjab & Sind Bank 22 \n65. Malwa Gramin Bank State Bank of Patiala 41 \n66. Punjab Gramin Bank Punjab National Bank 148 \n PUNJAB  TOTAL  211 . \n67. Baroda Rajasthan Gramin Bank Bank of Baroda 267 \n68. Hadothi Kshetriya Gramin Bank Central Bank of India 81 \n69. Jaipur Thar Gramin Bank UCO Bank 209 \n70. Mewar Anchalik Gramin Bank Bank of Rajasthan 58 \n71. MGB Gramin Bank State Bank of Bikaner & Jaipur \n205 72. Rajasthan Gramin Bank Punjab National Bank 195 \n RAJASTHAN   TOTAL  1015 \n  73. Pallavan Grama Bank Indian Bank 50 \n74. Pandyan Gramin Bank Indian Overseas Bank 170 \n TAMIL  NADU   TOTAL  220 \n75. Tripura Gramin Bank United Bank of India 88 \n TRIPURA TOTAL  88 \n76. Aryavrat Gramin Bank Bank of India  293 \n77. Ballia Kshetriya Gramin Bank Central Bank of India 87 \n78. Baroda Eastern Uttar Pradesh \nGramin Bank \nBank of Baroda 539 \n79. Baroda Western Uttar Pradesh \nGramin Bank \nBank of Baroda 116 \n80. Devi Patan Kshetriya Gramin Bank Punjab National Bank 77 \n81. Etawah  Kshetriya Bank Central Bank of India  50 \n82. Kashi Gomti Samyut Gramin Bank Union Bank of India 336 \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG.. 2007] Unstarred questions     81  \n1 2 3 4   \n83. Kisan Gramin Bank Punjab National Bank 55 \n84. Kshetriya Kisan Gramin Bank UP State Coop. Bank 63 \n85. Lucknow Kshetriya Gramin Bank Allahabad Bank    240 \n86. Prathama Bank Syndicate Bank 175 \n87. Purvanchal Gramin Bank State Bank of India  305 \n88. Rani Lakshmi Bai Kshetriya \nGramin Bank \nPunjab National Bank 43 \n89. Shreyas Gramin Bank Canara Bank 183 \n90. Triveni Kshetriya Gramin Bank Allahabad Bank 208 \n91. Uttar Pradesh Gramin Bank Punjab National Bank 88 \n UTTAR  PRADESH  TOTAL  2858 \n92. Nainital Almora Kshetriya \nGramin Bank \nBank of Baroda 58 \n93. Uttaranchal Gramin Bank State Bank of India 107 \n UTTARANCHAL TOTAL  165 \n94. Bangiya Vikash Gramin Bank United Bank of India 547 \n95. Paschim Banga Gramin Bank UCO Bank 216 \n96. Uttar Banga Kshetriya \nGramin Bank \nCentral Bank of India  114 \n WEST  BENGAL TOTAL  877 \n ALL INDIA  TOTAL  14519 \nShortage of coins \n356. SHRI ABU ASIM AZMI: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Central Government are well aware that there is an acute shortage of \ncoins of Rs. 1/ Rs. 2 and Rs. 5 in the country as on date and public is in lot of difficulties \nfor want of coins; \n(b) if so, the steps being taken by Central Government for proper circulation and \navailability of the small coins in the country; and \n(c) whether the small coins are black -mailed and shortage if created and if so, what \naction has been taken against the persons found guilding in this regard? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PA WAN KUMAR \nBANSAL): (a) to (c) Certain reports have appeared in the press and complaints from \npublic/trade bodies have also been received regarding shortage of coins in certain \nmarkets. The following steps have been taken to augment the availability of coins: \n\n82      Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \n(i) The Government has been minting Rs. 2 coins in Ferritic Stainless Steel \ninstead of cupro-nickel since 2005 and has also decided to mint Rs. 5 coi n \nin Ferritic Stainless Steel instead of Cupro -nickel to abort melting by \nunscrupulous elements. \n(ii)    Minting of Re.l, Rs. 2 and Rs. 5 coins has been placed on fast track. \n(iii) Several issue offices of Reserve Bank of India held coin melas distributed \ncoins through mobile banks at market place etc. Reserve Bank of India also \nadvised select currency chest branches to do the same. \n(iv) To cater to the large demand of hotels restaurants, retail shops etc., \ndistribution arrangements have been made by Reserv e Bank of India \nthrough registered Association/Trade bodies wherever they have come \nforward for the purpose. \n(v) In Mumbai, arrangements have been made for distribution of coins through \nfive post offices and similar arrangements is being put in place in ot her \nStates. \n(vi) All the Banks were advised to ensure distribution of coins through their \ncurrency chests branches proactively and they were also advised to identify \nsome branches in each district for the specific purpose of coin distribution. \n(vii) Banks have been advised to consider installation of coins vending machines. \nGranting loans to farmers by banks \n357. SHRI ABU ASIM AZMI: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Central Government are well aware that large number of farmers  \nare being deprived of Rabi and Kharif loans annually by the Nationalised Banks in  \nthe country; \n(b) if so, whether any action has been taken against such Nationalised Banks \nresponsible for not granting crop loans to the farmers; \n(c) if so, the details thereof in this regard; and \n(d) how the financial help shall be provided to the farmers to meet their situation?  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) to (d) The agricultural credit disbursed by Public Sector \nCommercial Banks has increased substantially during the last four years as may be \nobserved from the data furnished below:- \n(Rs. in crores)  \nYear Amount disbursed \n2003-04 42.211 \n2004-05 65,218 \n2005-06 94,278 \n2006-07 1,14,480 \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions     83 \nNationalised Banks provide Rabi and Kharif loans to farmers as per the guidelines \nframed by RBI. There is no specific report regarding deprivation of the farmers for Rabi \nand Kharif loans by the nationalised banks. \nAssessment of loan paying capacity of States \n†358. SHRI HARISH RAWAT: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n(a) the details of the loans taken from different national and international financial \ninstitutions and market by Uttarakhand, Jharkhand, Chhattisgarh and Gujarat d uring the \nlast six years; and \n(b) whether the assessment of the capacity of the loan repayment of these States has \nbeen made? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) The details of the loans taken from different national and \ninternational financial institutions by Uttarakhand, Jharkhand, Chhattisgarh and Gujarat \nin the last six years is given in the enclosed Statement (See below). \n(b) No Sir. However, for a limited purpose of debt sustainability, States having \ninterest payments to Total Revenue Receipts ratio of greater than 20% are taken to be \ndebt stressed. \nStatement \nLoans taken by States from National and International Sources from \n2001-02 to 2006-07* \n(Rs.  in  crores)  \nSI.     States loan taken from 2001- 2002- 2003- 2004- 2005- 2006- \nNo.  02 03 04 05 06 07 \n1        2 3 4 5 6 7 8 9 \nI.      Chhattisgarh National  FIS 318.89 1070.65 800.63 580.32 183.78 117.28 \n International   FIS 4.72  38.33 3.50 ,8.26 44.55 45.03 \n GRAND TOTAL 323.61 1108.98 804.13 588.58 228.33 162.31 \n2.      Gujarat National  FIS 1883 62 2706.62 3897.91 4343.05 1482.27 879.00 \n International   FIS 623.53 1123.46  704,05 853.77 459.72 616.27 \n GRAND TOTAL 2507. i 5 3830.08 4601.96 5196.82 1941    99 1495.27 \n3. Jharkhand National  FIS 1364.29 1720.30 873.47 2218.04 5603.00 910.52 \n International   FIS 0.00 0.00 1.06 1.02 2.05 12.44 $ \n GRAND TOTAL 1364,29 1720.3 874.53 2219.06 5605.05 922.96 \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi \n\n84      Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions  \n1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 9 \n4 Uttarakhand \nNational   FIS \nInternational   FIS \n2018.64 \n17.41 \n2885.34 \n200.71 \n3474.04 \n115.17 \n2439 86 \n65.79 \n1976.89 \n34.82 \n1904.35 \n47.9497  $ \n  GRAND TOTAL: 2036.05 3086.05 3590.11 2505.65 2011.71 1952.30  \n* Based on responses received from State Govts. \n$ ACA released for Externally Aided Projects in the State Sector \nNew banking Ombudsman Scheme \n359. SHRI S. S. AHLUWALIA: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that Government have introduced a new banking Ombudsman \nScheme; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) the salient distinction between the new scheme with the old one; \n \n(d) the number of complaints registered with the Banking Ombudsman by intending \nborrowers, falling under the priority sector of loans, belongi ng to minority, ST, SC and \nother disadvantaged categories respectively about denial of loans by PSU and private \nsector banks year-wise since 2000-01 till 2006-07; \n(e) the details of steps taken for redressal in respective cases; and \n(f) steps being taken to improve upon the Ombudsman mechanism in future? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) and (b) A new Banking Ombudsman Scheme came into effect \nfrom January 1, 2006. The Banking Ombudsman Scheme, 2006, inter -alia, covers new \ngrounds of complaints such as Credit Card complaints and complaints against non-\nadherence to Fair Practice Code by banks, facilitate online submission of complaims, \netc,. \n(c) The salient distinction between the Banking Ombudsman Scheme, 2002 and \n2006 are given in Statement (See below). \n(d) and (e) The database of RBI does not generate the information in the manner as \ncalled for in the question. However, the Year -wise details of number of complaints \nagainst sanctioning of loans and advances dealt under\" the Banki ng Ombudsman \nScheme from the year 2001-02 to 2005-06 are given as below:  \nDuring the year 2001-02 2002-03 2003-04 2004-05 2005-06 \nLoans and advances 1982 1651 1226 2291 5215 \n(f) Reserve Bank constantly works on making appropriate changes to the Banking \nOmbudsman Scheme so that the same will serve the customers of banks. An amendment \nto BO Scheme, 2006 was made in May, 2007 allowing complainants to appeal against \nthe decisions of the Banking Ombudsman to reject a complaint. \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG.. 2007] Unstarred questions     85 \nAs  on  date,  no  further changes to the  Banking Ombudsman  Scheme,  2006  is \ncontemplated. \nStatement \nThe main differences between the banking Ombudsman scheme, 2002 and the \nbanking Ombudsman scheme, 2006  \nSI. No.   Banking Ombudsman Banking Ombudsman \nScheme, 2002 Scheme, 2006 \nAPPOINTMENT  \n1.           Retired Executive Directors and Serving senior officers in the \nabove of banks or Joint Secretaries rank of General Manager and \nand above of Government were Chief General Manager will be \nposted as Banking Ombudsmen. posted as Banking Ombudsmen. \nSTAFFING OF BANKING OMBUDSMAN OFFICES \n2.           The staff of BO Offices was from RBI will staff the BO Offices \nRBI and SLBC Convenor banks. fully. \nFUNDING OF THE SCHEME  \n3.            The Scheme was funded by the The Scheme will be fully funded \nparticipant banks in the proportion by RBI.     \nof their working funds.  \nGROUNDS OF COMPLAINT  \n4. Various new grounds of \n complaints such as credit \n complaints have been included \n in the BO Scheme, 2006. \nCOMPLAINT SUBMISSION  \n5.            Mandatory that the specified Complaints can be in any form \ncomplaint submission form be as long as all information is \nused. submitted. \n Further, complaints can be \n- submitted by email and online. \n Further, complaints can be \n forwarded by RBI/Government to \n the Banking Ombudsmen. \nAPPEAL PROCESS  \n6.            Review Process. Appellation process. \n7.            Only the banks can file for a Both the banks and the \nreview against the Award. complainant can appeal. \n\n86     Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \nForeign exchange reserves \n360. SHRI S.S. AHLUWALIA: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n(a) the details of foreign exchange (forex) reserve as on June 30, 2007; \n(b) whether it is a fact that Reserve Bank of India (RBI), is the sole custodian of the \nforex reserve; \n(c) whether RBI lend funds out of its forex reserve to any borrower other than \nGovernment; \n(d) if so, the relevant legal provision therefor; \n(e) entities which are eligible to borrow; \n(f) whether RBI has lent and/or is contemplating to lent funds from forex reserves to \nvarious organisations, including foreign based; and \n(g) if so, details thereof as on 30th June, 2007 indicating terms of such lending? \nTHE MINISTER OF STA TE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) The details of foreign exchange reserves as on June 30, 2007 \nare: \n(in US $ million)  \nForeign Currency Assets 206,106 \nGold 6,787 \nSDRs 1 \nReserve Position in IMF 460 \nForeign Exchange Reserves (Total) 213,354 \n(b) Foreign currency assets and gold are held on the balance sheet of RBI and RBI is \nthe sole custodian of foreign exchange reserves held in the form of foreign currency \nassets and gold. SDRs and Reserve Position with IMF are held in the books o f \nGovernment of India. \n(c) RBI is empowered to extend loans and advances in foreign currency to \nscheduled banks and certain specified financial institutions provided the borrowing bank \nor financial institution has made loans or advances in foreign currency for  financing \ninternational trade or import of capital goods or for such other purposes as approved by \nthe Central Government and the outstanding amount of loan obtained from RBI is \nlimited to the amount of loan or advance so made and outstanding. Such loans are not \ntreated as a part of foreign exchange reserves. \n(d) The relevant provision is Section 17(12B)  of Reserve Bank of India Act, 1934. \n(e) Scheduled Banks, IDBI, EXIM Bank, IRBI, SIDBI, IFCI, any state financial \ncorporation and any other financial institution approved by the Central Government. \n(f) and (g) Finance Minister in his Budget Speech (2007 -08 Budget) indicated that \nthe Government in consultation with RBI would consider the recommendation \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions     87 \nof Dee pak Parikh Committee to \"use a small part of the foreign exchange reserves \nwithout the risk of monetary expansion\", for the purpose of financing infrastructure \ndevelopment projects. No funds have been lent from forex reserves as on 30th June, \n2007. \nBad credit given by Punjab National Bank \n361. SHRI VAR1NDER SINGH BAJWA: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased \nto state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that Government have stopped investigations into the bad \ncredit given by Punjab National Bank to private corporate borr owers during 2005- 06 \nand 2006-07, which put the bank to a loss of one thousand crore of rupees and thereby \nabsolved the top officials including the CMD of the bank of any accountability in the \nmatter; \n(b) if so, the reasons therefor; and \n(c) whether Government propose to bring the matter to the knowledge of RBI and \nproceed with the investigations to their logical conclusion? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) and (b) Government does not conduct investigation into the \ncredit given by the banks. However, specific complaints when referred to the Ministry \nare examined in consultation with RBI/CVC/the bank concerned and suitable action \ntaken. The bank conducts internal investigations of the accounts which become NPA for \nexamining staff accountability in accordance with the laid down procedure. CBI/Police \nalso investigates into the cases suo motu or when referred to them by the banks. \n(c) Every fraud case is reported to RBI by the banks and Government has no role in \nhis. \nBungling of funds by PNB \n362. SHRI VARINDER SINGH BAJWA: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased \nto state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that Punjab National Bank enhanced their exposure in favour \nof a big diamond exporting company by Rs. 350 crore without the concurrence  of the \nleader of the consortium and whether the enhanced exposure has put the bank to loss of \nRs. 200 crore as no exports have been made by the concerned borrower; \n(b) if so, whether it is a fact that the bank has not reported the matter to Government \nagencies; and \n(c) if so, the action proposed to be taken in the matter against those responsible for \nthe bungling? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) to (c) The information is being collected and will be laid on \nthe Table of the House. \n\n88     Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \nMonitoring of LC operations \n363. SHRIMATI PREMA CAR1APPA: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased \nto state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that in the absence of Centralized Monitoring Mechan ism to \nkeep track of economic offences, fly by night IT Firms are making hay and duping \nindividuals and companies in a fraud totalling Rs. 15,000 crores and Rs. 3 lakhs;  \n(b) whether it is also a fact that there is a nexus between private and foreign banks \nand agencies and touts: \n(c) whether Goverment are considering to set up a regulatory authority to monitor \nLetter of Credit (LC) operations, in the absence of which the forgeries of this kind are \non the rise; and \n(d) whether Anti -national elements are allowing the inflow of circulation of fake \nLetter of Credits (LCs) in collusion with Indian banks, at grave risk to the national \nsafety and what measures are taken in this regard? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI S.S. \nPALANIMANICKAM): (a) to (d) Infor mation is being collected and will be laid on the \nTable of the House. \nSmuggling of small denomination coins \n364. SHRI RAJKUMAR DHOOT: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) whether it is a fact that small denomination coins of India are being sm uggled \ninto Bangladesh for melting and put to varied uses such as making shaving blades;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) the steps taken to stop such malpractice? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI S.S. \nPALANIMANICKAM): (a) and (b) such instance has come to notice. \n(c) All field formations have been sensitized to maintain strict vigil in this regard. \nRaising of foreign currency loans \n365. SHRI P.R. RAJAN: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government propose to all ow  Indian companies to raise foreign \ncurrency loans at rates much lower than that of external commercial borrowings; and  \n(b) if so, the details thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) and (b) As per existing pol icy, the all- in-cost ceiling for \nforeign currency loans/External Commercial Borrowings (ECBs) has been fixed as \nfollows: \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions    89  \nAverage Maturity period of loan All-in-cost ceilings over 6 month LIBOR* \nThree years and upto five years 150 basis points p.a. \nMore than five years 250 basis points p.a. \n*London  Interbank offered rate for the respective currency of borrowing or \napplicable benchmark. \nThe all-in-cost ceiling include rate of interest, and oth er fees and expenses payable \nin foreign currency except commitment fees, prepayment fee and fees payable in Indian \nRupees. \nThe above mentioned ceiling denotes the maximum cost permissible for raising \nECB. Indian Companies are permitted to raise foreign cur rency loans at rates lower than \nthe ceiling mentioned above. \nTarget based remuneration system for PSU employees \n366. SHRI P.R. RAJAN: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government are implementing target based remuneration system for  the \nemployees in PSUs; and \n(b) if so, the details thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) and (b) The information is being collected and will be\" laid on \nthe Table of the house. \nThrust to less capital intensive sectors \n367. SHRIMATI SUPRIYA SULE: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) whether sectors that are less capital and infrastructure intensive such as food \nprocessing, leather products, footwear, textiles, tourism and construction are likely to \nget more government support and incentives; \n(b) if so, whether these labour -intensive sectors hold the promise of job creation in \nrural areas and would go a long way in alleviating poverty, as per the economic survey;  \n(c) if so, what are the main thrust that Gover nment have given to these sectors \nduring the recent budget; and \n(d) by when, these schemes are likely to be implemented? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) to (d) Government has been encouraging industries in genera l \nby providing an enabling macro economic environment; non distortionary and \ninternationally competitive tax rates; and infrastructure support. Initiatives taken for the \ndevelopment of food procession, leather products, footwear, textiles, tourism and \nconstruction in Budget, 2007-08 include: \n\n90     Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \n1. Technology Upgradation funds scheme for textiles is proposed to be continued \nin the Eleventh Five Year Plan: \n2. Additional integrated textiles Parks have been sa nctioned for enabling textile \nsector to meet global demand; \n3. A cluster approach for the development of handlooms sector was introduced in \n2005-06. Addition 100- 150 clusters are proposed to be taken up in 2007-08; \n4. A credit policy for small and medium enterpr ises was announced in August, \n2005. As a result of this outstanding credit to SMEs increase from Rs. 135,200 \ncrore at the end of December 2005 to Rs. 173. 460 crore at the end of \nDecember, 2006; \n5. Excise duty exemption limit for small scale industries was ra ised to Rs 1.5 \ncrore; \n6. Biscuits whose retail sale price does not exceed Rs 100 per kilogram have been \nexempted from excise duty. All kinds of food mixes including instant food \nmixes have also been exempted from excise duties; \n7. Excise duty on part of footwear was also reduce from 16 per cent to 8 per cent; \nTax incentives to housing sector and emphasis on road connectivity are providing a \nboost to the construction and tourism activities. \nIncome given to States from divisible pool \n†368. SHRI LALIT KISHORE CHATUR VEDI: Will the Minister of FINANCE be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that the income received from service tax has not been kept in \ndivisible pool with the State Governments; \n(b) the amount of income given to the State Governments, if it is in the divi sible \npool alongwith State-wise and year-wise details thereof; and \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) No, Sir. As recommended by the 12th Finance Commission in \npara 7.36, service tax is part of the divisible pool of net proceeds of all sharable central \ntaxes. Presently service tax is not leviable in the State of Jammu & Kashmir and its \nproceeds are. therefore, not assignable to this State. \n(b) The details showing the share of net proceed s of Service Tax devolved to State \nGovernments for the last three financial years is given in the Statement (See below). \n(c) The question does not arise. \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi- \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions     91 \nStatement \nRelease of Service Tax to State Governments during the last \nthree years 2005-06 to 2007-08 \n(In crore of Rupees)  \nSI. No. State 2005-06 2006-07 2007-08 \n1 2 3 4 5 \n1. Andhra Pradesh 512.58 850.60 1107.94 \n2. Arunachal Pradesh 20.08 33.32 43.41 \n Assam 225..37 374.00 487.15 \n4. Bihar 768.42 1275.15 1660.94 \n5. Chhattisgarh 184.93 306.89 399.74 \n6. Goa 18.02 29.90 38.95 \n7. Gujarat 248.69 412.69 537.54 \n8. Haryana 74.90 124.28 161.89 \n9. Himachal Pradesh 36.38 60.37 78.64 \n10. Jammu & Kashmir 0.00 0.00 0.00 \n11. Jharkhand 234.18 388.61 506.18 \n12. Karnataka 310.72 515.63 671.63 \n13. Kerala 185.69 308.15 401.37 \n14. Madhya Pradesh 467.60 775.96 1010.72 \n15. Maharashtra 348.21 577.83 752.65 \n16. Manipur 25.24 41.88 54.56 \n17. Meghalaya 25.86 42.91 55.90 \n18. Mizoram 16.64 27.62 35.97 \n19. Nagaland 18.29 30.36 39.54 \n20. Orissa 359.62 596.78 777.33 \n21. Punjab 90.51 150.19 195.63 \n22. Rajasthan 390.85 648.59 844.82 \n23. Sikkim 15.82 26.25 34.19 \n24. Tamil Nadu 369.59 613.32 798.88 \n25. Tripura 29.78 49.42 64.37 \n26. Uttar Pradesh 1342.27 2227.44 2901.34 \n27. Uttarakhand 65.47 108.65 141.52 \n28. West Bengal 491.74 816.01 1062.90 \n TOTAL: 6877.45 11412.80 14865.70 \n\n92      Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \nRecovery of loans by banks \n369. SHRI MAHENDRA MOHAN: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) whether Government are aware that the reports have been coming for quite some \ntime that at times Banks resort to the use of coercive methods for recovery of loan in \ncase of default; \n(b) whether Government are also aware that many Banks have outsourced their loan \nrecovery to private companies which at times use musclemen to recover the amount and \nthese agents insult the person and make all kind of threatening calls to the person and \nhis family: \n(c) what are the guidelines of the RBI in this regard; and \n(d) what action has been taken against such banks and how many complaints have \nbeen received by Government in this regard? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) and (b) Reserve Bank of India (RBI) has reported that \nincidents of alleged use of coercive methods by some banks for recovery of loans in \ncase of default has come to their notice. There are some banks that have outsourced their \nloan recovery to private recovery agencies for assisting them in follow -up of loan \nrecovery from defaulting customers. On receiving reports in respect of use of \nmusclemen and harassment of the defaulting customers, RBI takes up the issue with the \nrespective banks for appropriate action. \n(c) and (d) As per RBI guidelines on Fair Practice Code for Lenders issued in May, \n2003, it is inter-alia, stipulated that in the matter of recovery of loans, the lender should \nnot resort to undue harassment viz. persistently bothering th e borrowers at odd hours, \nuse of muscle power for recovery of loans etc. \nRBI has issued instruction to all commercial banks/NBFCs (excluding Regional \nRural Banks) in November, 2005 regarding Credit Card Operations, these inter -alia \nstipulate that their age nts should not resort to intimidation or harassment of any kind, \neither verbal or physical, against any person in their debt collection efforts. This \nincludes acts intending to humiliate publicly or intrude the privacy of the credit card \nholders family members, referees and friends, making threatening and anonymous calls \nor making false and misleading representations. In case of any violation of extant \nguidelines by a bank, appropriate regulatory action is taken by RBI. \nThe Code of Banks' Commitments to Cus tomers formulated by Banking Codes and \nStandard Boards of India (BCSBI) provides, inter -alia, that the collection policy of the \nbank be built on courtesy, fair treatment and persuasion and the bank follows a security \nrepossession policy in consonance with the law. \nThe IBA has formulated a \"Model Code for collection of dues and repossession of \nSecurity\" which has been recommended to banks in January, 2007 for adoption \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions     93 \nand implementation. The Mode l Code, inter alia, provides that banks commit to \nmaintaining the dignity and respect of customers in their Debit Collection Policy and \nnot follow policies that are unduly coercive in collection of dues. \nDifficulties in filing of Income Tax Returns \n370. SHRI MAHENDRA MOHAN: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) whether Government are aware that filling up tax forms this time has become \nbig problem for the ordinary people and even the two -page form ITR -I meant for \nindividuals having income from s alary, pension and interest, requires expert assistance \nwhen it comes to filing returns; \n(b) if so, what is prompting Government to keep on changing the format everytime \nand forcing people to go to the consultants; and \n(c) what was the reason behind discontinuing 'Saral' form? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI S.S. \nPALANIMANICKAM): (a) and (b) The Income -tax Department has undertaken a \ncomprehensive exercise to computerize all its business processes. In line with the best \ninternational practice, electronic receipt' of return is an integral part of this exercise. It \nwas, therefore, necessary to redesign the return forms so as to make them annexure- less \nand make them amenable to electronic filing. This initiative was undertaken for the first \ntime in respect of returns for assessment year 2006 -07. The return forms for assessment \nyear 2006-07 were reviewed by the Parliamentary Standing Committee on Finance and \ncertain recommendations were made. \nThe income tax return forms are assessment year specific and are, therefore, required \nto be notified separately for every assessment year. Keeping in view the \nrecommendations of the Parliamentary Standing Committee on Finance, the legislative \nchanges, feed back from the public and the Institute of Chartered Accountants of India \nand the need for computerization, the Government notified a new series of income tax \nreturn forms ITR -1 to ITR -8. These forms have been made user friendly by providing \ndetailed instructions for the guidance of tax payers. \nTherefore, a tax  payer would not require any expert assistance in filling the return \nforms if he follows the instructions carefully. However, if a tax payer continues to have \nany difficulty in this regard, he can access any Help Centre of the Income -tax \nDepartment for assistance. \n(c) The earlier 'Saral' form neither provided the steps for computation of income nor \ndid they provide any kind of guidance/assistance leading to the computation of a \ntaxpayer's tax liability. They were, therefore, not user -friendly. Further, sepa rate \ncomputation sheet running into couple of pages, TDS certificates, account statements \nand other documents were invariably annexed to the earlier 'Saral' form. Thus, the earlier \n'Saral' form was not compatible with the electronic mode of filing returns.  Hence, it was \ndecided to discontinue with the earlier 'Saral' form. \n\n94      Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \nUse of force for recovery of loans \n371. SHRI C. RAMACHANDRAIAH: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased  \nto state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that a franchise recovery wing of a private bank was \nresponsible for the death of a Government employee in Hyderabad; \n(b) if so, whether any measures are being taken/proposed to be taken to restrain the \nprivate banks in adopting coercive measures to recover the outstanding advances from \nthe loanees; \n(c) if so, provide the details of the guidelines issued by the RBI for the purpose and \nthe action taken against the delinquent elements of the banks; and \n(d) if not, explain the reasons therefor? \nTHE MINISTER OF S TATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI PAWAN \nKUMAR BANSAL): (a) A borrower of ICICI Bank Ltd. Shri Y. Yadiah, collapsed in \nthe office of a collection agent of the bank, and died. The bank and its agency officials \nhave categorically denied having touched or hurt the borrower in any way. However, the \ncause of death of Shri Yadiah has not been ascertained so far. \n(b) to (d) Reserve Bank of India has issued guidelines on Fair Practices Code for \nLenders on 5th May, 2003 whereby the lenders are prohibited from res orting to means \ncausing undue harassment of the customers for recovery of their dues such as \npersistently bothering the borrowers at odd hours, use of muscle power for recovery of \nloans, making threatening calls, etc. Further, Indian Banks' Association has  issued a \n\"Model Code for collection of dues and repossession of security\" to be followed by its \nmember banks to maintain dignity and respect of the customers in their Debt Collection \nPolicy and not to follow policies that are unduly coercive in collection  of their dues. The \nBanking Codes and Standards Board of India.(BCSBI) has been set up to plan, evolve, \nprepare, develop, promote and publish voluntary comprehensive codes and standards for \nbanks for providing fair treatment to their customers. \nFurther, RBI has also issued Master Circular on credit card operations of the banks \non 02.07.2007 advising banks/NBFCs/their agents not to resort to intimidation or \nharassment of any kind, either verbal or physical, against any person in their debt \ncollection efforts. \nHawala route for NRI income \n372. SHRI C. RAMACHANDRAIAH: Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased  \nto state: \n(a) whether the Ministry has come across any strong indication that a part of the  \nNRI income is taking the hawala route and are entering into the c ountry's burgeoning  \nreal estate sector; \n(b) if so, whether any steps have been initiated  to contain the above  \ndevelopment: \nc) if so, what has been the outcome of the measures taken -upto discourage the \ndiversion of foreign exchange; and \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions     95 \nd) if not, the reasons therefor? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI S.S. \nPALANIMANICKAM): (a) No Sir. \n(b) to (d) Does not arise in view of reply to part (a) above. \nCoal gasification project by BHEL \n373. SHRI SANTOSH BAGRODIA: \nSHRI HARISH RAWAT: \nWill the Minister of HEAVY INDUSTRIES AND PUBLIC ENTERPRISES be \npleased to state: \n(a) Whether BHEL propose to set up 400 MW power plant based on coal \ngasification; \n(b) if so, whether BHEL have requisite experience for building up such a power \nplant based on Integrated Gasification Combined Cycle (IGCC) technology; \n(c) whether it is a fact that BHEL and NTPC have not been able to solve the \ndifferences on technical issues on 125 MW IGCC project; and \n(d) by when the 125 MW project is likely to commence? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE DEPARTMENT OF HEAVY INDUSTRY. \nMINISTRY OF HEAVY INDUSTRIES AND PUBLIC ENTERPRISES (SHRIMATI KANTI \nSINGH): (a) No Sir. At present there is no such proposal. \n(b) Does not arise in view of (a) above. \n(c) and (d) The Principal Scientific Advisor to the Government of India had set up a \nNational R&D Committee in 2003. As per the advice of the National R&D Committee, \nBHEL and NTPC have jointly prepared a Detailed Project Report (DPR). BHEL Board \nhas agreed with the approach indicated in DPR and has forwarded it to NTPC for \nnecessary action at their end. \nAppointment of independent directors in BHEL \n374. SHRI PRASANTA CHATTERJEE: \nSHRI TAPAN KUMAR SEN: \nWill the Minister of HEAVY INDUSTRIES AND PUBLIC ENTERPRISES be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether Government's attention has been drawn by various trade unions \nincluding President of the Centre of Indian Trade Unions (CITU) regarding appointment \nof independent directors in BHEL; \n(b) whether someone from ZARDA (Chewing Tobacco) indus try and another \nhaving links with a competitor of BHEL are functioning as independent director of \nBHEL; \n(c) if so, the details of the professional backgrounds of independent directors in \nBHEL board; and \n\n96      Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \n \n \n(d) the basis of selecting independent directors in PSEs to ensure that there is no \nconflict of interests? \nTHE MINISTER OF HEAVY INDUSTRIES AND PUBLIC ENTERPRISES \n(SHR1 SONTOSH MOHAN DEV): (a) to (d) From time to time references have been \nreceived regarding appointment of independent directors to the Boards of various PSEs \nincluding BHEL. Independent Directors are selected by the administrative ministries on \nthe recommendations of the Search Committee under the Department of Public \nEnterprises. Names of the identified candidates by the Search Committee are sent to the \nrespective PSEs for ascertaining if there is any conflict of interest. Only after receipt of \n\"No Objection\" from PSEs, the administrative ministries seek the approval of \nAppointments Committee of Cabinet. \nShri Ashok K. Aggarwal, who was appointed as independent director of BHEL with \neffect from 16-11-2005 for a period of 3 years, also happens to be Managing Director of \na Tobacco Manufacturing Company. He has requisite qualification and ex perience \n(B.A., L.L.B.; PGDPM & 1R; Dip. Labour Law and Member of FICC1, PHDCC1 & \nFI1) as necessary for an independent director. He has rich experience of 28 years of \nworking in senior positions in Public and Private Corporate Sector. \nNo independent direct or on the Board of BHEL is presently having any direct links \nwith a competitor of BHEL as per records available with the Company. The names of \nindependent directors of BHEL and their qualifications are as follow: \n1. Shri Sanjay M. Dadlika :     B.Com, Social Worker \n2. Shri Shekhar Datta :     B.E.(Mechanical Engineering),       FIMA & \n Ex-President CII 3. \n3. Shri Manish Gupta :     IAS (Retd.), Ex-Chief Secretary, Govt. of \n West Bengal, B.Sc, Certificate in  Field \nEngineering (Civil, Mechanics & Electrical), \nPost Graduate Certificate in Business  \nManagement \n4. Shri A.K. Aggarwal :    B.A., L.L.B, PGDPM&1R, Diploma  in \nLabour Law, M.D. Gopal Group. \n5. Shri Madhukar :     M.A.(Eco.), L.L.B., Ex-CMD UBI,  & Ex- \n                 Member SEB1 \n \nTechnology for enhancing production from ONGC's ageing oil wells \n \n375. SHRI VIJAY J. DARDA: Will the Minister of PETROLEUM AND \nNATRUAL GAS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government are negotiating for getting technology for improving \nproduction from ONGC's ageing oil wells; \n(b) if so, wh ether Government would ensure that only established technology is \ntaken as normally foreign companies try to pass on either obsolete or innovative \ntechnology;  and \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions     97 \n(c) whether it would be made mandatory for the foreign company to transfer this \ntechnology to ONGC within the period of proposed agreement? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL \nGAS (SHRI DINSHA J. PATEL): (a) New technologies are beneficial for increasing \nproduction from old oil wells producing sub-optimally. \nOil And Natural Gas Corporation Ltd. (ONGC) is implementing proven new \ntechnologies for such wells. Various well techniques like drilling of horizontal and \nmultilateral wells by side -track in old wells, i ntelligent well completion, workover, \nstimulation, hydro -fracturing, acidisation etc. are being implemented to augment oil \nproduction, wherever feasible. \n(b) ONGC inducts only proven equipment and technologies. In case of new  \ntechnology, pilot studies are carried out before full scale implementation. \nIn-house expertise is available in ONGC for evaluating advanced technologies. \nHowever, experts are also hired wherever necessary. \n(c) Engineers and scientists from ONGC are always associated during  \nimplementation of any new technology in order to gain expertise for future  \nimplementation. \nIndo-Iran-Pakistan Pipeline Project \n376. SHRI P.R. RAJAN: Will the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) how much progress has been made in the construction  of the proposed India -\nIran-Pakistan pipeline; and \n(b) by when the final deal may be expected to form up? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL \nGAS (SHRI DINSHA J. PATEL): (a) and (b) India has been pursuing the import of \nnatural gas from Iran through the Iran-Pakistan-India (IPI) transnational gas pipeline. \nSuch multilateral projects involve protracted discussions, as all the aspects have to \nbe carefully examined and deliberated upon to the satisfaction of the participating \ncountries to protect each country's interests as also to avoid any future problems in the \nsuccessful operation of the project. As such, the agreement will only be signed after \nsatisfactory resolution of issues under discussion. \nLoss of Gas bid in Myanmar \n377. SHRI MA HENDRA MOHAN: Will the Minister of PETROLEUM AND \nNATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether is it a fact that India lost the gas bid to China in Myanmar due to spat \nbetween the Ministry of Petroleum and Natural Gas and the Ministry of External \nAffairs; \n(b) if so, what will be its impact on future overseas bidding in the field of oil and \nnatural gas; \n\n98      Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \n(c) whether it is also a fact that the Ministry did not make serious efforts to secure \ngas in Myanmar; \n(d) if so, whether any responsibility has been fixed by the Ministry; and \n(e) if not, the reasons thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL \nGAS (SHRI DINSHA J. PATEL): (a) No, sir \n(b) Does not arise. \n(c) Ministry of Petroleum & Natural Gas keenly pursued the import of natural gas \nfrom Myanmar. Government of Myanmar issued Letter of Intent on February 11, 2004 \nand subsequently signed an MoU on March 9, 2006 during the visit of President of India \nto Myanmar, acknowledging GAIL as a preferential buyer of gas from AI and A3 blocks \nin Myanmar. \nHowever, in August 2006, Myanmar invited bids for selling natural gas from AI and \nA3 offshore blocks. GAIL submitted its bid. Subsequently, Government of Myanmar \nreviewed their decision to sell this gas through the  pipeline route and invited bids for \nsale of 3.5 MMTPA of Liquefied Natural Gas (LNG). GAIL again submitted its bid. The \nmatter was discussed in various meetings held between Government of Myanmar and \nConsortium in January, February and March, 2007. \nIn the  meeting held in February 2007 between Myanmar Government and Petro \nChina, Myanmar Government decided that the gas from Al and A3 blocks would be \nsold to China through the pipeline route. This development was conveyed to the \nconsortium partners by Myanmar Government during a meeting held at Nay Pyi Taw \n(NPT) on March 16, 2007. GAIL impressed upon the other partners and the Myanmar \nGovernment that GAIL's pipeline  offer was still  the most competitive and offered \noptimum value for them due to proximity of Indi a to these fields. However, Myanmar \nGovernment stuck to their decision to sell the gas to China. \n(d) and (e) Do not arise in view of the reply to part (c) above. \nHike in fuel prices \n378. SHRI C. RAMACHANDRAIAH: Will the Minister of PETROLEUM AND \nNATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the oil PSUs, namely Indian Oil Corporation, Bharat Petroleum and \nHindustan Petroleum Corporation Ltd. are losing revenue on account of selling petrol, \ndiesel, LPG and kerosene below the cost price; \n(b) if so, the revenue so far lost during the current fiscal; \n(c) whether Government propose to hike the prices of the fuels to overcome the \nabove  losses; \n(d) if so, the details thereof; and. \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG.. 2007] Unstarred questions     99 \n(e) if not, the details of the steps proposed to be implemented to meet the situation? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL \nGAS (SHRI DINSHA J. PATEL): (a) and (b) Yes, Sir. The estimated gross under \nrecoveries of Public Sector Oil Marketing Companies on sale of s ensitive petroleum \nproducts, i.e. petrol, diesel, PDS Kerosene and domestic LPG for the 1st quarter of \n2007-08 are given as under: \n(Rs. crore)  \nUnder-Recovery* Apr-Jun'07 (Prov) \nPetrol 1,553 \nDiesel 4,633 \nPDS kerosene 4,028 \nDomestic LPG 2,698 \nTotal 12,912 \n*Gross under -recoveries without considering, refinery discount,  oil  bonds and  \nupstream assistance. \n(c) to (e) Government has been taking all possible measures to ensure that there is no \nhardship to common man on account of price rise. Government ad opted the principle of \nequitable burden sharing amongst three stakeholders, namely, the consumers, the PSU \noil companies and the Government to protect the interest of the common man and the \nvulnerable sections of society. The Government is closely monitori ng the international \noil prices and will continue to protect the interests of consumers. \nProfit earned by Oil Marketing Companies \n379. SHRI M.V. MYSURA REDDY: Will the Minister of PETROLEUM AND \nNATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that oil marketing companies i.e. IOC, BPC, HPC, IBP have \nearned profit during the years, 2004-05, 2005-06 and 2006-07 (provisional); and \n(b) if so, the details of profit after deducting tax, company-wise? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL \nGAS (SHRI DINSHA J. PATEL): (a) Yes, Sir. \n(b) The details of the profit after tax earned by public sector Oil Marketing \nCompanies, viz. Indian Oil Corporation Limited (IOC), Hindustan Petroleum \nCorporation Limited (HPCL), Bharat Petroleum Corporation Limi ted (BPCL) and IBP \nCompany Limited (IBP) during the years, 2004- 05, 2005-06 and 2006- 07 are given in \nthe Statement. \n\n100   Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \nStatement \nDetails of Profit Earned by Public Sector Oil Marketing Companies \nProfit earned by public sector Oil Marketing Companies (OMCs), viz. Indian \nOil Corporation Limited (IOC), Hindustan Petroleum Corporation Limited  \n(HPCL), Bharat Petroleum Corporation Limited (BPCL) and IBP Co. Limited \n(IBP) during the years, 2004-05, 2005-06 and 2006-07  \nSI. No. Name of \nthe \nOMCs \n Profit After-Tax \n(Rs. In crores) \n \n 2004-05 2005-06 2006-07 \n1. IOC 4891.38 4915.12 7499.47 \n2. HPCL 1277.33 405.63 1571.17 \n3. BPCL 965.80 291.65** 1805.48** \n4. IBP 58.88 12.44  __ * \n* . Consequent to the merger of I BP Co. Ltd., with Indian Oil Corporation Limited \npursuant to order dated 30th April, 2007 and completion of merger formalities \neffective 2nd May, 2007, IBP financials has been merged with Indian Oil \nCorporation Limited for the year 2006- 07 and hence there is no separate \nprofitability reported for IBP Co. Ltd., for 2006-07. \n** Pursuant to approval received from Ministry of Company Affairs to the Scheme \nof Amalgamation of Kochi Refinery Limited (KRL) with the BPCL, the \nPerformance of 2005-06 and 2006-07 of BPCL includes  KRL also. \nTheft of oil from ONGC Pipeline \n380. SHRI MAHMOODA. MADANI: \nSHRI SANTOSH BAGRODIA: \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government have taken note of the media reports showing theft of oil \nfrom the ONGC pipeline in Gujarat; \n(b) the steps being taken by Government beyond taking up the issue with the \nconcerned State Government; \n(c) whether it is a fact that Government have had information of such activities \never.since 2005; \n(d) whether it is also a fact that t he safety of the pipeline is the responsibility of the \ncentral security forces; and \n(e) whether Government Uave ascertained the responsibility of security agencies in \nthe breach of the safety of the pipeline? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL \nGAS (SHRI DINSHA J. PATEL): (a) Yes sir. \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions   101 \n(b) Oil and Naural Gas Corporation Limited (ONGC) has recently undertaken a \nreview of crime situation, especially issue related to theft and pilf erage in Gujarat, and \nhas prepared an action plan to curb the theft/pilferage in the State. Recently, a \nMemorandum of Understanding (MoU) has been signed between the Government of \nGujarat and ONGC for deployment of one Battalion of State Reserve Police (SR P), \nexclusively for the protection of ONGC installations and pipelines. \n(c) ONGC keep a strict vigil on safety and security of oil pipelines. \n(d) No Sir, Safety of the pipeline is the responsibility of the ONGC and the State \nGovernment. However, wherever required, in addition to State Police and CISF, Central \nForces have also been deployed. \n(e) All cases of theft are reported to jurisdictional Police stations and  \ninvestigations are undertaken. \nForeign Capital Investment in Public Sector Oil Refineries \n†381. SHRI RAJ MOHINDER SINGH MAJITHA: \nDR. MURLI MANOHAR JOSHI: \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that there is a proposal before Government for approval of 49 \nper cent of foreign capital investment in the public sector refineries; \n(b) if so, from where this proposal has come and the reasons for its acceptance; \n(c) the percentage of foreign capital investment allowed in this area in the country at \npresent; and \n(d) whether paucity of domestic capital investment is being felt in the field of oil \nrefinery in the country? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL \nGAS (SHRI DINSHA J. PATEL): (a) and (b) Foreign Direct Investment (FDI) Policy \nincluding policy for Petroleum and Natural Gas Sector, is reviewed on a continuous \nbasis. No general decision to change the extant FDI policy for public sector oil \nrefineries has been taken. \n(c) In terms of Press Note No.4 (2006) Series dated 10.02.2006 of Department of \nIndustrial Policy and Promotion, Ministry of Commerce and Industry, for the refining \nsector, FDI up to 26% in case of Public Sector Undertakings is permissible through \nForeign Investment Promotion Board. However, FDI up to 100% in the refining sector \nis permitted through the automatic route in case of private companies. \n(d) Refining sector in India has seen significant addition to capacity involving \nprimary domestic capital infusion in recent times, both in the private and public sector. \nIn a liberalized policy regime, however, the entrepreneurs are competent to take \nindependent commercial decisions on sourcing either domestic or foreign capital for \nsetting up and expanding refining activity. \n†Original notice of the, question was received in Hindi \n\n102   Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \nLNG import from Iran \n382. SHRI AMAR SINGH: \nSHRI ABU ASIM AZMI: \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that Government have signed a 22 billion  dollar deal to \nimport 5 million  tonnes of LNG from Iran in 2005; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) whether the present Government in Iran have refused to honour that  \nagreement and if so, the reasons therefor? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL \nGAS (SHRI DINSHA J. PATEL): (a) to (c) A Sale -Purchase Agree ment (SPA) was \nsigned on June 13, 2005 in Tehran for 5 million  metric tonnes per annum (MMTPA) \nof LNG between the National Iranian Gas Export Company (N1GEC) and the Indian \nConsortium of GAIL (India) Limited (GAIL), Indian Oil Corporation (IOC) & Bharat \nPetroleum Corporation Limited (BPCL). The contract period was for 25 years, \nbeginning from the last quarter of 2009. Simultaneously, the parties also signed a side \nletter to the LNG SPA on June 13, 2005, by which NIGEC had to obtain the approval of \ntheir parent company, National Iranian Oil Company (NIOC), for the SPA to become \neffective. NIGEC has not conveyed the approval of NIOC's Board till now. \nMatter is being consistently pursued with Iran through written communications as \nwell as personal discussions. During the visit  of Minister (P&NG) to Iran during April \n25-26, 2007, the issue was discussed and the Iranian President assured the Indian \ndelegation that Iran is committed to the LNG contract. \nAPM of natural gas \n383. SHRI MOHAMMED AMIN: \nSHRI TAPAN KUMAR SEN: \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n(a) the methodology of determining Administrative Price of natural gas; \n(b) the minimum and maximum return on investment envisaged in the above \nAdministrative Pricing Mechanism (APM); and \n(c) the return on investment assessed for RIL -NIKKO's K.G -D6 block with the \nproposed base gas price 4.33 dollar/million btu? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL \nGAS (SHRI DINSHA J. PATEL): (a) and (b) The determination of producer price of gas \nproduced from fields given to ONGC and OIL on nomination basis is done taking into \naccount the investments made for development of infrastructure, including the cost \nincurred on exploration, as also the market conditions, including the price of alte rnative \nfuels and price of natural gas from alternate source, such as JV, LNG, etc. The issue has \nbeen examined by various Committees appointed by the Government \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions   103 \nfrom time to time. 15% post tax return on net worth has usually been considered for \ndetermination of price of APM gas. \n(c) The RIL  and NIKKO K.G- D6 block project was assessed for development and \nproduction of gas in Dhirubhai -I and Dhirubhai -III discoveries. The Net Present Value \n(NPV) o f the project was calculated using a discounting rate of 10% and NPV was \nfound to be positive. This standard procedure is being followed for NPV calculation of \nall development plans under the Production Sharing Contracts of the New Exploration \nLicensing Policy (NELP). \nSale of holding in OIL \n384. SHRI TAPAN KUMAR SEN: \nSHRI MOHAMMED AMIN: \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n(a) the reserve and surplus of Oil India Ltd. (OIL); \n(b) the debt equity ratio of OIL; \n(c) the capital investment of OIL during last three years; \n(d) whether Government are planning to sell 10 per cent of holding in Oil; and  \n(e) If so, the reasons therefor? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL \nGAS (SHRI DINSHA J. PATEL): (a) The reserve and surplus of O il India Ltd. (OIL) \nas on 31 st March, 2007 was Rs. 6,635.07 crore. \n(b) The debt equity ratio of OIL as on 31.3.2007 was 1:8.41. \n(c) The capital investment by OIL all during the last three years is as under:- \n(Rs in crore)  \n2006-07 2005-06 2004-05 \n7,141.22 5,916.79 5,196.13 \n(d) Yes, Sir. \n(e) It would enable the company to get its shares listed on the stock exchanges and \nwould also help them raise resources for growth and expansion. \nImport of LPG \n385. SHRI TAPAN KUMAR SEN: \nSHRI MOHAMMED AMIN: \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n(a) the present domestic demand of LPG per annum; \n(b) the present domestic produ tion of LPG by oil companies, both privare and \npublic, per annum; \n\n104   Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \n(c) the import of LPG, if any, during last three years; \n(d) the import envisaged during the years, 2007-08 and 2008-09; and \n(e) the action plan to reduce import of LPG? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL \nGAS (SHRI DINSHA J. PATEL): (a) The domestic demand of  LPG during, 2006- 07 \nwas 9750.0 TMT (provisional). \n(b) The domestic production of LPG by oil companies, both private and public, \nduring 2006-07 was 8454 TMT (provisional). \n(c) The import of LPG during the last three years is as under: \nYear TMT \n2006-07 (Prov.) 2288 \n2005-06 2883 \n2004-05 — 2334 \n(d) The import envisaged during 2007-08 and 2008-09 is as under: \nYear TMT \n2007-08 1730 \n2008-09 1550 \n(e) In order to reduce the import of LPG, the domestic production of LPG in the  \ncountry is sought to be increased through refin ery expansion/modernization plans,  \nimplementation of LPG maximization project and enhanced capacity utilization and  \nalso through supply of Piped Natural Gas (PNG) to rationalize demand for LPG. The  \nWorking Group on Petroleum and Natural Gas Sector for the Eleventh Plan has  \nprojected reduction in deficit of LPG availability to 100 TMT by the terminal year of  \nEleventh Plan, i.e. 2011-12 from the current level of 2288 TMT in 2006-07. \nConsumption of Natural Gas \n386. SHRI MOHAMMED AM1N: \nSHRI TAPAN KUMAR SEN: \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n(a) the base price of Natural Gas in dollar/million btu in the following areas/sector \nfor North East and for Fertilizer and Power under Administrative Pricing Mechanism \n(APM); \n(b) the total gas consumed in the above areas/sector in the last three years; and \n(c) the annual consumption of natural gas during the last three years? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL \nGAS (SHRI DINSHA J. PATEL): (a) The basic price of gas supplied und er the \nAdministrative Price Mechanism (APM) in the North East to consumers of Power and \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions   105 \nFertilizers sector is Rs. 1920 per Metric Standard Cubic Metre (MSCM). The basic price \nof gas for small sc ale consumers, i.e., those consuming below 50,000 Standard Cubic \nMetres per day, and court mandated consumers in the North -East is Rs. 2304/ MSCM. \nAt an exchange rate of Rs. 40.42 = 1 US $, these rates would be US $ 1.2/ MMBTU and \nUS $ 1.44/MMBTU respectiv ely. The basic price of gas for Fertilizers and Power \nsectors for the rest of the country is Rs. 3200/MSCM, which is equivalent to US $ \n2.00/MMBTU at the mentioned exchange rate. The mentioned gas price is for Net \nCalorific Value (NCV) of 10,000 K. cal/SCM. \n(b) The APM gas consumed in the North-East in the last three years is as follows: \n(in MMSCMD)  \n2004-05 2005-06 2006-07 \n5.522 6.001 6.343 \nThe APM gas consumed in the Fertilizers and Power sectors in the last three years is \nas follows: \n(in MMSCMD)  \n2004-05 2005-06 2006-07 \n45.09 41.68 41.13 \n(c) The annual consumption of natural gas in the country during the last three years is \nas follows: \n(in MMSCMD)  \n2004-05 2005-06 2006-07 \n84.3 85.0 96.7 \nPrice fixation of gas \n†387.  SHRI RAM JETHMALANI: \nSHRI RAJ MOHINDER SINGH MAJITHA: \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that fixation of selling price of gas has become a matter  \nof dispute in the country; \n(b) if so, the facts thereof and the views of the gas sellers and the gas buyers on price \nfixation of gas; and \n(c) whether it is also a fact that in the absence of well considered basis for selling \nprice of gas, the sellers and also the buyers are feeling cheated themselves if not, the \nreaction of the Government thereto? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL \nGAS (SHRI DINSHA J. PATEL): (a) to (c) The average gas supply in the country \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\n106   Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \nduring, 2006-07 was 96.71 MMSCMD. Out of this, around 53 MMSCMD is APM gas. \nAPM gas outside the North East is sold at Rs. 3200/MSCM for power and fertilizers \nsector consumers, and at Rs. 3840/MSCM for small scale consumers, i.e., those \nconsuming below 50,000 standard cubic metres per day, and court -mandated customers. \nAPM gas for customers in the North East is sold at Rs. 1920/MSCM for power and \nfertilizers sector consumers and at Rs. 2304/MSCM for small scale consumers and \ncourt-mandated customers. Producers of gas, viz., ONGC and OIL, have been \ndemanding higher price for APM gas, considering various factors like cost of \nproduction, prices of alternate fuels, prices of natural gas from alternative sources, such \nas JV gas, LNG, etc. \nGas produced by pre -NELP JVs is sold under the provisions of the concerned \nProduction Sharing Contracts (PSCs); this includes gas from Panna -Mukta and Tapti \n(PMT) Joint Venture (JV), Ravva JV and Ravva Satellite. The producers of Ravva \nSatellite demanded a higher price in 2006, a s provided for under the Gas Sales \nAgreement (GSA). The consumers of gas, especially lPPs in Andhra Pradesh, demanded \nthat the price should not be increased beyond the then prevailing price of US$ \n3.3/MMBTU. Finally, GAIL negotiated a price of US$ 4.40/MMBTU (including GAIL's \nmarketing margin). \nPrice of Liquefied Natural Gas (LNG) imported under term contracts is governed by \nthe Sale Purchase Agreement (SPA) between the LNG seller and the buyer. In order to \nmake the price of RLNG affordable, the Government decided to pool the prices of LNG \nprocured under different terms contracts at different prices, to supply RLNG on a non -\ndiscriminatory basis to all existing and new consumers. Some of the existing consumers, \nwho have been getting RLNG at lower rates, have contested this decision in the Courts. \nRegarding price of gas from fields under the New Exploration Licensing Policy \n(NELP), the producers of gas are demanding a market determined price under the \nprovisions of PSC, while the consumers have requested gas to be made available at \nlesser rates. The Government is considering the constitution of an Empowered Group of \nMinisters (EGoM) to deliberate on the issue. \nPetroleum and Natural Gas Regulatory Board \n†388.  DR. MURLI MANOHAR JOSHI \nSHRI RAM JETHMALANI \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be please to state: \n \n(a) whether it is a fact that it had been decided to establish Petroleum and Natural \nGas Regulatory Board in March, 2006 in the country; \n(b) if not, when this decision was taken; and  \n(c) the reasons for not implementing the above decision till now?: \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL \nGAS (SHRI DINSHA J. PATEL): (a) Yes, Sir. The Petroleum and Natural Gas \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions   107 \nRegulatory Board ( PNGRB) has been set up w.e.f. 25.06.2007 under the PNGRB Act, \n2006. \n(b) to (c) Do not arise in view of (a) above. \nDetails of prices of gas \n389. SHRI PRASANTA CHATTERJEE: Will the Minister  of PETROLEUM AND \nNATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n(a) the basic price of gas supplied under administrative price mechanism in North \nEast and North West areas; \n(b) the basic price of gas supplied by NELP blocks, joint ventures and private \nproducers; and \n(c) the average price of imported LPG? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL \nGAS (SHRI DINSHA J. PATEL): (a) The basic price of gas supplied under the \nAdministrative Price Mechanism (APM) in the North East to consumers of Power and \nFertilizers sector is Rs. 1920 per Metric Standard Cubic Metre (MSCM). The basic price \nof gas for small scale consumers, i.e., those consuming below 50,000 Standard Cubic \nMetres per day, and court mandated consumers in the North -East is Rs. 2304/ MSCM. \nThe basic price of gas for the rest of the country, including North West areas, is Rs. \n3200/MSCM for Power and Fertilizers sector consumers and Rs. 3840/ - MSCM for \nsmall scale consumers and court mandated consumers. The mentioned gas price is for \nNet Calorific Value (NCV) of 10,000 K. cal/SCM. \n(b) Prevailing gas prices under NELP blocks/Joint Ventures/Private Producers are as \nunder:  \nField Gas price \nPanna-Mukta and Tapti 3.96, 4.75 and 5.58 US $ per \nMMBTU \nRavva Field 3.50 US $ per MMBTU \nRavva Satellite 4.30 US $ per MMBTU \nHazira 3.24 US $ per MMBTU \nCB OS/2 3.68 and 4.45 US $ per \nMMBTU \nDholka Rs. 2 per SCM \nCB ONN 2000/2 (NSA, \nBheema) \n3.45 US $/1000 cubic feet \n(c) The average price of imported LPG for the period April -June, 2007 was Rs. \n25,489/MT \n\n108   Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \nGreen belt land for oil refinery \n390. SHRI THENNALA G. BALAKRISHNA PILLAI: Will the Minister of \nPETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that every oil refinery requires a Green belt land; \n(b) if so, whether all the oil refineries in India have fulfilled this specification; and  \n(c) if any oil refinary has not fulfilled this specification what action would be taken?  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL \nGAS (SHRI DINSHA J. PATEL): \n(a) to (c) Mandatory acquisition of Green belt has been notified by Central Pollution \nControl Board under Environment Protection (EP) Act, 1986. The refineries, which have \nbeen established after promulgation of this Act, are obliged to fulfill this requirement. \nThe refineries commissioned after 1986 have provided for Green belt in line with the \nstipulations laid down in the EP Act, 1986. \nThe above Act is not obligatory to refineries established prior to EP Act, 1986. \nHowever, these refineries have undertaken tree plantation surrounding the refineries to \nmitigate environmental emissions. \nPrice rise of petrol and diesel \nt391. SHRI BANWARI LAL KANCHHAL: Will the Minister of PETROLEUM AND \nNATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n(a) the extent of rise in prices of petrol and diesel during the last three years; \n(b) whether Government have any plan to control ever increasing prices; and \n(c) if so, the details thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL \nGAS (SHRI DINSHA J. PATEL): (a) The major price re visions carried out for petrol \nand diesel during the last three years are as under:  \n Retail se slling Price at Delhi \n(Rs./litre) \n Petrol  Diesel \n1 2  5 \nPrice as on 01.04.04 33.70  21.73 \nRevisions thereafter    \n16.06.04 35.71  22.74 \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions   109  \n1  2 3 \n01.08.04  36.81 24.16 \n05.11.04  39.00 26.28 \n16.11.04  37.84 - \n21.06.05  40.49 28.45 \n07.09.05  43.49 30.45 \n06.06.06  47.51 32.47 \n30.11.06  44.85 31.25 \n16.02.07  42.85 30.25 \nCurrent price (price revised 43.52 30.48 \non account of VAT revision)   \n(Price revisions on account of VAT/sales tax, revision in siding/shunting charges not \nconsidered) \n(b) and (c) Government has been taking all  possible measures to ensure that there is \nno hardship to common man on account of price rise. Government adopted the principle \nof equitable burden sharing amongst three stakeholders, namely, the consumers, the \nPSU oil companies and the Government to protect the int erest of the common man and \nthe vulnerable sections of society. Government is closely monitoring the international \noil prices and will continue to protect the interests of consumers. \nGas from Myanmar \n392. SHRI R. SHUNMUGASUNDARAM: Will the-Minister of PETROLEUM AND \nNATURAL GAS be pleased to state; \n(a) whether the Ministry has failed to take adequate efforts to bid for gas exported \nfrom Blocks 41 and 43 of Myanmar; \n(b) if so, how much gas India failed to import from Myanmar; and \n(c) whether any further bid will be made to import gas from Myanmar? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL \nGAS (SHRI DINSHA J. PATEL): (a) and (b) Ministry of Petroleum & Natural Gas has \nkeenly pursued the import of natural gas from Myanmar. Government of Myanmar \nissued a  Letter of Intent on February 11,2004 and subsequently signed an MoU on \nMarch 9, 2006 during the visit of President of India to Myanmar, acknowledging GAIL \nas a preferential buyer of gas from Al and A3 blocks in Myanmar. \nHowever, in August 2006, Myanmar invited bids for selling 15.8MMSCMD natural \ngas from Al and A3 offshore blocks through the pipeline route. GAIL submitted its bid. \nSubsequently, Government of Myanmar reviewed their decision to sell this gas through \nthe pipeline route and invited bids for sa le of 3.5 MMTPA of Liquefied Natural Gas \n(LNG). GAIL again submitted its bid. The matter was discussed in various meetings \nheld between Government of Myanmar -and Consortium in January, February and \nMarch, 2007. \n\n110   Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \nIn the meeting held in February 2007 between Myanmar Government and Petro \nChina, Myanmar Government decided that the gas from Al and A3 blocks would be sold \nto China through the pipeline route. This development was conveyed to the consortium  \npartners by Myanmar Government during a meeting held at Nay Pyi Taw (NPT) on \nMarch 16, 2007. GAIL impressed upon the other partners and Myanmar Government \nthat GAIL's pipeline offer was still  the most competitive and offered optimum value for \nthem due to proximity of India to these fields. However, Myanmar Government stuck to \ntheir decision to sell the gas to China. \n(c) In order to achieve energy security for the country, India will continue to make \nefforts for import of natural gas/LNG from all  possible sources, including Myanmar. \nIndo-Pak-Iran gas pipeline project \n393. SHRI SYED AZEEZ PASHA: Will the Minister of PETROLEUM AND \nNATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n(a) at what stage is the talks on building an overland pipeline to convey natural gas \nfrom Iran to India through Pakistan; \n(b) whether Iran has proposed that the Prime Minister of India and the President of \nPakistan should travel to Tehran to sign the final agreement with President of Iran later \nthis year; and \n(c) if so, the details thereof and Government's reaction thereto? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL \nGAS (SHRI DINSHA J. PATEL): (a) India has been pursuing the import of natural gas \nfrom Iran through the Iran-Pakistan-India (IP1) transnational gas pipeline.  \n(b) No, Sir. \n(C) Does not arise. \nLPG connection in Kerala \n394. SHRI K..E. ISMAIL: Will the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) the details of the application for new LPG connection pending with various oil \ncompanies in Kerala, district-wise as on June 30,2007; \n(b) the measures being taken to clear these pending applications; \n(c) whether it is a fact that the application for second cylinder is not being \nentertained by oil companies in the State for a long time; and \n(d) if so, the reasons therefor and by when such reque sts are expected to be \nentertained? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL \nGAS (SHRI DINSHA J. PATEL): (a) Indian Oil Corporation Limited (IOC) has reported \na waiting list of 31974 in the State of Kerala as on 30.6.2007 for release of new LPG \nconnections with their LPG distributors due to inadequate supply of cylinders by the  \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG.. 2007] Unstarred questions   111 \ncylinder manufacturers. The District-wise details as on 30.6.2007 is given in the \nStatement (See below) \n(b) Intimation letters have been sent to all the waitlisted persons as on 30.6.2007 for \navailing new LPG connections. Advertisements have also been released in local and \nnational newspapers of Kerala advising the public that they should approach the \ndistributors for availing the same. Further, Government has advised IOC to make \nalternate arrangements for supply of cylinders through other suppliers on a priority \nbasis. The waiting list for new connections has since reduced to 21347 as on 1.8.2007.  \n(c) No, Sir. \n(d) Does not arise in view of (c) above \nStatement \nRelease of the district-wise details of the application for new LPG connection  \nSI. No. District Applicants  \n1. Allepy 955  \n2. Calicut 2211  \n3. Ernakulam 4447  \n4. Idukki 25  \n5. Kannur 3088  \n6. Kasargode 258  \n7. Kollam 7686  \n8. Kottayam 0  \n9. Malappuram 1173  \n10. Palakkad 976  \n11. Pathanamthitta 0  \n12. Trichur 3655  \n13. Trivandrum 7122  \n14. Wayanad 378  \n TOTAL: 31974  \nUS pressure against agreements with Iran \n395. SHRI DEVDAS APTE: Will the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL \nGAS be pleased to state: \n   (a) whether Government has decided to sign the Framework Agreement and Gas \nSales and Purchase Agreement with the Government of Iran; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the US Governments has warned Government against this agreement; \n\n112   Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \n(d) if so, the details thereof; and \n(e) the steps Government would take to protect the energy needs of the country?  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL \nGAS (SHRI DINSHA J. PATEL): (a) and (b) India has been pursuing the import of \nnatural gas from Iran through the Iran-Pakistan- India (IPI) transnational gas pipeline. \n(c) and (d) During visit of US Secretary of State Dr. Condoleezza Rice to India in \nMarch 2005, she informed the media that the US had conveyed its concerns on the \nproposed gas pipeline cooperation between India and Iran. The US legal position on this \nissue flows from its Iran - Libya Sanctions Act (ILSA) of 1996 that provides for the \nimposition of sanctions against foreign companies that make an investment of more than \nUS $ 20 million in one year in Iran's energy sector. \n(e) Government is pursuing the import of natural gas from Iran in national interest in \norder to meet the energy requirements of the country. \nProduction in ONGC's Assam fields \n396. SHRI B.K. HARIPRASAD: Will the Minister of PETROLEUM AND \nNATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the crude production from the ONGC's Assam fields are showing a \ndeclining trend; \n(b) whether ONGC h as firmed up a contract with US -based Haliburton for \ndeployment of cutting edge technology used worldwide, to enhance crude production \nfrom its Assam fields; and \n(c) whether ONGC has weighed the negative aspects of insurgency/militancy \nprevailing in the region while making additional investment in Assam fields? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL \nGAS (SHRI DINSHA J. PATEL): (a) Yes, Sir \nHowever, continuous efforts are being made by Oil & Natural Gas Corporation Ltd. \n(ONGC) to maintain and enhance the production. \n(b) Yes, Sir \nThe contract for technical study, technological induction and operational services \ndelivery has been signed between Assam Asset of ONGC and Halliburton Offshore \nServices Inc. (HOSI) during April, 2007 for producti on optimization, reservoir \nmanagement activities and to enhance the oil production in major oil fields of Assam \nAsset. \n(c) ONGC has been producing oil in Assam for the last forty years. Environmental  \nproblems are taken up at different levels of State Govt.  and resolved with joint  \ndiscussions/actions. \nHowever, ONGC is concentrating all its resources to maintain and augment \nproduction from Assam fields. . \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions   113 \nONGC's refinery in Rajasthan \n397. SHRI RAMD AS AGARWAL: Will the Minister of PETROLEUM AND  \nNATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether ONGC and the State Government of Rajasthan are at loggerheads over \nphysical incentives to set up a $ 3 billion refinery in Rajasthan; \n(b) if so, how much fiscal support ONG C has sought from the State Government to \nmake the fefinery project viable; and \n(c) the stand taken by the State Government of Rajasthan in this matter and by when \nthe above project is likely to start work? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL \nGAS (SHRI DINSHA J. PATEL): (a) to (c) ONGC/MRPL and a delegation from the \nGovernment of Rajasthan's have interacted and held discussions on the subject in June, \n2007. ONGC has intimated that they are exploring feasibility and doing due diligence  \nfor setting up an optimum capacity well head refinery in Rajasthan with due facilitation \nfrom the Government of. Rajasthan. ONGC has informed that fiscal incentives of the \norder of Rs. 1300 crore per annum for 15 years would be needed to make the 7.5 \nMMTPA project viable. \nThe matter is further being discussed between ONGC and the State Government of \nRajasthan. \nONGC is continuing optimisation studies for improving techno-commercial viability \nof refinery. \nCNG stations in metros \n398. SHRIMATI PREMA CARIAPPA: Will the Minister of PETROLEUM AND  \nNATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether there are adequate number of CNG stations in all the metros; \n(b) if not, the reasons therefor; and \n(c) by when the same would be set up in all the metros? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE PN THE MTNISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL \nGAS (SHRI DINSHA J. PATEL): (a) and (b) There are 127 CNG stations in Mumbai \n(including in Thane and Mira Bhayandar). There are 153 CNG filling stations in Delhi \nincluding 1 station in Noida. Presently, there are three CNG stations in Hyderabad. \nCNG facility is not being provided in other metros as natural gas is still not available \nthere. More CNG filling stations would be opened in Delhi and Mumbai, depending \nupon demand and economic viability. Regarding Hyderabad, CNG is pre sently being \ntransported through mobile cascades from Vijaywada, which is about 275 km away; \nhence opening of new CNG filling stations in Hyderabad would also depend upon \navailability of gas. \n(c) The expansion of CNG infrastructure in different metros in t he country is being \ntaken up in a phased manner, depending upon availability of gas and economic viability.  \n\n114   Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \nAmalgamation of Indian Oil and IBP \n†399. SHRI LAL1T KISHORE CHATURVEDI: Will the Minister of PETROLEUM \nAND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the amalgamation process of Indian Oil and IBP has been completed; \n(b) if not, the obstacles therein and reasons therefor; \n(c) the time-frame of this amalgamation and by when it would be completed; and \n(d) whether this amalgamation would result in loss of net worth of Indian oil and if \nso, the quantum of loss to Government? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL \nGAS (SHRI DINSHA J. PATEL): (a) to (c) The process of Amalgamation of IBP C o. \nLimited (IBP) with Indian Oil Corporation Limited (IOC) has been completed and the \nMinistry of Company Affairs has accorded sanction to the scheme of amalgamation on \n30.4.2007. The amalgamation became effective on 2.5.2007 with the filing of the copies \nof the order by both the companies with the Registrar of Companies at Mumbai and \nKolkata. \n(d) IOC has reported that there is no loss to the Government of India due to the \namalgamation of IBP with IOC except for the marginal reduction in the percentage of \nGovernment of India's (GoI) holding in IOC from 82.03% to 80.35% due to issuance of \nshares to IBP shareholders in the approved swap ratio of 110:100. There is no change in \nthe number of shares held by Gol consequent to the merger. \nDiesel subsidy to farmers of Haryana and Punjab \n†400. SHRI AJAY SINGH CHAUTALA: Will the Minister of PETROLEUM AND \nNATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether there is any proposal under consideration of Government to provide  \ndiesel at low rates or provide subsidy on diesel to far mers of Haryana and Punjab,  \nwhich are primarily foodgrain producing States; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL \nGAS (SHRI DINSHA J. PATEL): (a) to (c) No, Sir. Diesel is already being sold across \nthe country at price lower than international price. Custom's Duty on Diesel has been \nreduced to 7.5% from 10% with effect from 14.6.2006. Price of diesel was reduced on \n30.11.2006 and again on 16.2.2007. The ad-valorem excise duty component on diesel \nhas been reduced from 8% to 6% in the Union Budget, 2007-08. \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions   115 \nInvestment in Mumbai High oilfields \n401. SHRI SHANTA RAM LAXMAN NAIK: Will the Minister of PETROLEUM \nAND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Oil and Natural Gas Corporation has any plan to re- develop its prime \nMumbai High Oil fields to arrest production decline from the ageing fields; \n(b) if so, the quantum of investment proposed to be made for the purpose; and \n(c) the quantum of additional oil expected on account of the investment? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL \nGAS (SHRI DINSHA J PATEL): (a) To arrest the oil production decline in Mumbai \nHigh field, major initiatives in the form of redevelopment programme were being \nlaunched by Oil & Natural Gas Corporation Ltd. (ONGC) in 2000- 01. These projects \nare redevelopment projects of Mumbai High North (MHN) and Mumbai High South \n(MHS). The inputs envisaged under these projects have been completed by April 2007. \nThe implementation of these projects has successfully arrested the production decline \nfrom Mumbai High field. \nA project for Addittional development of Al Layer of L -III reservoir of  Mumbai \nHigh North is currently under implementation and will be completed during 2007- 08. \nIn order to maintain the high level of production and further improve the oil recovery \nfrom Mumbai High field, the feasibility study for next phase of re -development program \nis being carried out by ONGC. \n(b) and (c) The quantum of investment proposed to be made for next phase of \nredevelopment programme and the quantum of additional oil expected would depend on \nfeasibility study. \nIndia-Pakistan-Iran gas pipeline agreement \n†402. SHRI DATTA MEGHE: Will the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL \nGAS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether gas pipeline agreement has been finalized among India, Pakistan and \nIran; \n(b) if not, the hurdles being faced in finalizing this agreement; \n(c) the efforts bein g made to solve the problems being faced in finalizing this \nagreements; and \n(d) by when the country is likely to get gas after finalizing of this agreement? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL \nGAS (SHRI DINSHA J. PATEL): (a) No, Sir. \n(b) Such multilateral projects involve protracted discussions, as all the aspects have \nto be carefully examined and deliberated upon to the satisfaction of the participating \ncountries to protect each country's interests as also to avoid any future problem s in the \nsuccessful operation of the project. \n†Original noiice of the question was received in Hindi \n\n116   Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \n(c) India has been pursuing the import of natural gas from Iran through the Iran -\nPakistan-India (IPI) transnational gas pipeline. \n(d) Apart from other issues, the schedule of gas import from the proposed pipeline is \nalso under discussion. \nDevelopment of tourism in Rajasthan \n†403. DR. PRABHA THAKUR: Will the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Central Government have prepared any outlines for development of \ndifferent tourist places in Rajasthan; \n(b) whether the Central Government have any scheme regarding development of \nnew tourist places alongwith old tourist places, if so, the nature of such scheme; and \n(c) the names of districts in Rajasthan for which the proposal of development of \ndifferent tourist places have been received by the Central Government from the State \nGovernment and out of them the names of tourist places for which the Central \nGovernment have prepared development schemes and the target fixed to complete those \nschemes alongwith the details thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM (SHRIMATI AMBIKA SONI): (a) and (b) \nDevelopment of tourist places in the country is undertaken primarily by the Stat e \nGovernments/UT Administrations. The Ministry of Tourism, Government of India, \nextends financial assistance to the State Governments/UT Administrations for tourism \nrelated projects under the schemes for Product/Infrastructure Development of \nDestinations/Circuits and Assistance to Large Revenue Generating Projects. \n(c) A project 'Floodlighting of monuments in Rajasthan' has been sanctioned for Rs. \n800.00 lakh in the current financial year. This project covers 28 monuments spread \nacross different districts s uch as Jaipur, Karauli, Udaipur, Ajmer, Dausa, Chum, \nDholpur, Madhopur, Jaisalmer, Bharatpur, Jhalawar, Bikaner and Kota. \nImplementation of projects is the responsibility of the State Government. Ministry \nof Tourism, Government of India monitors the progre ss of the projects from time to \ntime. \nPromotion of tourism in Punjab \n404. SHRI VARINDER SINGH BAJWA: Will the Minister of TOURISM be pleased \nto state: \n(a) whether any proposals/suggestions made by the State Government of Punjab for \nthe promotion of tourism in that State are presently pending with the Central \nGovernment for approval/clearance; \n(b) if so, the details thereof, item-wise; and \n(c) by when, they are likely to be approved/cleared? \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions   117 \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM (SHRIMATI AMBIKA SONI): (a) No project \nproposals have been received from the State Government of Punjab in the current \nFinancial Year. \n(b) and (c ) Do not arise. \nAccess of tourists in North-East \n405. SHRI S.S. AHLUWALIA: Will the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that due to restrictions, tourists are not allowed to access  \nseveral States in the north-eastern India; \n(b) if so, the details thereof indicating principa l reasons for imposing such \nrestrictions; \n(c) whether Government have decided to promote tourism in the North -East by \nlifting curbs in restricted areas for tourists; \n(d) if so, the details thereof; and \n(e) the basis, on which, the curbs are being lifted? \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM (SHRIMATI AMBIKA SONI): (a) Yes, Sir. \n(b) These restrictions are on account of various reasons such as proximity to \ninternational borders, law and order situation etc. \n(c) to (e) The review of restrictions is a continuous process and is undertaken by th e \nMinistry of Home Affairs on receipt of proposals from the concerned States and in \nconsultation with the security agencies. \nHeritage places in Rajasthan \n†406. SHRI LALIT KISHORE CHATURVEDI: Will the Minister of TOURISM be \npleased to state: \n(a) the action bein g taken by the Central Government at its own level or in \ncollaboration with the State Government towards the development of tourist and \nheritage places in Rajasthan; and \n(b) the details of amount spent during the last three years in this regard, year-wise? \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM (SHRIMATI AMBIKA SONI): (a) Development \nand promotion of tourism is undertaken primarily by the State Governments/UT \nAdministrations. The Ministry of Tourism, Government of India, extends financial \nassistance to the State Governments/U T Administrations for tourism related projects \nwhich are identified in consultation and interaction with them under schemes for \nProduct/Infrastructure Development of Destinations/Circuits and Assistance to Large \nRevenue Generating Projects. \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi \n\n118   Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \nProject proposals received from the State Governments/Union Territories that are \ncomplete in all respects are approved on the basis of inter -se priority and funds released \nsubject to availability under respective heads. \nA project 'Floodlighting of monuments in Rajasthan' has been sanctioned for  \nRs.800.00 lakh in the current financial year.  \n(b) Details of CFA released for development of tourism heritage place s in Rajasthan \nyear-wise during the last three years is given below: \n(Rs. in lakh)  \nYear Amount \n2006-07 953.84 \n2005-06 2099.69 \n2004-05 2519.06 \nCruise Tourism \n†407. SHRI DATTA MEGHE: Will the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government have formulated any scheme to promote tourism in the \ncountry by identifying some of the ports as cruise tourism centres in the coastal areas of \nIndia; \n(b) if so, the names of such ports; and \n(c) by when these cruise tourism centres would be developed? \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM (SHRIMATI AMBIKA SONI): (a) Ministry of \nTourism promotes Cruise Tourism in India through India tourism offices in domestic \nand overseas markets. \n(b) and (c) The draft Cruise Shipping Policy of the Ministry of Shipping provides for \ndevelopment of cruise facilities in ports in a phased manner. \nNew tourism policy \n†408. SHRI JAI PARKASH AGGARWAL: Will the Minister of TOURISM be \npleased to state: \n(a) the details of those State Governments including Delhi Government which have \nformulated new tourism policy  to develop tourist spots and to attract large number of \nforeign tourists; \n(b) whether Government have approved the above policy. State-wise; and \n(c) if so, the details thereof? \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions   119 \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM (SHRIMATI AMBIKA SONI): The information is \nbeing collected and will be laid on the Table of the Sabha. \nReligious tourism in Orissa \n409.  SHRI B. J. PANDA: \nMS. PRAMILA BOHIDAR: \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government are aware that Orissa is the cultural and religious heritage  \ncentre of Buddhism and sea -route is a convenient mode of travel for South- East \nAsian people to visit the large numbers of ancient Buddhist temples  located in the  \nState; \n(b) whether to boost religious tourism in the State, there is an urgent need to \ndevelop auxiliary infrastructure i.e. roads etc., which are in bad shape; and \n(c) the action plan of the centre to help the cash -strapped State to develop the ro ad \ninfrastructure to boost religious tourism in the State with a view to improve its \neconomy? \n