Nagar district of \nUttar Pradesh is under consideration of Government; and \n(b) if so, the details of action taken in this regard? \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM (SHRIMATI AMBIKA SONI): (a) No, Sir. (b) \nDoes not arise. \nSex Tourism \n†418. SHRI BRIJ BHUSHAN TIWARI: Will the Minister of TOURISM be pleased \nto state: \n(a) whether Government's attention has been drawn towards the sex tourism \nspreading in some parts of the country; \n(b) if so, the action being taken in this  regard; and \n(c) the details thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM (SHRIMATI AMBIKA SONI): (a) No specific \ncomplaint or report on prevalence of sex tourism in the country has been received in the \nMinistry of Tourism. \n(b) and (c) Do not arise \n†Original notice of the question was received   in Hindi. \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions   135 \nEnhancement of employment potential \n419. SHRI M.V. MYSURA REDDY: Will the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) whether it is a fact that the Tenth Five Year Plan envisaged to enhance \nemployment potential within the tourism sector as well as to foster economic \nintegration; \n(b) if so, the details of investment stimulated and unique tourism products \ndeveloped till date during the last three years, State-wise; \n(c) the details of employment generated in th is sector till date during the last three \nyears, State-wise; and \n(d) the revenue earned till date during the last three years? \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM (SHRIMATI AMBIKA SONI): (a) Yes, Sir. \n(b) During the Tenth Five Year Plan, the Ministry of Tourism has sanctioned  Rs. \n2011.68 crore for 1160 projects for development of Destinations, Circuits, Rural \nTourism, Events, Fairs and Festivals and IT projects. The unique tourism products \ndeveloped in the last 3 years in the whole of the country include Medical tourism, \nCruise tourism, Eco- tourism, Convention tourism etc. \n(c) As per the Tourism Satellite Account the total employment generated in tourism \nsector during the last three years is as under:  \n Year  Employment generated \n 2004-05 \n2005-06 \n2006-07 \n 44.7 million \n47.8 million \n51.1  million (Estimated) \n(d) As per the Tourism \nGDP (direct and Indirect) \nSatellite Account the contribution of Tourism Sector to in \nIndia is as under: \n    Year            Contribution of Tourism Sector to GDP (direct and indirect) \n 2002-03 \n2003-04 \n2004-05 \n 5.83% \n5.90% \n6.11 % \nThe Foreign Exchange earned during the last three years is as under: \n Year  Foreign Exchange earned \n 1  2 \n 2004  4769 US $ million \n\n136   Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions  \n1 2 \n2005 5731 US $ million \n2006 6569 US $ million \nTourism in concurrent list \n420.  SHRIMATI SHOBHANA BHARTIA: \nSHRIMATI SUPRIYA SULE: SHRI \nKARNENDU BHATTACHARJEE: \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government propose to bring tourism in the concurrent list of the  \nConstitution for the orderly growth of the sector on a pan -India basis and the Indo - \nAmerican Chamber of Commerce has recommended that the empowered Centre can  \nplay a more proactive role in developing the tourism sector and there is a need that  \ntax str ucture be harmonized to ensure uniformity of levying tax on tourism related  \nsectors like hotels, tour operators and travel services etc.; and \n(b) if so, whether Government are considering the suggestion as India is  \nbecoming one of the tourist destinations with its varied and rich natural  \nendowments? \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM (SHRIMATI AMBIKA SONI): (a) and (b) The \nissue of inclusion of Tourism in the Concurrent List of the 7th Schedule of the \nConstitution had earlier been examined by the Ministry of Tourism. It was felt that since \n\"Tourism\" is not appearing in any of the three lists of the 7th Schedule of the \nConstitution, the Central Government is competent to legislate on any matter under the \nresiduary powers. After detailed examination and taking into accou nt all the factors, it \nwas decided that the matter should be dropped. \nThe Ministry of Tourism functions as the nodal agency for development of tourism \nin the country. Some of the steps taken up by the Ministry for development of the \ntourism sector in the c ountry include development of tourist spots under its various \nschemes of infrastructure development for tourist circuits and destinations, taking up \npromotional activities in overseas markets through various media to attract more tourists \nto India, request ing State Governments to set up Tourist Police and deploy them at \nimportant popular tourist destinations for safety and security of tourists, launching of a \nsocial awareness campaign \"Atithi Devo Bhavah\" to sensitize the stakeholders in \ntourism, and the public in general, and granting recognition/approval to Guides/Travel \nAgents and Transport Operators in order to standardize their services and to avoid \nunscrupulous activities, fleecing, overcharging etc. by them. \nThe Ministry of Tourism also liaises  with various Ministries/Departments on issues \nlike availability  of visa timely, good airports, cheap airfares, good air connectivity, \nsufficient and affordable accommodation units, proper road and rail connectivity of the \ndestinations to the National Highways, entry permits for restricted areas, rationalization \nof taxes, etc. as well as to sort out any issues detrimental to the overall tourism \ndevelopment. \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions   137 \nTourist centres around Ganga river \n†421. SHRI  JAI PARKASH AGGARWAL: Will the Minister of TOURISM be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether Government propose to set up tourist centers at the places where the \nGanga, the Jamuna and the Saraswati rivers pass through; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether Government have prepared any plan to conduct a study on these rivers; \nand \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM (SHRIMATI AMBIKA SONI) (a) to (c) No, Sir. \n(d) Does not arise. \nDepletion of ground water level \n422. SHRI O.T. LEPCHA: Will the Minister of WATE R RESOURCES be pleased \nto state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that the ground water level in various districts of the country \nhas gone down thereby making the situation alarming; \n(b) if so, the city-wise/State-wise details thereof; and \n(c) the remedial measures Government propose to take in this respect? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES \n(SHRI JAY PRAKASH NARAYAN YADAV): (a) and (b) Decline in ground water \nlevels has been observed in various parts of the country in consultation the State \nGovernments. As per the latest assessments of ground water resources carried out by \nCentral Ground Water Board (CGWB) and States in 2004, out of 5723 assessment units \n(Blocks/Mandals/Talukas), 839 units are 'over -exploited' (where stage of ground water \nexploitation is  more than 100% with significant decline in long term trend of ground \nwater level in either pre- monsoon or post -monsoon or both), 226 units are 'critical' \n(where ground water exploitation is between 90% and 100% with significant decline in \nlong term trend of water level in both pre -monsoon and post -monsoon periods). The \nState-wise details of over-exploited, critical and semi-critical assessment units are given \nin the Statedment (See below) \n(c) 'Water' being a State subject, it is the responsibility of the concerned State \nGovernments to take suitable action for arresting the declining ground water level in \ntheir respective States. However, based on the survey reports, some of the important \nmeasures taken by the Central Government for improvement of ground water level • in \nthe country are:- \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\n138   Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \n(i) Constitution of 'Artificial Recharge of Ground Water Advisory Council' with the \nobjective to popularize  the concept of artificial recharge among various \nstakeholders. \n(ii) Preparation of 'Master Plan for Artificial Recharge to Ground Water' which has \nidentified a total of 4.5 lakh sq.km. area in the country needing artificial \nrecharge of ground water. \n(iii) Circulation of Manual/Guide on Artificial Recharge to Ground Water to the \nStates/Union Territories to enable them to formulate area specific artificial \nrecharge schemes to check the declining trend of ground water levels. \n(iv) Implementation of 165 demons trative recharge projects in various States under \nthe Central Sector scheme on 'Study of recharge to ground water' in Ninth \nPlan. \n(v) A demonstrative scheme on \"Rain Water Harvesting and Artificial Recharge to \nGround Water\" during 2006 -08 under Central Sector Scheme of 'Ground \nwater management and regulation' in identified areas of the States of Andhra \nPradesh, Karnataka, Madhya Pradesh and Tamil Nadu. \n(vi) During the XI Plan, CGWB has planned to take up 75 artificial recharge and \nrainwater harvesting studies. \n(vii) Central Ground Water Authority, constituted under Section 3(3) of the \nEnvironment (protection) Act of 1986, has notified 43 Over -exploited areas in \nthe country for regulation of ground water development and management and \nalso notified 65 over -exploited areas in various States, for registration of \nground water abstraction structures. \n(viii) The Central Ground Water Authority has directed the Chief Secretaries of the \nconcerned States to take all measures to adopt artificial recharge to ground \nwater/promote rain water harvesting in all the Over -exploited areas falling \nunder their jurisdiction and ensure inclusion of roof top rain water harvesting \nin the building bye-laws. \nStatement \nCategorization of blocks/mandals/talnkas in India (Assessment - 2004)  \nSI. States/Union Tota No. of Over exploited Critical Semi-critical \nNo. Territories Assessed  Units Nos. % Nos. % Nos.  