Significant decline \nin long term water level \ntrend  in  either pre-monsoon or post-monsoon period \n\n140   Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \nPani Satyagraha \n423. SHRIMATI SYEDA ANWARA TAIMUR: \nSHRI VIJAY J. DARDA: \nWill the Minister of WATER RESOURCES be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government are aware of the Pani Satyagraha started on 30th of \nJanuary coinciding the death anniversary of Mahatma Gandhi, demanding that green \nbelts and water bodies across the country be protected by noti fying them as Water \nSanctuaries; and \n(b) if so, whether Government are moving in that direction to ensure sustainable \nwater supply for aam aadmil \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES \n(SHRI JAY PRAKASH NARAYAN YADAV): (a) The Ministry of Water Resources has \nnot received any information from any source about Pani Satyagraha demanding that \ngreen belts and water bodies across the country be protected by notifying them as Water \nSanctuaries. \n(b) Does not arise. \nLinking of Mahanadi with Brahmani River \n424. MS. PRAMILA BOHIDAR: \nSHRI B. J. PANDA: \nWill the Minister of WATER RESOURCES be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is proposed to link  Mahanadi with Brahmani in Orissa to control \nfloods in the coastal districts of the State; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n  (c) what are the details of Central help that is proposed to be extended to the States in \nthe execution of this gigantic task? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES \n(SHRI JAY PRAKASH NARAYAN YADAV): (a) There is no proposal of Natio nal \nWater Development Agency (NWDA) to link Mahanadi with Brahmani in Orissa. \nHowever, proposed Subernarekha - Mahanadi link by NWDA crosses Brahmani river, \nbut there is no proposal to withdraw the water from or to supplement the water of \nBrahmani river through this link. \n(b) Does not arise. \n(c) Based on various studies conducted, NWDA under National Perspective Plans \n(NPP) has identified 30 links (16  Peninsular and 14 Himalayan) for preparation of \nFeasibility Reports (FRs). NWDA has prepared FRs of 14 links under Peninsular \nComponent and 2 links under Himalayan Component (Indian Portion). Preparation of  \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions   141 \nDetailed Project Report (DPR) for one priority link viz. Ken-Betwa link has been taken \nup by NWDA afte r signing of a tripartite agreement between the Government of India \nand concerned States of Madhya Pradesh and Uttar Pradesh on 25.08.2005. \nImplementation of inter basin water transfer proposals depends upon consensus and \ncooperation of the concerned states. No interlinking project under NPP is under \nimplementation and the stage has not come for the financial planning of interlinking of \nRivers (ILR) project. \nDiversion of river water by China \n†425.  SHRI RAJ MOHINDER SINGH MAJITHA: \nSHRI RAM JETHMALANI: \nWill the Minister of WATER RESOURCES be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that India had offered to constitute a joint team to China after \nappearing of news -item with regard to Chinese efforts for diverting water of \nBrahmaputra river, which is flowing towards India into its territory; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) time when this proposal had been prepared at first; \n(d) whether the proposal has been implemented so far; and \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES \n(SHRI JAY PRAKASH NARAYAN YADAV): (a) to (c) There have been reports about \nChina undertaking projects to divert waters of Brahmaputra river. Government of India \nhas taken up this issue with the Chinese side several times including during the visit of \nthe Hon'ble President of China to India in November 2006. The joint declaration issued \nduring the visit inter-alia states, \"The two sides will set up an expert -level machanism \nto discuss interaction and cooperation on the provision of flood season hydrological data \nemergency management and other issues regarding trans -border rivers as agreed \nbetween them.\" \n(d) and (e) In pursuance of the above, both the sides have constituted their expert \ngroups. \nReport regarding old dams \n†426.  SHRI SATYAVRAT CHATURVEDI: \nSHRI MOTILAL VORA: \nWill the Minister of WATER RESOURCES be pleased to state: \n(a) total number of dams which are hundred years or more than hundred years old at \npresent in the country; \n(b) the number of reports the Central Water Commission has received from States \nduring last two years regarding the status of dams alongwith details thereof; \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\n142   Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \n(c) the names of the States which have not sent any report during last two years \nwith regard to old dams; and \n(d) whether the Central Water Commission gets these dams checked by  \nexperts? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES \n(SHRI JAY PRAKASH NARAYAN YADAV): (a) As per National Register of Large \nDams 2002, there are ninety nine dams, which are more than hundred years old. \n(b) Central Water Commission has received 18 reports from the States during the  \nlast two years regarding the status of dams, details of which are furnished in the \nStatement (See below). \n(c) State Government of Andhra Pradesh, Jharkhand, Karnataka, Punjab,  \nUttarakhand and West Bengal have not sent any report during the last two years  \nwith regard to the health status of the dams. \n(d) Checking of all dams is the responsibility of the Dam Safety Org anization/Dam \nSafety Cell of the owners of the dams, i. e. , State Governments/other organizations.  \nCentral Water Commission arranges to send team of experts only when specific  \nrequest from the dam owners is received in this regard. \nStatement \nThe status of reports received from State Governments during the \nlast two years i.e., 2005-06  \nSI. No. Name of State Reports received for \n1. Bihar 2004-05 and 2005-06 \n2. Chhattisgarh 2004 \n3. Gujarat 2005 \n4. Kerela 2004 & 2005 \n5. Madhya Pradesh 2003-04 & 2004-05 \n6. Maharashtra 2004 & 2005 \n7. Orissa 2004 & 2005 \n8. Rajasthan 2004-05 & 2005-06 \n9. Tamil Nadu 2004-05 & 2005-06 \n10. Uttar Pradesh 2004 & 2005 \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG, 2007] Unstarred questions   143 \nSafe drinking water for habitations \n427.  SHRI K. E. ISMAIL: \nSHRI SYED AZEEZ PASHA: \nWill the Minister of WATER RESOURCES be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that thousands of habitations in different States are identified \nas having water sources contaminated by chemicals like iron, arsenic, fluoride, nitrates, \nsulphates and other salts; \n(b) if so, the details of such habitations State-wise; and \n(c) the measures being taken to provide these habitations with safe drinking water?  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES \n(SHRI JAY PRAKASH NARAYAN YADAV): (a) and (b) As per available information, \nground water in major parts of the country is by and large potable. There are, however, \nareas which have poor quality of ground water mainly due to contamination from \nground water bearing rocks, excessive use of fertilizers, industrial and domestic \neffluents, mining wastes, saline water ingress etc. The Central Ground Water Board \n(CGWB), during its surveys and studies of the contamination of ground water have \nrevealed that arsenic and fluoride are the main geogenic contaminants. As per the \ninformation provided by Central Pollution Control Board (CPCB), the names of the \ndistricts of some States where ground water has been found contaminated and reasons \nthereof are given in the enclosed Statement {See below). \n(c) \"Water\" being a State subject, it is primarily the responsibility of the State \nGovernments to take steps to check ground water pollution. However, the Union \nGovernment has also taken following measures to mitigate the problem of ground water \npollution:- \n(i)   The CGWB drills arsenic free wells in the arsenic affected areas and \nprovides technical guidance for clearing of source of drinking water in \narsenic affected areas. It also conducts mass awareness programmes and \nwater management training programmes  in these areas and carries out \nresearch to understand the origin and distribution of arsenic in ground \nwater for adopting long term remedial measures. \n(ii)   The Union Ministry of Rural Development (Department of Drinking Water \nSupply) under the Rajiv Gandhi National Drinking Water Mission provides \nfinancial and technical assistance to States through a Centrally Sponsored \nScheme, namely Accelerated Rural Water Supply Programme (ARWSP) \nfor provision of safe drinking water to the rural population as per the \nstipulated norms. In order to provide focussed attention to tackle water \nquality problems, Sub- Mission guidelines under ARWSP have been \nrevised in February, 2006, wherein, 20% of ARWSP funds is retained at \nthe Centre and released to States on need-basis. \n(iii) To ensure safe drinking water supply to all the urban population of the \ncountry, the Ministry of Urban Development have launched two new \nprogrammes, viz. Jawaharlal Nehru National Urban Renewal Mission \n\n144   Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \n(JNNURM) and Urban Infrastructure Development Scheme for Small and \nMedium Towns (UIDSSMT), to provide infrastructure facilities to 5161 \nurban towns of the country. The project envisages to provide safe drinking \nwater to every citizen of India within a time frame of 7 years. \n(iv) Central Pollution Control Board (CPCB), under the Ministry of Environment \nand Forests has adopted several measures like directing the industries \nthrough State Pollution Control Boards to limit the discharge of effluents \nwithin ,the stipulated standards, initiation of schemes to give assistance to \nclusters of small scale industrial units for setting up common effluent \ntreatment plants for implementation of a scheme for monitoring of water \nquality in the critically polluted areas. \nStatement \nState-wise details of contamination of ground water in some areas of the districts \ndue to various contaminants  \nSI.       State Iron Fluoride Nitrate Arsenic \nNo.     \n1         2 3 4 5 6 \n1.        Andhra - Prakasam, Vishakhapatnam, - \nPradesh  Nellore \nAnantpur, \nEast Godavari, \nKrishna, Prakasam \n \n  Nalgonda, \nRangareddy, \nAdilabad \nNellore, Chittoor, \nAnantpur, Cuddapah \nKurnool, \nMehboobnagar, \nRangareddy, \nMedak, Adilabad, \nNalgonda, \nKhammam \n \n2.        Assam Northern \nBank of \nBrahmaputra \n- - Dhimaji, \nKarimganj, \n3.        Bihar Champaran, Giridih, Palamau, Gaya, Bhojpur, \n Muzaffarpur, Jamui, Patna, Nalanda Paschimi \n Gaya, \n \nDhanbad Nawada, Champaran, \n Deoghar,  Bhagalpur, Purba \n Madhubani,  Sahebgunj, Champaran \n Patna, \nPalamau, \nNalanda, \nNawada, \nBanka \n Banka Sitamarhi \nSupaul, Araria, \nKishanganj, \nPurnia, \nKatihar, \nPatna, \n \n  \n                                  (Chhapra) Vaishali, \n    Madhubani \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions   145  \n1 2 3                       4 5 6 \n4. Chhattisgarh     -                    - - Rajnandgaon, \nDurg \n5. Gujarat Kachchh, Surendra \nNagar,  Rajkot, \nAhmedabad, \nMehsana, \n  \n  Banaskantha. \nSabarkantha, \n  \n  Panchmahal, \nKheda \n  \n6. Haryana Rohtak, Jind, \nHissar, Bhiwani, \nMahendragarh, \nFaridabad \nAmbala,  Sonepat, \nJind, Gurgaon, \nFaridabad, Hissar, \nSirsa, Karnal, \nKurukshetra, \n,   Rohtak,  Bhiwani, \nMahendragarh \n \n7. Himachal \nPradesh \n- Kulu, Solan,  Una - \n8. Karnataka Tumkur,  Kolar, \nBangalore, \nGulbarga Bellary, \nRaichur \n  \n9. Kerala Palghat - - \n10. Jharkhand - - Sahibganj \n11. Madhya \nPradesh \nBhind Morena, \nGuna, Jhabua, \nChhindwara, Seoni, \nMandla,  Raipur, \nVidisha \nSehore  \n12. Maharashtra Bhandara, \nChandrapur, \nNanded, \nAurangabad \nThane, Jalna,  Beed, \nNanded,  Latur, \nOsmanabad, Solapur, \nSatara, Sangh, \nKolhapur,  Dhule, \nJalgaon, Aurangabad, \nAhmednagar,  Pune, \nBuldhana, Amravati, \nAkola, Nagpur, \nWardha, Bhandara, \nChandrapur, Gadchiroli \n \n\n146   Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions  \n1 2                         3 4 5 6 \n13. Orissa          Parts of \ncoastal Orissa \nBolangir - - \n14. Punjab Ludhiana \nFaridkot, \nBhatinda, \nSangrur, \nJalandhar, \nPatiala, Faridkot, \nFirozpur, Sangrur, \nBhatinda \n \n  Amritsar   \n15. Rajasthan     Bikaner, Barmer, Jaipur, Churu, - \n Alwar, Bikaner, Ganganagar,  \n Dungarpur Ganganagar, \nJalore, Nagaur, \nPali, Sirohi \nBikaner, Jalore, \nBarmer, Bundi, \nSwai Madhopur \n \n16. Tamil Nadu Dharmapuri, \nSalem North \n'Coimbatore, Periyar, \nSalem \n- \n Arcot-Ambedkar,   \n  Villipuram-\nPadayatchi, \n  \n Muthuramalingam,   \n  Tiruchirapalli, \nPudukottai \n  \n17. Tripura     Dharmnagar, \nKauleshaher, \n - - \n Khowai, Ambasa,   \n Amapur and \nparts of \n   \n Agartala valley   \n18. Uttar Bulandshahar, Orai, Jhansi, Pilibhit, \n Pradesh Aligarh, Agra, Lalitpur, Lakhimpur, \n Unnao, Rae-Bareli' Faizabad, Baharaich, \n   Sultanpur, \nMaharajganj, \nGorakhpur, \nDeoria \nShravasti, \nBalrampur, \nSiddarthnagar, \nMaharajganj, \nKushinagar, \nBallia, Unnao, \nLucknow \n19. West         Midnapore, Birbhum Uttar Dinajpur, Kolkata, \n Bengal         Howrah,  Malda, Birbhum, Malda, South \n Hoogly,  Nadia, Midnapore, 24-Paraganas, \n Bankura  Howrah, Murshidabad \nPurulia \nNorth \n24-Paraganas \nNadia, \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions   147  \n1 2 3 4 5 6 \n     Hoogly \nMurshidbad, \nBradhaman, \nHowrah \n20. NCT of \nDelhi \n- - Shahdara, & \nMehrauli \nBlocks \n \nAdditional funding for AIBP \n428. SHRIMATI PREMA CARIAPPA: \nSHRIMATI SHOBHANA BHARTIA: \nWill the Minister of WATER RESOURCES be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Centre is likely to provide additional funding of Rs. 2,000 crore for the \nAccelerated Irrigation Benefit Programme during the current financial year and the \nprogramme is the part of the Government's seven point Bharat Nirman programme to \npush the development of rural India into fast gear; \n(b) whether the additional fund for AIBP will be in addition to the budgetary \nprovision of Rs. 3,580 crore during 2007-08; and \n(c) whether the States will be persuaded to earmark more funds for the irrigation \nprojects which play a crucial role in improving farm output and bridging the yield gap \nof crops? \nTHE MI NISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES \n(SHRI JAY PRAKASH NARAYAN YADAV): (a) to (c) A budgetary provision of Rs. \n3580 crores during the year, 2007- 08 has been made for Accelerated Irrigation Benefit \nProgramme. Planning Commission have indicated  a upward revision of AIBP outlays \nby Rs.2000 crores. AIBP meets the demands of the Bharat Nirman programme under \nwhich a major thrust on irrigation is included. Releases under AIBP are based on AIBP \nproposals submitted by the State Governments with adequa te budget provision for \nworks in State's budget and AIBP ceiling for the State fixed by the Planning \nCommission in consultation with the State Governments. \nBringing of more hectares of land under irrigation \n†429. DR. MURLI MANOHAR JOSHI: \nSHRI RAJ MOHINDER SINGH MAJITHA: \nWill the Minister of WATER RESOURCES be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that a new Accelerated Irrigation Benefits Programme has \nbeen prepared for bringing one crore hectare of land under irrigated area in the country;  \n†Original noiice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\n148   Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \n(b) if so, the percentage of funds to be invested by the Central Government and \nState Governments respectively on the projects for implementation of this programme; \n(c) whether Government have got the consent of the State Governments for \ninvesting the above proposed percentage of funds; and \n(d) if so, the details in this regard and the State Governments who have given their \nconsent on the matter? \nTHE MINISTER OF STA TE IN THE MINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES \n(SHRI JAY PRAKASH NARAYAN YADAV): (a) Irrigation component of Bharat \nNirman envisages creation of irrigation potential of one crore hectare. Accelerated \nIrrigation Benefit Programme (AIBP) is providing assistance to th e Major, Medium and \nERM (Extension, Renovation and Modernization) projects in the country and surface \nwater minor irrigation schemes in the special category States and drought prone/tribal \narea in the non -special category States. Grant ranging from 25% to 90% of the project \ncost is provided to the projects included in AIBP which is expected to give a big boost \nto irrigation area in the country. \n(b) As per prevailing guidelines, grant amounting to 25% of the project cost of \nmajor and medium projects included in AIBP for normal category States and grants up \nto 90% of the project cost for major and medium project benefiting drought prone/ tribal \narea is provided. Eligible surface water minor irrigation schemes in special category \nStates and schemes benefiting droug ht prone/tribal area in non special category States \nare provided 90% grant of the project cost included in AIBP. Balance cost is to be borne \nby the State Governments. \n(c) and (d) The releases under AIBP are based on AIBP proposals submitted by the \nState Governments on year to year basis with adequate budget provision for works and \nAIBP ceiling for the State fixed by the Planning Commission in consultation with the \nState Governments. \nMelting of glaciers in Himalayan region \n430. SHRIMATI N.P. DURGA: Will the Mini ster of WATER RESOURCES be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that as per a study conducted by ISRO, the fast melting of \nglaciers in the Himalayan region is going to pose a threat to hydro projects in the \nnorthern region: \n(b) if so, whether any assessment has been made to the extent of impact it will have \non the hydro plants; and \n(c) if so, the details of parts (a) and (b) above? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES \n(SHRI JAY PRAKASH NARAYAN YADAV): (a) The Department of Space have \nreported that no specific studies have been carried out by Department of Space/ Indian \nSpace Research Organisation (DOS/ISRO) in this regard. \n(b) and (c) Do not arise. \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG, 2007] Unstarred questions   149 \nMaintenance of older dams \n†431. SHRIMATI SUSHMA SWARAJ: Will the Minister of WATER RESOURCES \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that nearly one hundred large dams have turned more than a \nhundred years old; \n(b) State-wise number of dams more than a hundred- years old; and the efforts being \nmade for maintenance and operation of these old dams? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES \n(SHRI JAY PRAKASH NARAYAN YADAV): (a) and (b) As per National Register for \nLarge Dams -2002, there are 99 large dams which are more than hundred y ears old. \nState-wise details are given in the enclosed Statement (See below). \n(c) Responsibility for maintenance and operation of dams lies with the owners,  \ni.e., State Government(s) concerned/other Organization. Central Water Commission  \nplays only an advi sory role for suggesting remedial measures to relieve distress in  \nthe dams, if specific request is received from the State Government/Organization in  \nthis regard. \nStatement \nState-wise numbers of large dams which are more than hundred years old  \nSI. No. Name of State No. of large dams more \n  than hundred years old \n1. Andhra Pradesh 4   \n2. Bihar 1   \n3. Gujarat 16   \n4. Karnataka 13   \n5. Maharashtra 27   \n6. Madhya Pradesh 2   \n7. Orissa 2   \n8. Rajasthan 28   \n9. Tamil Nadu 2   \n10. Uttar Pradesh 4   \n TOTAL: 99   \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\n150   Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \nPresent status of Kalasa and Banduri Nala Projects \n432. DR. VIJAY MALLYA: Will the Minister of WATER RESOURCES be pleased \nto state: \n(a) what is the present status of the Kalasa and Banduri Nala Projects of Karnataka; \n(b) what are the decisions made and recommended by the Central Water \nCommission in respect of the project; \n \n(c) what is the total estimated cost of the project and what is the amo unt of grants \nreleased so far; and \n(d) what is the future action plan of Government to complete the said project? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES \n(SHRI JAY PRAKASH NARAYAN YADAV): (a) As informed by Government of \nKarnataka, they have u ndertaken the work of diversion of channel of Kalasanala \ndiversion scheme in the non-forest area. \n(b) The Ministry of Water Resources gave an 'in principle' clearance to the proposal \nof Government of Karnataka for the diversion of 7.56 TMC of water from Mahada yi \nbasin to Malprabha basin to meet the drinking water need of Hubli/ Dharwad from \nwater availability angle on 30th April, 2002. The Government of Goa in July, 2002 took \nstrong exception to the grant of this clearance by the Ministry and requested for sett ing \nup of an Inter -State Water Disputes Tribunal under the Inter -State Water Disputes Act, \n1956 for resolution of the dispute. In view of this, the 'in principle' clearance granted by \nthe Ministry was placed 'in Abeyance' in September, 2002. \n(c) and (d) As inf ormed by Government of Karnataka, the estimated cost of the \nproject is Rs.93.98 crore at 1999- 2000 price level. Water being a State subject, the \nWater Resources projects are planned, funded and executed by the State Governments \nas per their own priorities from their own resources. \nAmount Sanctioned for Jala Yagnam in Andhra Pradesh \n433. SHRI RAVULA CHANDRA SEKAR REDDY: Will the Minister of WATER  \nRESOURCES be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government have sanctioned any amount for Jala Yagnam in Andhra \nPradesh; \n(b) if so, the details thereof during the last three years, year-wise; \n(c) the projects initiated during the last three years; \n(d) how many irrigation projects have been taken up and are under construction; \n(e) by when all the projects would be completed under Jala Yagnam; and \n(f) the cases of misuse reported in the irrigation projects during the last three years?  \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions   151 \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES \n(SHRI JAY PRAKASH NARAYAN YADAV): (a) to (f) Ir rigation is a States's subject \nand planning, execution as well as funding of the irrigation projects is within the \npurview of the State Government. Jala Yagnam is a programme of the State \nGovernment of Andhra Pradesh. Central Government provides financial assistance to \nthose projects of Jala Yagnam which are under AIBP. Assistance provided under AIBP \nto these projects during last three years is given in the Statement (See below). The \nGovernment of Andhra Pradesh has informed that projects included in Jala Y agnam are \nto be completed in two to five years. No report of misuse of funds under Jalayagnam \nhave come to the notice of Ministry of Water Resources during last three years. \nStatement \nCentral Assistance (CA/grant released to Andhra Pradesh (for Projects \nincluded in JALA YAGNUM) under AIBP during 2004-05 to 2007-08 \n(Rs. in crore)  \nSI. \nNo. \nName of \nState/Project (Started \nin Plan) \n  Amount   \n 2004-05 2005-06 2006-07 2007-08 \nupto 9.4.07 \n Loan Grant Total           Grant Grant Grant \n1 2 3 4 5                  6 7 8 \nMajor, Medium \nIrrigation Projects \n     \n1. ANDHRA PRADESH \nSomasilla (V) \n     \n2. Vamsedhera Ph.I 14.8246 6.3534 21.1780 6.6830  \n3. Flood  Flow Canal  \nof SRSP \n  120.0000 127.4000  \n4. Sriramsagar Project - II   2.2690 72.0000  \n5. Tadlpudi   LIS    48.2200  \n6. Pushkara LIS    33.1155  \n7. Ralivagu    6.7095  \n8. Gollavagu    28.3500  \n9. Mathadivagu    28.3500   \n10. Peddavagu    50.6250   \n1 1. Gundlakamma \nReservoir \n  59.6100 39.7425  \n 12. Valligallu Reservoir    36.0855   \n13. Ali Sagar LIS    13.5000   \n14. J.Chokkarao  LIS    298.1300   \n15. Guthpa LIS    10.9500   \n16. Nilwai    2.8500  6.1500 \n17. Khomaram  Bhima    7.7800  74.5400 \n18. Thotapalli  Barrage    63.1410  \n\n152   Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions  \n1 2 3  . 4 5 6 7 8 \n19. Tarakarma Thirtha \nSagaram  Project \n    33.0060   \n20. SwarnamukhiMed \nIrrigation   Project \n   5.9310 5.9310  \n21. Palemvagu     9.5355   \n (Andhra  Pradesh)-Total 14.8246  6.3534  21.1780  293.4925 816.4220  80.6900 \nHotels located in Ponds \n†434. DR. PRAB HA THAKUR: Will the Minister of WATER RESOURCES be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether some hotels are located amidst the ponds in Rajasthan, if so, the details \nthereof and the details of ponds amidst which the hotels are located; \n(b) whether the water of the ponds is b eing used by people as drinking water and \nwhether it is safe to be used as drinking water; \n(c) whether arrangements of sewer line have been made by such hotels for drainage \nof dirty water; and \n(d) if so, the hotels where such arrangements have been made in Rajasth an and the \nplaces where these sewer lines open and the details thereof and if not, the reasons \ntherefor? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES \n(SHRI JAY PRAKASH NARAYAN YADAV): (a) The Ministry of Tourism has \ninformed that no hotels are located amidst ponds in Rajasthan. However, there are \nhotels located in the middle of lakes in Rajasthan. \n(b) to (d) Do not arise. \nWater disputes with China \n435. SHRI NABAM REBIA: Will the Minister of WATER RESOURCES be pleased \nto state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that there exist water disputes between the People's Republic \nof China and India; \n(b) if so, whether any joint committee/commission has been constituted by both the \ncountries to solve such disputes; and \n(c) the names of the main rivers where such disputes do arise? \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions   153 \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES \n(SHRI JAY PRAKASH NARAYAN YADAV): (a) No Sir, there is no water dispute \nbetween the Peoples' Republic of China and India. \n(b) Does not arise. \n(c) Does not arise. \nContamination of ground water \n436. DR. M. A. M. RAMASWAMY: Will the Minister of WATER RESOURCES be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that various States are affected by the contaminated ground \nwater with poisonous substances; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Central Government are providing assistance to various States to \nfight out the menace of arsenic contamination of ground water;    ' \n(d) if so, the details thereof; \n(e) whether the situation of arsenic contamination of ground water has improved; \nand if not, the reasons therefor? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES \n(SHRI JAY PRAKASH NARAYAN YADAV): (a) and (b)  The studies conducted by \nCentral Ground Water  Board (CGWB) have revealed that quality of ground water in \nmost parts of the country is potable except in a few areas where ground water is \ncontaminated due to inherent properties of rock formations which hold water or through \nwhich the ground water moves . Fluoride and Arsenic are the main geogenic \ncontaminants which when exceed the permisible limit as prescribed by BIS cause health \nhazard. The State- wise details of Flouride and Arsenic contamination in ground water \nare given in the enclosed Statement-I (See below). \n(c) and (d) To assist various States in fighting the menance of arsenic contamination \nin ground water, CGWB carries out exploratory drilling in the arsenic affected areas in \norder to delineate arsenic free potential aquifer zones. Successful exp loratory wells \ntapping arsenic free deeper aquifers are handed over to the user agencies for utilization \nin their water supply scheme. State -wise details of arsenic free exploratory wells drilled \nby Central Ground Water Board are given in the enclosed Statement-II (See below). \n(e) and (f) Once the aquifer has been contaminated, it is difficult to improve the \nquality of ground water insitu. Protective measures for providing safe drinking water \nsupply in the affected areas are being taken up by the State Governments of Assam, \nChhattisgarh, Bihar, Uttar Pradesh and West Bengal. \n\n154   Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \nStatement-I \nParts of the districts affected by excess Fluoride and Arsenic \ncontamination in ground water  \nSI, Name of Name of the Districts  \nNo. the State Fluoride (above 1.-5 ppm) Arsenic(0.01 ppm) \n1 2  4 \n1. Andhra Prakasam, Anantapur, Nellore. —  \n Pradesh Nalgonda, Ranga Reddy, Adilabad, \nKrishna, Kurnool, Cuddapah, Guntur. \nKarimnagar \n \n2. Assam Kamrup. Nowgong, Karbi-Anglong Dhemaji \n3. Bihar Munger, Nawada, Gaya, Jamui Bhojpur, Buxar, \nVaishali, Saran, \nSamastipur, \nBegusarai, Patna, \nKhagaria, Munger, \nBhagalpur, Katihar \nand Darbhanga \n4. Chhattisgarh Bastar, Bilaspur, Dhamtari, Kanker, \nKorba, Koriya, Raipur. Rajnandgaon \nRajnandgaon \n5. Delhi North-west, West, South-west —  \n6. Gujarat Ahmedabad, Amreli, Banaskantha, \nBhavnagar, Junagarh, Rajkot, \nSurendranagar, Vadodra, Kachchh, \nPanchmahal, Mehsana, Sabarkantha, \n \n7. Haryana Rohtak, Jhajjar, Jind, Hissar, Bhiwani, \nMahendragarh. Faridabad, Gurgaon, Kaithal, \nKarnal, Kurukshetra, Sirsa, Sonepat, Rewari, \nFatehabad. Panipat \n \n8. Jharkhand Giridih, Bokaro —  \n9. Karnataka Bijapur, Gulbarga, Bellary, Raichur, \nChitradurga, Kolar, Gadag \n—  \n10. Kerala Palghat, Alleppey  \n11. Madhya Bhind, Morena, Hoshangabacl, Guna, —  \n Pradesh Jhabua, Tikamgarh, Chhindwara, Seoni, \nMandla, Dindori, Sheopuri, Dhar,   \nJabalpur city \n \n12. Maharashtra Bhandara, Chandrapur, Nanded, \nGadchiroli, Yavatmal, Sholapur \n—  \n13. Orissa Bolangir, Khurda, Kalahandi, —  \n14. Punjab Bhatinda, Sangrur, Mansa, Moga, \nFeorzepur, Faridkot, Muktsar, Patiala \n \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions   155  \n1 2 3 4 \n15. Rajasthan Ajmer, Barmer, Bhilwara, Bikaner, Dungarpur, \nGanganagar, Hanumangarh, Jaipur, Jaisalmer, \nJalore, Jhunjhunu, Jodhpur. Nagaur, Pali, \nRajsamand, Sikas Sirohi \n \n16. Tamil Nadu Dharmapuri, Salem, Erode, Vellore, \nNamakkal. Tiruchirapalli, \nRamanathapuram. Villupuram. \nCuddalore, Tirunelveli \n \n17. Uttar Fatehpur, Raibareli, Lakhimpur Kheri, Ballia, Gonda, \n Pradesh Lucknow, Uannao. Kanpur, Hardoi, Lakhimpur Kheri, \n  Bulandshahar, Aligarh, Agra, Mathura, Siddarth Nagar \n  Ghaziabad, Meerut, Firozabad, Etah, \nFatehgarh, Mainpuri, Mahoba, Allahabad, \nVaranasi \nBallia, Gonda \n18. West Birbhum. Howrah, North 24 Parganas Malda, \n Bengal  Murshidabad, \nNadia, North 24 \n   Parganas, South 24 \nParganas. Hoogli, \nHowrah, Bardhawan \nStatement-II \nState-wise details of arsenic free exploratory wells drilled CGWB  \nSI. No State/ District No. of arsenic free EW constructed \n1 2   \nBihar    \n1. Bhojpur 7  \n2. Patna 5  \n3. Samastipur 1  \n4. Buxar 1  \n    Uttar Pradesh   \n1. Ballia 6  \n2. Balrampur 3  \n3. Gonda 2  \n4. Lakhimpur Kheri 2  \n5. Siddarth Nagar 1  \nWest Bengal   \n1. Murshidabad 31  \n\n156   Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions  \n1               2 3 \n2.              Nadia 17 \n3.              N 24 Parganas 35 \n.4.             Hugli 4 \n5.              Bardhaman 9 \n6.              Haora 2 \n7.              Malda 4 \nChhattisgarh  \n1.             Rajnandgaon 6 \nCess levied on bottled water \n437. DR. M.A.M. RAMASWAMY: Will the Minister of WATER RESOURCES be \npleased to state: \n(a) the cess being levied on bottled water and beverages industry at present; \n(b) whether a sub -committee appointed by his Ministry have recommended \nincreasing cess on bottled water and beverages industry which use water as a basic raw \nmaterial; \n(c) if so, the details thereof; and \n(d) the other recommendation of the sub-committee? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES \n(SHRI JAY PRAKASH NARAYAN YADA V): (a) Water cess is levied and collected \nby State Pollution Control Boards for prevention and control of water pollution. \nDifferential rates of cess are charged depending upon the use of water, the rate being \nhigher where pollution load is expected to be  more which results in higher cost of \npollution abatement. It varies from 2 paise/kiloliter for domestic purposes to 30 paise \nkiloliter for industrial purposes. \n(b) to (d) The Sub -Committee on Policy for Water for Industries constituted by the \nMinistry of Water Resources under the Artificial Recharge of Ground Water Advisory \nCouncil has submitted its report to the Ministry. \nUtilisation of rain water by UT \n†438. SHRI AJAY SINGH CHAUTALA: Will the Minister of WATER RESOURCES \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether IIT h as developed such a technique for water conservation during two \nyears whereby rain water will be utilized successfully in the urban and rural areas of the \ncountry; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\nWritten answers to [14 AUG., 2007] Unstarred questions   157 \n(c) to what extent this technique is likely to be useful in solving the problem of water \ncrisis in the country? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES \n(SHRI JAY PRAKASH NARAYAN YAD AV): (a) to (c) Rain water harvesting is a \ntraditional technique for water conservation and has been adopted at different places \nwith necessary variations to suit the requirement since very long time. However, \ninnovative ideas have been suggested from time  to time by many organizations \nincluding UTs to make it more effective with due consideration to local factors. \nDepletion of ground water \n439. SHRI K. CHANDRAN PILLAI: Will the Minister of WATER RESOURCES be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that the ground water table is depleting in all geographical \nregions in the country; \n(b) if so, whether it poses serious water crisis; and \n(c) if so, what steps have been taken/propose to take for the preservation of water \nbodies and to prevent ground water depletion and also to replenish the water table? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES \n(SHRI JAY PRAKASH NARAYAN YADAV): (a) and (b) Yes, Sir. A long-term analysis \nof ground water level by the Central Ground Water Board (CGWB), under the Ministry \nof Wa ter Resources, during the years 1995 -2004, indicates decline in ground water \nlevels in some parts of various States/Union Territories in the country. Depletion of \nground water table leads to drying up of existing wells and hence requires deepening of \nwells thereby leading to increased energy consumption and pumping costs. \n(c) 'Water' being a State subject, it is the responsibility of the concerned State \nGovernments to take suitable action for the preservation of water bodies and arresting \nthe declining grou nd water level in their respective States. However, the steps taken for \nthe preservation of water bodies and to prevent ground water depletion and to replenish \nthe water table by the Central Government in the country are:- \n(i)   Constitution of 'Artificial Recharge of Ground Water Advisory Council' \nwith the objective to popularize the concept of artificial recharge among \nvarious stakeholders. \n(ii)   Preparation of 'Master Plan for Artificial Recharge to Ground Water' which \nhas identified a total of 4.5 lakh  sq.km. area in the country needing \nartificial recharge of ground water. \n(iii)  Circulation of Manual/Guide on Artificial Recharge to Ground Water to \nthe States/ Union Territories to enable them to formulate area specific \nartificial recharge schemes to check the declining trend of ground water \nlevels. \n\n158   Written answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred questions \n(iv)  Implementation of 165 demonstrative recharge projects in various States \nunder the Central Sector scheme on Study of recharge to ground water in \nIX Plan. \n(v)   Implementation of a demonstrative scheme on \"Rain Water Harvesting and \nArtificial Recharge to Ground Water\" by the CGWB in identified areas of \nthe States of Andhra Pradesh, Karnataka, Madhya Pradesh and Tamil \nNadu. \n(vi)  Implementation of 7 5 artificial recharge and rainwater harvesting studies \nby CGWB during XI Plan. \n(vii) Constitution of the Central Ground Water Authority (CGWA) under \nSection 3(3) of the Environment (Protection) Act of 1986, notification of \n43 Over -exploited areas in the co untry for regulation of ground water \ndevelopment and management and notification of 65 Over -exploited areas \nin various States for registration of ground water abstraction structures. \n(viii) Direction issued by the CGWA to Chief Secretaries of the concerned  \nStates to take all measures to adopt artificial recharge to ground water/ \npromote rain water harvesting in all  the Over -Exploited areas falling \nunder their jurisdiction and ensure inclusion of roof top rain water \nharvesting in the Buildmg Bye-Laws. \n(ix)   Implementation of pilot scheme on \"Repair, Renovation and Restoration of \nWater Bodies\" was taken up in the X Five Year Plan at the cost of Rs. 300 \ncrore. Under the scheme, sanctions were issued to concerned 1098 water \nbodies for 26 districts in 15 States.  For the XI Plan, a scheme on \nrenovation of water bodies with external assistance has been announced by \nthe Finance Minister in his Budget Speech for 2007-08. \nDigging of wells in Vidarbha region \n†440. SHRI DATTA MEGHE: Will the Minister of WATER RESOURCES be pleased \nto state: \n(a) the number of wells proposed to be dug in the Vidarbha region of Maharashtra \nfor irrigation under the Prime Minister's special package scheme; \n(b) the number of wells dug in the Vidarbha region till now; . \n(c) the number of wells dug for the purpose of irrigation and for drinking water out \nof these wells;' and \n(d) by when this entire project will be completed? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES \n(SHRI JAY PRAKASH NARAYAN YADAV) (a) Government of Maharashtra have \nreported that there is no programme of digging wells under Prime Minister's Special \npackage for Vidarbha. However, under the package provision has been made for 557 \nminor irrigation schemes. \n(b) to (d) Do not arise. \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi \n\nMinister [14 AUG., 2007] Inroduced   159 \n12:00 Noon \nMINISTER INTRODUCED \nTHE PRIME MINISTER (DR. MANMOHAN SINGH): Hon. Chairman, Sir, with \nyour permission, I wish to introduce to you and through you to this august House my \ncolleague, Shrimati V. Radhika Selvi, Minister of State in the Ministry of Home Affairs \nwho has recently been inducted into the Council of Ministers. (Interruptions) \n \nडा .   वी  मेत्रयेन  ( त�मलनाड़ ) : सर , एक चपर�सी  को  appoint \nकरते  समय भी  ...( व्यवधान )... वै�र�फक े शन  होता  है  ।  \n...(व्यवधान )... \nMR. CHAIRMAN: Please sit down. (Interruptions). \nTHE MINISTER OF SHIPPING ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS (SHRI T.R. \nBAALU): Sir, they are just...(Interruptions). \nPAPERS LAID ON THE TABLE \nMemorandum of Understanding (2007-2008) between the \nGovernment of India and HDPEL \nTHE MINISTER OF SHIPPING, ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS (S HRI \nT.R. BAALU): Sir, I lay on the Table, a copy (in English and Hindi)  of the \nMemorandum of Understanding between the Government of India (Ministry of \nShipping, Road Transport and Highways, Department of Shipping) and the Hoogly Dock \nand Port Engineers Limited (HDPEL), for the year 2007 -2008. [Placed in Library. See \nNo. L.T. 6839/07] \nNotifications of the Ministry of Financ